{
  "raw": {
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188935": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Experimental Hypersonics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Experimental Hypersonics Department of Engineering Science, The Oxford Thermofluids Institute, Southwell Building, Osney Mead, Oxford, OX2 0ES 3 POSTS AVAILABLE About the role We are looking for 3 Postdoctoral Research Assistant’s in Experimental Hypersonics The role for the successful applicants will be to undertake experiments in the hypersonic facilities at the University of Oxford and in international facilities. This will involve the exploration of non-equilibrium thermochemistry, radiation and convective heating for Earth and other planets (Neptune, Martian) entry; heat flux augmentation from non-conformal surfaces; supersonic boundary layer combustion. These will involve the application of both existing and new experimental techniques. You’ll plan and run test campaigns, analyse data to advance understanding of material–flow interaction, and disseminate results in seminars, journals, and international conferences. The role will require discussion of results with both international collaborators and funders. Additionally, the candidate will have some freedom and time to explore their own pursuits, research funding permitting. Involvement in other ongoing research programs is also possible, based on the progress in the main project. The candidate will be expected to perform additional engineering tasks to ensure the development of wind tunnel infrastructure. The post holder provides guidance to junior members of the research group including research assistants, PhD students, and/or project volunteers. About you You will hold a PhD/DPhil in a relevant discipline, or be near completion with your thesis submitted, and have experience relevant to the research area. You will bring practical expertise in the operation of high-speed or high-enthalpy wind tunnels, impulse facilities, or related experimental facilities, together with a strong understanding of the analysis and interpretation of complex experimental datasets. The successful candidates will have demonstrated excellence in experimental fluid dynamics, including the design and execution of sophisticated fluid experiments and the application of advanced data analysis techniques. You will possess well-developed skills in experimental and component design, along with experience in the development and application of optical diagnostic techniques for fluid flow measurement and characterisation. You will be capable of managing your own research programme and associated activities, balancing multiple priorities while delivering high-quality outcomes. Experience contributing to peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations is essential, as is the ability to communicate complex technical concepts clearly and effectively to a range of audiences. We are looking for candidates who can contribute creatively to the development of new research ideas, support the preparation of research funding proposals, and play an active role in the continued growth of the research programme. Strong written and verbal communication skills are essential, including the ability to prepare publications, present research findings and proposals, and represent the research group in meetings with collaborators, stakeholders, and the wider research community. This would suit independent, collaborative, and highly motivated researchers who are passionate about experimental aerodynamics and advancing world-leading research in high-speed flow physics. What we offer At the University of Oxford your happiness and wellbeing at work is important to us. We have a number of generous benefits including 38 days' annual leave (inclusive of bank holidays and fixed closure days), generous pension schemes and free access to the Oxford colleges. The other benefits include: Travel - We have several travel schemes available for staff which promote sustainable modes of transport. Family friendly benefits - We aim to be a family-friendly employer and offer a range of flexible working options. We have some of the most generous family leave provision in the Higher Education sector. Sports – We have several schemes to help you stay fit and healthy, including discounted membership of the university sports centre on the Iffley Road Personal and professional development - We actively encourage all staff to participate in planning their personal and professional development and provide a broad range of opportunities to enable them to reach their full potential. University club - All staff are entitled to free membership of the University Club. It offers social and hospitality facilities, a small gym and exercise classes. Staff discounts - University staff can access shopping discounts in several ways; by purchasing a TOTUM card, by showing an employee pass in selected local stores, or online by using any of the listed discount codes Childcare services - We offer excellent childcare services including five University nurseries as well as University-supported places at other local private nurseries. How to apply Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system and should include the covering letter/supporting statement (describing how you meet the criteria), a CV, and names of two academic references. The position is an 18-month fixed term post The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 16 October 2026 Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Matthew McGilvray: matthew.mcgilvray@eng.ox.ac.uk Further information can be found at https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. The Department is committed to equality and valuing diversity and holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting gender equality in academia. The University is a Living Wage Employer, holds an Athena Swan Silver Award, an HR Excellence in Research and a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and is a Stonewall Diversity Champion. Our staff and students come from all over the world, and we seek to create a friendly and inclusive culture. Diversity is positively encouraged, through our EDI Committee, working groups and networks, for example eng.ox.ac.uk/women-in-engineering , as well as a number of family friendly policies . Contact Person : Professor Matthew McGilvray Vacancy ID : 188935 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : matthew.mcgilvray@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188672": {
      "title": "Hooke & Titchmarsh Research Fellow (Complex Systems)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Hooke & Titchmarsh Research Fellow (Complex Systems) Andrew Wiles Building, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG We are seeking talented postdoctoral researchers to take up an exciting Fellowship opportunity at the Mathematical Institute. The prestigious Hooke/Titchmarsh Research Fellowship scheme provides an ideal opportunity for candidates to pursue an independent research programme in applied/pure mathematics, and to join a large existing cohort of leading researchers on Fellowship programmes at the Mathematical Institute: https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/people/research-fellowship-programmes/hooke-and-titchmarsh-fellowships The Fellowship is full-time, and fixed-term for up to 36 months; applications for job shares or for part-time working to accommodate family circumstances will be considered. Candidates will have a background in mathematics or physics and a record of outstanding research in the mathematical theory of Complex Systems, including Random Matrix Theory and its Applications, and Statistical Mechanics, interpreted broadly. They will complement, enhance and diversify existing strengths within the Mathematical Physics Research Group, as well as other related groups in the Mathematical Institute. Members of the Mathematical Institute are often associated with several Research Groups. Successful applicants will be talented researchers, who have already produced excellent research during their PhD and in any postdoctoral positions. Securing a Fellowship will offer you the freedom to conduct your own research programme. To aid the development of their academic profile, Fellows will be required to contribute up to three hours of teaching per week for the 24 weeks of each academic year to support the department, averaged over the period of the Fellowship to allow for extended visits elsewhere. (The exact nature of this teaching will be in accordance with departmental need as determined by the Head of Department, noting that the department may require you to teach in a college to provide cover for a member of faculty). Candidates will be expected to hold, or be close to completion of, a doctorate in mathematics or a related discipline They should also show evidence of independence and the ability to undertake research of the highest academic standard, hold an outstanding record of papers published in major international journals (commensurate with career stage), and have a realisable and relevant research plan which would enhance the research profile of the Mathematical Institute. Additional information about the posts, and a full list of selection criteria, are available in the job descriptions. We proudly hold a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award and an institutional Race Equality Charter Bronze Award. Please direct informal enquiries to the Recruitment Coordinator (email: recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk), quoting vacancy reference 188672. Please note: Applicants are responsible for asking two referees to send their reference letters directly to references@maths.ox.ac.uk by the closing date. Full details are contained in the job description. Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 19 October 2026 will be considered. Contact Person : Recruitment Coordinator Vacancy ID : 188672 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £43,984 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188882": {
      "title": "Laboratory and Electronics Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Laboratory and Electronics Technician Applications are invited for a Laboratory and Electronics Technician position in the Department of Physics at the University of Oxford to support advanced research activities in integrated terahertz (THz) technologies. The project will unite novel nanowire terahertz technology from Oxford with unique custom-designed integrated circuits from project partners in Germany, to create sensitive terahertz receivers. Our objective is to create multipixel arrays for applications in non-destructive testing, remote sensing and quality control. The successful candidate will provide specialist technical expertise in electronics, RF systems, device integration and experimental laboratory systems within a multidisciplinary research environment. The role involves applying substantial technical knowledge and practical expertise to develop, assemble, test and optimise bespoke electronic and optoelectronic systems supporting frontier THz instrumentation research. The postholder will contribute specialist technical input to the design and implementation of experimental systems and will work closely with academic staff, research staff and external collaborators to deliver technical solutions to complex research requirements. You should hold a degree, HND, NVQ4, BTEC Advanced Award or equivalent professional experience in electronics, physics, engineering, or a related technical discipline, with a strong background in advanced laboratory-based research or technical development activities within a scientific or engineering environment. Demonstrating an excellent practical problem-solving ability, strong communication and collaborative skills, and ability to work independently within a research environment is essential for this role. We will consider an underfill at grade 5 if the successful applicant cannot meet all of the criteria, with an appropriate amendment of duties . Please direct enquiries about the role to Prof Michael Johnston michael.johnston@physics.ox.ac.uk The post is available initially for a fixed-term until 31 May 2028, with the possibility of extension subject to funding. The closing date of applications is 12 noon on Monday 19th October 2026 Application Process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 188882 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681 - £41,636 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188838": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Retinal Gene Therapy",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Retinal Gene Therapy Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN), John Radcliffe Hospital, Headington, Oxford, OX3 9DU Applicants are invited to apply for a for a postdoctoral research assistant vacancy within the Kapetanovic Group reporting to the PI, Professor Jasmina Kapetanovic, Associate Professor at the University of Oxford and Consultant Vitreoretinal Surgeon at the Oxford Eye Hospital. Over the past decade, Professor Kapetanovic has been at the forefront of basic science and translational medical research into novel clinical treatments for incurable retinal diseases. The main objectives of Professor Kapetanovic’s research programmes are the: Discovery and development of new genetic therapies for retinal diseases. Investigation of innovative ocular surgical techniques including robotic eye surgery. Testing of novel therapies for retinal diseases in human clinical trials. We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral research assistant to join our translational gene therapy program, developing novel adeno-associated virus (AAV)-based gene therapy treatments for inherited retinal disease (IRD). The successful candidate will generate translational research data required to support the preclinical development of new treatments including in vitro and in vivo proof-of-concept efficacy data. This is an opportunity to work across multiple disciplines spanning retinal biology, viral vector technology and translational medicine in a milestone-driven research environment that bridges early discovery and clinical application. We are looking for a highly qualified individual with outstanding academic credentials, who can work well under pressure and manage multiple tasks in a milestone-orientated research environment. The candidate should hold, or be close to completion of, a PhD/DPhil in biomedical sciences or a related field. Prior experience in the development of retinal gene therapies, including viral vector engineering and electrophysiological, functional and behavioural assays, is highly desirable. A background in retinal research and previous work with in vitro and in vivo models of genetic eye diseases would be advantageous. The post holder is a member of a research group with responsibility for carrying out research for a discrete area of a large project. The post holder provides guidance to less experienced members of the research group, including postdocs, research assistants, technicians, and PhD and project students. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the role, responsibilities, and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. This post is full time and fixed term for 12 months in the first instance. Only applications received before midday 12:00 on Monday 19th October 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN HR Recruitment Vacancy ID : 188838 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 – £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188997": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QU Postdoctoral Research Assistant Department of Physiology, Anatomy & Genetics, Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery Contract & job type: Fixed-term, Full-time for 32 months About us: At the Department of Physiology Anatomy & Genetics (DPAG) we undertake discovery science where we reassemble physiological processes at the molecular, cellular, tissue and systems level of organisation. In so doing we provide a bridge to translational medicine, and interface between physical and life sciences. We are committed not only to innovative research and the highest standard of teaching, but also to creating an inclusive and supportive working environment. Overview of the role: This post will investigate how Parkinson’s disease-associated genetic perturbations alter mitochondrial function and cellular metabolism in human neurons. The postholder will extend, quality-control and use focused CRISPRi/a sgRNA libraries in iPSC-derived cortical neurons and validate priority findings in iPSC-derived dopaminergic neurons. Cellular responses will be characterised using established high-content imaging, quantitative cellular phenotyping and assays of neuronal metabolism, under the supervision of Dr Brent Ryan in the Department of Physiology, Anatomy and Genetics. The successful candidate will be based in the Ryan Group within the Laboratory of Molecular Neurodegeneration at the Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, South Parks Road, Oxford. The group has established expertise, protocols and experimental platforms for CRISPRi/a functional genomics, sgRNA design and cloning, iPSC culture and neuronal differentiation, high-content imaging, mitochondrial phenotyping and quantitative data analysis. We welcome candidates with strong practical experience in one or more relevant areas, including CRISPR or molecular biology, functional genomics, iPSC or neuronal culture, high-content imaging, mitochondrial biology, cellular metabolism or quantitative cell biology. Training, established protocols and support from experienced members of the group will be provided for methods outside the successful candidate’s existing expertise. Working within a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment, the postholder will integrate these approaches to identify how common and rare Parkinson’s-associated genes affect neuronal mitochondrial biology, define shared cellular mechanisms and prioritise findings for further validation. The role provides an opportunity to build on the candidate’s existing strengths while developing broader expertise across functional genomics, human neuronal models and quantitative cellular phenotyping. Key responsibilities: Maintain iPSC cultures and differentiate iPSC lines into dopaminergic or cortical neurons. Perform medium-throughput experiments in iPSC-derived neuronal models using small-molecule treatments and CRISPR-based genetic perturbations. Create and maintain sgRNA libraries to enable CRISPR-mediated manipulation of disease-related genes in neurons Apply, develop and optimise cellular, molecular, high-content imaging and metabolic assays to assess mitochondrial, organelle and broader neuronal phenotypes relevant to neurological disease. Select, adapt and develop experimental protocols and determine the most appropriate methodologies to test hypotheses, identifying alternative approaches when technical problems arise. Use specialist scientific equipment in a laboratory environment. Gather, analyse, interpret and present scientific data from a variety of sources, refining working hypotheses as appropriate. Manage own research and administrative activities, including small-scale project management and coordination of multiple strands of work to meet deadlines. Contribute ideas for new research projects and to wider project planning. Contribute to the development of research proposals and ideas for generating research income. Collaborate in the preparation of scientific reports, journal articles and other research outputs, and present data, papers and posters at conferences, seminars and external meetings. Act as a source of information and advice to other members of the group on scientific protocols, experimental techniques and relevant methodologies. Collaborate effectively with other members of the research group and with colleagues in partner institutions and research groups to achieve shared project goals Essential selection criteria Hold a PhD/DPhil in neuroscience or cell/molecular biology or equivalent Have experience in neuronal/glial cell culture, preferably in human pluripotent stem cells Have experience in setting up cellular assays, such as image-based experiments Have knowledge of neurodegenerative disorders Be highly motivated, with the capacity to think creatively and work across teams Experience of creating collaborative and inclusive environments with people from different backgrounds. Desirable selection criteria Understanding of lysosomal and mitochondrial biology, and related cellular pathways Experience with implementing CRISPR methodologies Experience with implementing methodologies such as Cell Painting and metabolic flux assays A strong track record of contribution to scientific publications Experience with analysis pipelines of microscopy images What we offer: Your wellbeing at work matters, so we offer a range of family friendly and financial benefits including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle and electric car loan schemes • Employee Assistance Programme • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans While this is a full-time role, we welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. How to apply: Please provide a supporting statement outlining how you meet the selection criteria along with your CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is 12 noon on 19/10/26. Interviews are likely to take place during the week commencing 26/10/26, and will be face to face / held on Microsoft Teams, depending on applicant’s location. Applications are particularly welcome from women, black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. Follow us: Stay connected with us on LinkedIn , Bluesky and Instagram to learn more about our work and culture. Informal enquiries about the role may be directed to the DPAG HR Team: hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk DPAG’s Statement of Inclusion: We, as a Department and Community, will be considerate and welcoming of all people, regardless of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, and socio-economic background. We acknowledge societal inequalities and how these impact us, and those around us, personally and professionally. Our policies, practices and Respectful Behaviours Framework underpin this commitment. DPAG and Sustainability We have signed up to The Laboratory Efficiency Assessment Framework (LEAF) and Green Impact, actively implementing and encouraging eco-friendly practices that reduce waste, promote energy efficiency, and promote bio-diversity. See the job description for more detail. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 188997 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 45212"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188958": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Machine Learning and Quantitative Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Research Assistant in Machine Learning and Quantitative Finance Oxford-Man Institute of Quantitative Finance (OMI), Eagle House, Walton Well Road, Oxford Research Assistant in Machine Learning and Quantitative Finance This post is fixed-term until for 1 year. Reporting to Professor Álvaro Cartea, the post holder will undertake research at the intersection of machine learning and quantitative finance, working as part of a research team led by Professor Álvaro Cartea and Professor Mihai Cucuringu. The research will focus on the development and application of modern machine learning and statistical methods to problems arising in financial markets, with particular emphasis on large-scale financial datasets and quantitative modelling. The post holder will contribute to the development, implementation and empirical evaluation of new research methodologies, and to the preparation of research outputs for publication and presentation. You will Hold, or be close to completing, a first degree or integrated Master’s degree in mathematics, statistics, computer science, engineering, or another relevant quantitative discipline, together with relevant research experience. You will also possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programmes. We proudly hold a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award, which guide our progress towards advancing racial and gender equality. As part of our commitment to openness, inclusivity and transparency, we would particularly welcome applications from women and black and minority ethnic candidates, who are currently under-represented in positions of this type at Oxford. Applicants will be selected for interview purely based on their ability to satisfy the selection criteria as outlined in full in the job description. You will be required to upload a statement setting out how you meet the selection criteria, a curriculum vitae, further details/other documents e.g. publications list and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. Please note that applicants are responsible for contacting their referees and making sure that their letters are sent to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk directly by the closing date. Please direct informal enquiries about the post to HR hr@stats.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 188958 Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 19.10.2026 can be considered. Interviews are anticipated to be held shortly after. Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188958 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@stats.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681 - £41,636"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189200": {
      "title": "Research Associate – Molecular Neurodegeneration",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Research Associate – Molecular Neurodegeneration Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN), Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, New Biochemistry Building, Oxford, UK We are seeking an ambitious and highly motivated researcher to contribute to a programme of research investigating molecular mechanisms that underpin the pathogenesis of Parkinson’s disease. The study will involve the use of iPSC-based models as well as interrogation of pathways identified from previous studies in the lab. As such, the post will offer an excellent opportunity for mechanistic research using state-of-the-art models and facilities available at the Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery and Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences at the University of Oxford. The post-holder will work alongside a multidisciplinary team in the Molecular Neurodegeneration Research Group. For details check the lab website at: https://www.ndcn.ox.ac.uk/research/molecular-neurodegeneration-research-group You will have an aptitude for mechanistic studies with a proven track record of research in cellular and molecular biology as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications, and the ability to work collaboratively within a team. The post is suitable for a post-doctoral researcher with external funding. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the role, responsibilities, and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. This post is full time, fixed term until 14 November 2028. Only applications received before midday 12:00 on Monday 19th October 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189200 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 – £47,779 per annum. A less experienced candidate may be appointed at Research Grade 6 (£35,681 - £41,636 per annum), with a commensurate adjustment in either the essential criteria, responsibilities or duties."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188322": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow (Control Engineering)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Senior Research Fellow (Control Engineering) Department of Engineering Science, Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3PJ The Department of Engineering Science intends to appoint a full time Senior Research Fellow (SRF) in Control Engineering, from 1 January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. This is a permanent academic post, based in the Keble Road Triangle (OX1 3PJ). There is a procedure to apply for title of Associate Professor (under the MPLS Procedure for Conferment of Title ) and to apply for the title of Full Professor in due course. This appointment will strengthen the Department’s research in Control Engineering. The group’s current expertise includes, on the theory end, optimal, data-based, robust and model predictive control as well as large-scale, embedded, robust and distributed optimisation and polynomial/sum of squares methods; while applications span biology, battery/energy management systems, transportation, aerospace manufacturing systems, and agriculture. Candidates with research interests that align with or fall outside of the above areas are equally welcome. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct original research in Control Engineering and its applications. They will hold a doctorate with significant post-qualification research experience in Control Engineering or a similar field. They will also be expected to contribute to the department’s research portfolio in control engineering, obtaining external funding to enable development of a new and independent research program. Applications are sought from across the Control Engineering discipline, either being complementarity to existing activities, or bringing a new research dimension to the Control Group. The successful candidate will also assist in the teaching of their subject at the undergraduate and graduate level. This will include lectures, practical classes, demonstrations and design and project work. The subjects are expected to be in the general field of Control Engineering, Mathematics, and other areas of engineering, depending on the interests of the appointee. The department is developing a new MSc in Artificial Intelligence for Engineering Biology, and the successful candidate will be able to contribute to current and emerging areas of knowledge that are relevant and important to the theory and algorithms aspects of this MSc, as well as to the MSc's operation. Graduate teaching will also involve supervision of Masters and Doctoral students. They will report to the Head of Department and act as the leader of their research group, setting clear task objectives, developing productive working relations and line-managing the members of the team. To make an application, you will need to submit a supporting statement, a CV (including details of your most relevant publications) and a research proposal in a combined PDF totaling no more than 6 pages. You should not submit a separate cover letter, but if you do, it will be counted within the 6-page limit. Please do not attach additional material as your application will not be considered if it is overlength. Further details on the requirements for each of these documents can be found in the job description which can be obtained here: https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_details_form.jobspec?p_id=188322 The job reference is: DF26SRFC/188322. Please quote this in all correspondence. The closing date for applications is 12:00 noon on Monday 19 October 2026 . Initial online interviews are expected to take place in December with in-person interviews for shortlisted candidates in January. For those invited to in-person interview, the department can provide accommodation and will cover reasonable travel expenses and reasonable additional caring costs incurred as a result of attending the interview (within agreed policy limits). For queries about the post that are not answered in the job description please contact the Department on academic.recruitment@eng.ox.ac.uk . Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. The Department is committed to equality and valuing diversity and holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting gender equality in academia. The University is a Living Wage Employer, holds an Athena Swan Silver Award, an HR Excellence in Research and a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and is a Stonewall Diversity Champion. Our staff and students come from all over the world, and we seek to create a friendly and inclusive culture. Diversity is positively encouraged, through our EDI Committee, working groups and networks, for example eng.ox.ac.uk/women-in-engineering , as well as a number of family friendly policies . Contact Person : Academic Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188322 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 9 Contact Email : academic.recruitment@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £56,623 to £73,296 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188787": {
      "title": "Clinical Researcher CBD",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Clinical Researcher CBD Department of Psychiatry, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. This is an exciting opportunity for a clinical researcher to join a group with an international reputation for research on the mechanisms underlying psychosis and the development of novel treatments as part of STEP, an international multi-centre programme funded by the Wellcome Trust. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs About the Role The post is funded for 3 years and is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. The post is full time; however, consideration would be given to candidates wishing to work part-time on a pro rata basis. You will contribute to the assessment of participants in the early stages of psychosis and healthy controls. You will contribute to the evaluation of CBD as a novel treatment in psychosis in a clinical trial. You will have the opportunity to join ongoing research projects as well as develop your own academic and clinical interests. About You You will have a medical degree (MBBS MD or equivalent) and have completed your Foundation training or equivalent. You will have a demonstrable strong interest in psychiatric research. You will have excellent communication skills including the ability to form rapport with difficult to engage individuals. Membership of the Royal College of Psychiatrists (MRCPsych) or equivalent would be desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity, our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 12:00 midday on 20 October 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188787 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 20-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : CLINICAL RESEARCHER (NEW SCALE) (E63) Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £54,499 -£67,325 per annum (pro rata for part-time))"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188745": {
      "title": "Part-time Research Assistant: RELMED and RePram Studies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Part-time Research Assistant: RELMED and RePram Studies Department of Psychiatry, Warneford Hospital, Oxford, OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. We are looking for a Part-time Research Assistant to support two related research studies, RELMED and RePRAM in the Department of Psychiatry. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The post includes recruiting and testing study participants, and therefore requires onsite working, within standard working hours, in the Warneford Hospital in Oxford. About the Role The post is fixed term (funded for 12 months) and is part-time (80% FTE). It is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. The post offers you an opportunity to contribute to high-quality research in computational and personalised psychiatry, combining clinical-trial, behavioural and neuroimaging methods. Reporting to Professor Michael Browning and Dr Beata Godlewska, you will work closely with the study teams and Computational Psychiatry Research Group. You will liaise with professionals across primary and secondary care and research services, recruit and assess people with depression, and collect, analyse and present high-quality research data. The role also involves using EEG equipment and contributing to research reports, presentations and wider project activities. About You You will have a first degree in psychology, cognitive neuroscience or another relevant scientific discipline, together with relevant research experience, and sufficient specialist knowledge to work within established research programmes. You will have experience of selecting, following and adapting experimental or assessment protocols, and of gathering, managing and quality-checking scientific data from a range of sources along with experience of using EEG in a research environment and performing quality checks of EEG data. Excellent communication skills, including the ability to work effectively with research participants, colleagues and collaborators, and to maintain accurate records and confidentiality are essential. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity, ouractive Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 12:00 midday on 20 October 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188745 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 20-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681-£39,424 per annum pro rata"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188941": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Computing Modelling of Battery Cathode Materials",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Computing Modelling of Battery Cathode Materials Rex Richards Building, Department of Materials The Faraday Institution has funded a research consortium project entitled “Accelerated Development of Next Generation Li-Rich 3D Cathode Materials (3D-CAT)”. This collaborative project between Oxford Materials (Prof Robert House (PI), Prof Saiful Islam , Prof Sir Peter Bruce), and UCL Chemical Engineering (Dr Rhod Jervis ) brings together expertise in battery materials synthesis and device fabrication, advanced characterisation and materials modelling. The overall ambition for this project is to carry out multidisciplinary research that will explore Li-rich three-dimensional cathodes free from Co and Ni and with energy densities exceeding LMFP and competitive with NMC. A postdoctoral research position is now available on this 3D-CAT project in the area of computer modelling and materials design of lithium battery cathodes within the group of Professor Saiful Islam in the Department of Materials at the University of Oxford. The research programme at Oxford will investigate structural, redox, transport, and interface properties of Li-rich three-dimensional materials (including disordered rock salts) for lithium battery cathodes using advanced computational techniques based on density functional theory, molecular dynamics, cluster expansion, and machine learning. This work will have strong links to the experimental synthesis, diffraction and electrochemical work performed in the overall project. Previous experience in atomistic computer modelling of lithium battery materials or related energy materials and a recent track record of peer-reviewed publications are essential. The position is available from 1st December 2026 or shortly thereafter and ends on 30th September 2028. Group website with research details: Prof Saiful Islam: https://www.materials.ox.ac.uk/peoplepages/islam.html How to Apply All applications must be made online using the Oxford University E-Recruitment system, no later than 12.00 midday on 20 October 2026 . You will be required to upload a CV and a one-page Supporting Statement as part of your application . This one-page statement should include three short sections: (a) list of simulation codes used (b) your top three publications (c) previous experience in battery materials or energy materials modelling. Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. Only applications received online by 12.00 midday (GMT/BST) on Tuesday 20 October 2026 can be considered. Next Steps Interviews are scheduled to take place at the Department of Materials on 3rd November 2026 and you must be available on this date, either in person or online (Teams, Zoom). In line with internal policy, interview travel expenses within the UK will be reimbursed. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188941 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 20-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@materials.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189226": {
      "title": "Randomised Controlled Trial Independent Assessor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Randomised Controlled Trial Independent Assessor Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, OX1 3EL University of Oxford DEPARTMENT OF EXPERIMENTAL PSYCHOLOGY Randomised Controlled Trial Independent Assessor Grade 6: £35,681-£41,636 per annum (pro rata) The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The successful applicant will provide independent assessments for participants in the internet Cognitive Therapy for Prolonged Grief Disorder (iCT-PG) randomised controlled trial. They will interact with and conduct structured clinical assessments with eligible participants to follow research protocol, monitor risk and escalate where necessary to on call clinicians. Recording and providing clear documentation of the assessments and attend supervision sessions. The post is part-time working variable hours, usually every three months and is fixed term for 18 months. Applicants should hold a first degree, together with some relevant experience in bereavement research and clinical mental health, alongside relevant NHS clinical and research experience. They will posse sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within an established research programme along with experience of following and adapting protocols and selecting appropriate experimental methodologies. Experience of conducting interviews to protocol-based training materials is also required along with excellent communication skills and the ability to communicate sensitively. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Application process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on Tuesday 20th October and only applications received by this date will be considered. To apply for this role and for further details, including the job description and selection criteria, please see the link below: Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189226 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 20-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188841": {
      "title": "Florence Nightingale Bicentenary Fellow in Statistics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Florence Nightingale Bicentenary Fellow in Statistics Department of Statistics, 24-29 St Giles, Oxford, OX1 3LB This post is fixed-term for 2 years in the first instance The Department of Statistics is recruiting a Florence Nightingale Bicentenary Fellow . These are career development positions intended to carry around half the teaching load of an ordinary Oxford faculty position. Funding for research costs of £2,000 per year will be available. The post holder will join the dynamic and collaborative Department of Statistics. The Department carries out world-leading research in computational statistics, machine learning, theoretical statistics, and probability as well as applied statistics fields, including statistical and population genetics, bioinformatics and statistical epidemiology. The Department moved to a prominent and extensively renovated building in the centre of Oxford in 2015, and this move has further enhanced our vibrant research environment. We possess state-of-the-art facilities for our teaching and research, including two lecture theatres. Research from the Department of Statistics and the Mathematical Institute in Oxford was submitted together for the UK’s most recent national research assessment exercise, the Research Excellence Framework (REF) 2021. Overall, 78% of our submission was judged to be 4* (the highest score available, for research quality that is world-leading in terms of originality, significance, and rigour). This outstanding result is a testament to the breadth, quality and impact of the research produced by colleagues in our two departments, and the outstanding environment in which they work, supported by our excellent professional services staff. The successful candidate will hold a doctorate in the field of statistics or a related subject. The Department seeks candidate with interests in any field of research which integrates well with the research carried out within the Oxford Protein Informatics group. We proudly hold a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award, which guide our progress towards advancing racial and gender equality. As part of our commitment to openness, inclusivity and transparency, we would particularly welcome applications from women and black and minority ethnic candidates, who are currently under-represented in positions of this type at Oxford. Applicants will be selected for interview purely based on their ability to satisfy the selection criteria as outlined in full in the job description. You will be required to upload a statement setting out how you meet the selection criteria, a curriculum vitae, further details/other documents e.g. publications list and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. Please note that applicants are responsible for contacting their referees and making sure that their letters are sent to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk directly, q uoting the reference number & job title 188841, Florence Nightingale Bicentenary Fellow in Statistics, by the closing date. Please direct informal enquiries about the post to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 188841 Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 21.10.2026 can be considered. Interviews are anticipated to be held on 30.10.2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188841 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : hr@stats.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £49,119 - £58,265 (with a discretionary range to £65,336) per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188881": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL About the role The Department of Biology is seeking to recruit a Research Associate for a fixed-term period until end of May 2028. The post is financially supported by funds from the ARIA grant ‘OPTIMiSE’, awarded to Professor Francesco Licausi and Dr Ximena Chirinos, and the work will be conducted in the SynOxys Lab in the Department of Biology, University of Oxford. The postholder will contribute to the generation of transplastomic plants bearing substitutions designed to improve photosynthetic performance. This work is a collaboration with the groups led by Professor Steve Kelly, Professor Chris O’Callaghan and Dr Natali Flores. The successful candidate will optimise methods for DNA delivery to chloroplasts; transform leaf explants and regenerate transplastomic plants; and characterise the photosynthetic features of the regenerated plants. The role will also involve protocol development and working closely with other members of the group on chloroplast transformation. This post is full time and is available from 1 November 2026. About you You will hold, or be close to completion of, a relevant PhD/DPhil, together with relevant experience in molecular plant biology. You will have the ability to work both independently and as part of a team, with a willingness to learn new methods and skills and to persevere when faced with challenging experimental results. You will be highly organised, with excellent record-keeping skills and the ability to manage multiple tasks, including training junior members of the group. Previous experience of growing plants in soil and in vitro is desirable, although training will be provided. Please see the job description for the full list of selection criteria. How to apply Informal enquiries may be directed to Professor Francesco Licausi at francesco.licausi@biology.ox.ac.uk or Dr Ximena Chirinos at ximena.chirinosherrera@biology.ox.ac.uk. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post, using examples of your skills and experience. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 21 October 2026 . Interviews are likely to be scheduled for early November. The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or member of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Contact Person : HR Vacancy ID : 188881 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@biology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum, inclusive of Oxford University weighting."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188735": {
      "title": "Researcher in Large Language Models",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Researcher in Large Language Models Researcher in Large Language Models Faculty of Theology and Religion Grade 7, point 1-3: £39,424 - £41,336 per annum Full time Fixed-term (36 months / 3 years) About the Role The post holder is a member of the research team for the God and the Machine project, with responsibility for the technical aspects of the project’s experimental work with large language models and other AI tools. God and the Machine: Frontier Interactions between AI Science and Theology is a research project in the Ian Ramsey Centre for Science and Religion, Faculty of Theology and Religion, University of Oxford, funded by the John Templeton Foundation. The project develops a theological framework for understanding artificial intelligence as a mediating tool, working across two interrelated workstreams: Workstream 1 (Interpretation), which uses large language models as a computational hermeneutic tool, and Workstream 2 (Influence), which develops a theology of AI’s mediation of human formation. The post holder will primarily support Workstream 1 in its technical aspects, undertaking experimental work with large language models and other AI tools and techniques, and will also contribute to Workstream 2 as a technical expert. The gross annual salary has been set at Point 31 of the National Pay Spine, as appropriate for the career stage and experience of the post, and increments one scale point each October, in line with University of Oxford policy. The project runs from 1 December 2026 to 30 November 2029; the post is August 2026 2 available for up to 36 months within this period, with the exact start date to be agreed with the successful candidate. About You You will hold a PhD/DPhil in computer science, machine learning, or a closely related field, with a keen interest in theological research on artificial intelligence. You will be able to demonstrate expertise in large language models, including experience deploying and experimenting with local models (for example, interpretability, activation steering, or machine unlearning), as well as be proficiency in Python and relevant machine-learning frameworks. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement, CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet the selection criteria for the post, using examples of your skills and experience. The closing date for applications is 12.00pm on Wednesday, 21 October. Contact Person : Human Resources Vacancy ID : 188735 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@theology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39,424 - £41,336"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188736": {
      "title": "Researcher in Political Theology and AI Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Researcher in Political Theology and AI Science Political Theology and AI Science Faculty of Theology and Religion Grade 7, point 1-3: £39,424 - £41,336 per annum (pro-rata: £19,712 - £20,668) Part-time (0.5) Fixed-term (36 months / 3 years) About the Role The post holder is a member of the research team for the God and the Machine project, with responsibility for theological research on political loyalty within Workstream 2 (Influence). God and the Machine: Frontier Interactions between AI Science and Theology is a research project in the Ian Ramsey Centre for Science and Religion, Faculty of Theology and Religion, University of Oxford, funded by the John Templeton Foundation. The project develops a theological framework for understanding artificial intelligence as a mediating tool, working across two interrelated workstreams: Workstream 1 (Interpretation), which uses large language models as a computational hermeneutic tool, and Workstream 2 (Influence), which develops a theology of AI’s mediation of human formation. The post holder will contribute to Workstream 2, focusing on political loyalty: how AI recommender systems shape human desires and, in particular, the formation of political loyalties. The successful candidate will bring theological expertise in human formation and relationships, ideally with a specialisation in political theology. The gross annual salary has been set at Point 31 of the National Pay Spine, as appropriate for the career stage and experience of the post, and increments one scale point each October, in line with University of Oxford policy. The project runs from August 2026 2 1 December 2026 to 30 November 2029; for the post is available for up to 36 months within this period, with the exact start date to be agreed with the successful candidate. About You You will hold or be close to completing a PhD/DPhil in a closely related field, and have specialist expertise in political theology, the ordering of loves, and the theology of human formation including the formation of political loyalties. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement, CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet the selection criteria for the post, using examples of your skills and experience. The closing date for applications is 12.00pm on Wednesday, 21 October. Contact Person : Human Resources Vacancy ID : 188736 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@theology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : pro-rata: £19,712 - £20,668"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188737": {
      "title": "Researcher in Theology and AI Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Researcher in Theology and AI Science Researcher in Theology and AI Science Faculty of Theology and Religion Grade 7, point 1-3: £39,424 - £41,336 per annum Full time Fixed-term (36 months / 3 years) About the Role The post holder is a member of the research team for the God and the Machine project, with responsibility for providing systematic theological contributions across both of the project’s workstreams. God and the Machine: Frontier Interactions between AI Science and Theology is a research project in the Ian Ramsey Centre for Science and Religion, Faculty of Theology and Religion, University of Oxford, funded by the John Templeton Foundation. The project develops a theological framework for understanding artificial intelligence as a mediating tool, working across two interrelated workstreams: Workstream 1 (Interpretation), which uses large language models as a computational hermeneutic tool, and Workstream 2 (Influence), which develops a theology of AI’s mediation of human formation. The post holder will provide systematic theological contributions to both Workstream 1 and Workstream 2, acting as a general theological resource for the whole project. The successful candidate will bring experience in theology, ideally including work on the theology of AI. The gross annual salary has been set at Point 31 of the National Pay Spine, as appropriate for the career stage and experience of the post, and increments one scale point each October, in line with University of Oxford policy. The project runs from 1 December 2026 to 30 November 2029; the post is available for up to 36 months within this period, with the exact start date to be agreed with the successful candidate. About You You will hold a PhD/DPhil in a related field and have research experience in theology, ideally including work on the theology of AI. You will have specialist knowledge in systematic or constructive theology or theological ethics relevant to the project, as well as reading knowledge of German sufficient for close analysis of theological texts, which is essential for the project’s work on the German-language corpus of Karl Barth. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement, CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet the selection criteria for the post, using examples of your skills and experience. The closing date for applications is 12.00pm on Wednesday, 21 October. Contact Person : Human Resources Vacancy ID : 188737 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@theology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39,424 - £41,336"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189131": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Researcher in Computational Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Researcher in Computational Biology Oxford Vaccine Group, Centre for Clinical Vaccinology and Tropical Medicine/ Institute of Developmental and Regenerative Medicine, Headington, Oxford About the role We have an exciting opportunity for a highly motivated Senior Postdoctoral Research in Computational Biology to join the Oxford Vaccine Group (OVG). Working within a pioneering multidisciplinary research programme, you will contribute to the development of innovative computational and systems biology approaches to uncover the mechanisms underlying protection against infectious diseases and vaccine-induced immunity. By integrating complex datasets generated through vaccine studies and controlled human infection models (CHIMs), the programme aims to accelerate vaccine discovery through the application of systems immunology and artificial intelligence. The postholder will lead a defined area of computational biology and bioinformatics research within the programme, developing original research questions and applying advanced computational approaches to analyse and integrate complex clinical, immunological and multi-omic datasets generated through controlled human infection, vaccination and related studies. They will develop and implement new computational and bioinformatic methodologies, including reproducible analytical workflows, statistical and predictive approaches, to investigate immune responses and biological mechanisms associated with protection against infection and vaccination. Working closely with immunologists, clinicians, microbiologists, statisticians, data scientists and AI specialists, the postholder will provide computational leadership within multidisciplinary research projects, integrating computational and experimental findings to address the programme's scientific objectives. They will have substantial scope for scientific independence, including generating original research ideas, identifying new research opportunities arising from programme datasets and contributing to the development of the programme's computational biology research strategy. The postholder will also provide scientific guidance and supervision to junior researchers and students, contribute to collaborative funding applications, and lead and contribute substantially to the dissemination of research through high-quality peer-reviewed publications, seminars and presentations at scientific conferences You will be working in a growing department within the Medical Sciences Division. The Department of Paediatrics is a world leader in child health research and hosts internationally renowned research programmes in drug development, gastroenterology, haematology, HIV, immunology, neuroimaging, neuromuscular diseases and vaccinology. The Oxford Vaccine Group is a world-leading academic research group conducting clinical and translational studies to advance vaccine development working with a diverse portfolio funded by industry, government and charitable organisations. This position is offered full-time on a fixed-term contract initially for 3 years with the possibility to extend provided further external funding is available. About the department The Department of Paediatrics has been honoured with the Athena Swan Gold award, a national gender equality charter, recognising the Department's innovative policies and practices. We are committed to the professional development of our staff by providing up to ten paid days annually for skill enhancement and allowing applications for additional training funding. By joining us, you will have the opportunity to contribute to a forward-thinking department. We welcome new staff with ideas who are willing to shape the future of the department that thinks about its staff and wellbeing. In addition to the University of Oxford wellbeing resources , the Department of Paediatrics sponsors weekly exercise classes such as yoga and bootcamp sessions as well as a choir. For staff on work visas, we also offer financial assistance towards visa renewal fees. These activities are designed to promote physical and mental wellbeing among staff members. Please visit our website to find out more about the Department. About you You will hold a PhD/DPhil in computational biology, bioinformatics, biological sciences, statistics, mathematics or a related discipline, with significant post-qualification research experience and the specialist expertise required to independently lead a defined area of computational biology research. You will have substantial experience applying computational and bioinformatic approaches to complex biological datasets, including high-throughput and/or multi-omic data, together with proficiency in relevant programming languages such as R and/or Python and experience developing reproducible computational research workflows. You will have a strong publication record and demonstrable experience developing original research questions and translating these into appropriate computational and analytical approaches. You will be able to independently plan and manage research activities and associated resources, contribute to the development of research strategy and funding applications, and provide scientific guidance and supervision to junior researchers. Excellent scientific communication and collaborative skills are essential, including the ability to work effectively with immunologists, clinicians, microbiologists, statisticians, data scientists and AI specialists and to represent the research group externally. Experience of controlled human infection or vaccination studies, integration of single-cell and multi-omic datasets, machine learning or AI approaches, and HPC or cloud computing would be advantageous. Application Process You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity. To discuss the post in more detail, please contact Professor Daniela Ferreira, using the contact details below. Only online applications received before 12.00 midday on 21 October 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. The Department of Paediatrics offers an Interview Expense Reimbursement Policy in its efforts towards equality, diversity and inclusion. Should you be invited to interview, we will share further information about the policy at that stage. Contact Person : Daniela Ferreira Vacancy ID : 189131 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 21-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : daniela.ferreira@paediatrics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 49,119- 58,265 per annum (with a discretionary range to £65,336 per annum)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188842": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Cancer Vaccinology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Cancer Vaccinology Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Scientist to join us at the Oxford Centre for Immuno- Oncology. The research group led by Dr Carol Leung at the Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology focuses on developing novel immunotherapies against cancer, primarily on developing cancer vaccines that aim to stimulate the body's immune system to target and kill cancer cells. This project is part of the GO-PRECISE program, focuses on the development of preventative vaccines against cancer. We are seeking a passionate and motivated individual to join our team to contribute to cutting-edge cancer vaccine research. This role offers a unique opportunity to contribute to innovative, translational research within a collaborative and dynamic setting. You will work closely with scientists and industry collaborators to drive forward this ambitious research programme. GSK and Oxford University (GO) launched the PRECISE Alliance (Pre-Cancer Biology and Immunoprevention) in 2025 with the aim of conducting deep exploration of precancers to identify their molecular vulnerabilities and developing methods to intercept them. The alliance is directed by Prof Sarah Blagden and theme leads Professors Tim Elliott (immune-oncology), Ellie Barnes (immunoprevention) and Simon Leedham (precancer discovery). Within Oxford, the alliance spans multiple departments and networks basic science (including spatial biology, genomics, proteomics, immunopeptidomics), immunology (including machine learning, bioinformatics, preclinical validation) and clinical development (including trial design and delivery and regulatory approval) expertise as well as community engagement. This truly multi-disciplinary initiative seeks to transform cancer by actively preventing it from developing, initially in people at highest risk of the disease and later in the wider population. You will be responsible for managing your own academic research and associated administrative activities, including small scale project management. You will coordinate multiple aspects of work to meet deadlines. You will collaborate in the preparation of scientific reports and journal articles and occasionally present research findings through papers and posters at meetings. Applicants must hold a PhD/DPhil (or be close to completion) at the time of appointment, in a relevant discipline such as biological sciences or immunology. You will contribute intellectually to the research programme by developing and refining mechanistic hypotheses, designing and optimising experimental strategies, and interpreting complex datasets within the context of tumour immunology and cancer vaccinology. You will play an active role in shaping research direction through critical evaluation of results and integration of findings across in vitro and in vivo systems. You will support studies on cancer vaccine development using in vivo and in vitro techniques. Specifically, you should have experience with immunological methods such as co-culture assays, multicolour flow cytometry (FACS), ELISA, and in vivo tumour models. Additionally, familiarity with molecular biology techniques, including PCR, and Western blotting, is essential. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 31 December 2027 and is funded by GSK. Only applications received before 12 midday on Monday, 23 February 2025 will be considered. Please quote 184278 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 188842 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 22-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: salary in range £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189236": {
      "title": "Clinical Trials Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Clinical Trials Coordinator 189236 Clinical Trials Coordinator Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 6: Salary in the range of £35,681-£41,636 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full time (part-time at minimum of 80% FTE will be considered), fixed term position for 2 years. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. The Kadoorie Institute of Trauma, Emergency and Critical Care brings together interdisciplinary research across trauma and rehabilitation, emergency care and critical care, with a focus on improving outcomes for patients with life-threatening illness and injury. The Institute integrates expertise across these closely connected specialties within a single research environment. Based at the John Radcliffe Hospital, the centre of urgent and emergency care in Oxford, the Institute supports more than 100 multidisciplinary researchers working across the breadth of trauma, emergency and critical care research. Its research spans the use of routinely collected healthcare data and early- and late-phase clinical trials, enabling discoveries to be translated into improvements in clinical practice and patient care. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working About the Role In this role, you will work in collaboration with a Trial Manager, to share overall responsibility for the day to day management of a large complex clinical trial or work with minimal supervision on a medium-sized clinical trial. You will assist in finalising the protocol, study materials, training packages for collaborating sites and trial manuals, and preparing trial specific instructions. This post involves overseeing data entry on the trial database (assisted by a Data Entry Clerk) and performing regular systematic checks of information held on trial database as required. You will also organise regular Management Group and Steering Committee meetings, with joint responsibility for preparing documentation and reports for these meetings. About You You must hold a degree in a biomedical or associated subject, or other relevant qualification, or have proven experience in clinical research. You will have experience and an understanding of techniques for the management of medical research information e.g. data coding, entry validation and reporting, as well as experience of managing a REDCap database. Experience of working as a trial coordinator/manager and experience of working within trials involving minors or adults lacking capacity, are desirable. Application Process The University of Oxford is committed to equality and valuing diversity. All applicants will be judged on merit, according to the selection criteria. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and must include a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the selection criteria, using examples of your skills and experience. Please quote reference 189236 in all correspondence. Contact Person : NDORMS Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189236 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £35,681-£41,636 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188927": {
      "title": "Data Coordinator and Bioinformatician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Data Coordinator and Bioinformatician Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN), Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin Building, Sherrington Road, OX1 3QU We are looking for a Data Coordinator and Bioinformatician to join the Cader Group at NDCN. The Medical Research Council (MRC), in collaboration with the Wellcome Trust, NC3Rs and Innovate UK, has invested £15.9 million to support the novel human in vitro disease model clusters (IHDM-C) initiative, which aims to redefine human-based research models for greater understanding of disease and the acceleration of new medicines. As part of this initiative, there will be strategic coordination across five funded clusters to address common challenges in the field. We are looking for someone who will support data coordination and shared bioinformatics across the network, helping to ensure consistency, interoperability and discoverability through coordination and shared standards. The role will also involve using existing datasets to support the validation and value of developing in vitro models. Key responsibilities will include developing and maintaining shared metadata templates and standards, maintaining a programme-level data catalogue, and establishing shared GitHub resources to support reproducibility and reuse. The postholder will also work with the UKRI Functional Genomics Institute on the analysis of functional genomics datasets and liaise with researchers across partner institutions to support the deposition and linking of data, code and research outputs. The successful candidate will hold a Master’s degree or have equivalent experience in bioinformatics, computational biology, data science, biomedical sciences or a related quantitative discipline, with experience in bioinformatics analysis and/or data engineering of complex biological datasets. They will also have proficiency in a programming or scripting language such as Python or R, with experience of building reproducible analytical workflows. Excellent organisational, collaboration and communication skills are essential, together with the ability to coordinate data-related activities across multiple stakeholders or projects and manage competing priorities. The postholder should also have an understanding of FAIR data principles and awareness of data protection, research governance and ethical requirements in biomedical research data. Desirable experience includes working with functional genomics datasets, such as RNA-seq, single-cell or spatial transcriptomics, and experience with metadata standards, data repositories or research data management workflows. Experience supporting collaborative multi-site research projects or infrastructure programmes, and familiarity with high-performance computing or cloud-based analytical environments, would also be advantageous. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the role, responsibilities, and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. This post is full time, fixed term until 31st March 2029. Only applications received before midday 12:00 on Friday 23rd October 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN Recruitment Vacancy ID : 188927 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,424 – £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188903": {
      "title": "Neuroelectronics Research Engineer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Neuroelectronics Research Engineer Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Old Road Campus We are seeking a full-time (80% FTE part-time considered) neuroelectronics research engineer to join the Van Rheede group at the Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Department of Engineering Science (Old Road Campus, Headington). The post is fixed-term for up to 4 years, the hard end date for the project is the 31st March 2031. About The Role You will join an interdisciplinary research team with the goal of accelerating the development of better neuromodulation therapies through enabling better preclinical research on clinically relevant time scales. You will work closely with Dr Joram van Rheede as well as with the research groups of Prof. Andrew Sharott and Prof. Timothy Denison. You will be responsible for the development, testing and iterative improvement of an autonomous (fully embedded) battery powered closed-loop brain stimulation and recording system for preclinical research. The goal is to enable 24h+ continuous recording and stimulation in preclinical model animals (rats) in their home cage environment for research into the interaction between deep brain stimulation and sleep. System development work will include: 1) Design of a system architecture and firmware building on prototype systems previously developed in the Sharott and Denison labs 2) Integration of new integrated brain recording and stimulation chips provided by industry partner MintNeuro 3) Design and implementation of a system form factor and exterior suitable for an existing or modified 3d printed head mount 4) Managing and balancing constraints regarding size, weight and power in the context of preclinical rat experiments 5) Working closely with laboratory technicians and preclinical neuroscience researchers to understand the user requirements and constraints for device operation 6) Validation of the device using bench testing simulations of experimental conditions and providing support for validation in animal experiments (assisted by lab animal technician / research assistant) Additionally, you will be expected to carry out duties common to academic researchers, including contributing to scientific publications, supervision of students and junior researchers, and representing the research group at internal and external meetings and conferences. About You We are looking for someone motivated to make an impact on clinical neuromodulation and neuroscience research with strong academic and technical ability who wants to contribute to a positive research environment. You should have a PhD or equivalent professional/research experience in engineering, computer science, neuroscience or a closely related discipline. Additionally, you should have demonstrated experience in 1) system design for signal processing and real-time control, 2) working with embedded electronics, and 3) PCB design and debugging. What We Offer Your happiness and wellbeing at work matters to us, so we offer a range of family friendly and financial benefits including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans The University also runs an enormous number of social groups and sports clubs for those looking for more than just a great place to work. Application Process Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr. Joram van Rheede: joram.vanrheede@eng.ox.ac.uk For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on the 23rd October 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, (describing how past experience fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Dr. Joram van Rheede Vacancy ID : 188903 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : joram.vanrheede@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum, including Oxford University Weighting (OUW)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188924": {
      "title": "OCTRU Senior Medical Statistician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 OCTRU Senior Medical Statistician 188924 OCTRU Senior Medical Statistician Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 8: Salary in the range of £49,119 – £58,265 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full-time (part-time at minimum of 60% FTE will be considered), fixed term position for 3 years. An exciting opportunity has arisen for a Senior Medical Statistician based at the Oxford Clinical Trials Research Unit (OCTRU) within the Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) at the University of Oxford. The OCTRU Statistics Team, led by Professor Jonathan Cook, is looking for an experienced medical statistician to join the Senior Management Team. Working under the supervision of a Lead Statistician, the post-holder will contribute to the design, conduct, analysis and presentation of results of collaborative clinical research projects, mostly randomised clinical trials, across the different clinicals areas. OCTRU is one of the largest academic clinical trials unit in the UK (www.octru.ox.ac.uk). It undertakes trials of all phases across oncology, surgery, experimental medicine, orthopaedics and rheumatology. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working after an initial full time office based period About the Role In this role, you will lead, or undertake the senior statistical role in, the analysis of large and complex data that have been collected or are currently being collected by trials on the OCTRU portfolio https://www.ndorms.ox.ac.uk/octru/trials-portfolio as well as provide written statistical input into protocols and ethics committee submissions. You will also assist in and sometimes leading the development and submission of grant proposals to major national and international agencies, including industry, charities, research councils and the government. You will prepare and/or approve statistical analysis plans (SAP) where appropriate and the preparation of statistical programs for analysis of study data. Furthermore, the post-holder will play a key role in quality assurance within the OCTRU Statistics Team. You will be expected to be able to work independently and supervisor more junior staff on project work. The post is based at the Botnar Institute for Musculoskeletal Sciences. A pattern of hybrid working (involving part office, part remote location) will be possible with the agreement of your line manager. About You You must hold a postgraduate degree in medical statistics (or near to completion) or have equivalent experience and have specialist knowledge in analysis of complex and longitudinal data. You must have considerable experience working as a statistician, including statistical consulting, designing clinical research, contributing to grant applications, active running of research, analysing data and paper writing for peer-reviewed journals. Additionally, you will have a high level of proficiency in statistical programming in Stata and/or R. Experience of working with and supervising early career statisticians, as well as experience of working within a UKCRC clinical trials unit, is desirable. Application Process The University of Oxford is committed to equality and valuing diversity. All applicants will be judged on merit, according to the selection criteria. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and must include a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the selection criteria, using examples of your skills and experience. If you have any queries regarding the role, please email jonathan.cook@ndorms.ox.ac.uk. Interviews will take place during the week commencing 9 November and will be face to face. Please quote reference 188924 in all correspondence. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188924 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £49,119 – £58,265 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189000": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Solid-State Batteries",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Solid-State Batteries Department of Materials, Rex Richards Building, Oxford About the role We are seeking a well organised and self-motivated researcher to work on the Innovate UK funded CoRe-SoLiS project, reporting to Prof. Mauro Pasta. Applicants must hold PhD/DPhil in Materials Science or Chemistry (or be close to completion), together with relevant experience in the field of solid-state batteries, in addition to a first-class bachelor or master’s degree in Materials Science or Chemistry and have significant expertise in the following areas: • Mechanical and microstructural characterisation of ceramic materials; • Metallurgy and processing of air-sensitive alloys; • Characterisation of air- and beam-sensitive battery materials, particularly using: o Solid-state NMR; o (Plasma) focused ion beam (FIB) techniques; o Laboratory-based and synchrotron-based characterisation methods; • Solid-state electrochemistry, particularly: o Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) You should have significant experience in managing your own academic research and administrative activities along with excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings. You will also have a strong publication record in a related field, and possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programmes. You will have the ability to identify research objectives and to carry out appropriate research activities within a given timescale, demonstrating the ability to work co-operatively with colleagues in an extremely fast-paced and intellectually stimulating environment. This full-time fixed-term post is available until 30 May 2029 and is based at the Department of Materials Centre for Energy Materials Research, South Parks Road, Oxford. How to Apply All applications must be made online using the Oxford University E-Recruitment system, no later than 12 noon on Friday 23 October 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a Supporting Statement as part of your application . Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. Only applications received online by 12.00 midday (BST) on Friday 23 October 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled to take place at the Department of Materials in the week commencing Monday 2 November 2026 and you must be available on this date, either by Teams or in person. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189000 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@materials.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189091": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Experimental Political Science (TRUST-D Project)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Experimental Political Science (TRUST-D Project) Department of Politics and International Relations, Manor Road Building, Oxford, OX1 3UQ About Us The Department of Politics and International Relations (DPIR) is one of Oxford’s largest and most internationally recognised departments, home to around 90 academic staff and a thriving community of researchers and visitors. Ranked the Number One University in the UK for Politics by The Guardian in 2024, the department delivers world‑class teaching and research supported by experienced Professional Services teams working across Courses, Research, Finance, HR and Communications. We are strongly committed to equality, diversity and inclusion, including work towards an Athena Swan Bronze Award, and are based in the Social Sciences Building on Manor Road alongside Economics, Criminology, Socio‑Legal Studies and the Social Science Library. What We Offer Working at the University of Oxford offers several exclusive benefits, such as: 38 days of annual leave (pro rata if part time and inclusive of public holidays) to support your wellbeing and additional leave after long service. One of the most generous family leave schemes in UK higher education, offering up to 26 weeks of full-pay maternity and adoption leave, plus 12 weeks of full-pay paternity/partner leave. A commitment to hybrid and flexible working to suit your lifestyle. An excellent contributory pension scheme. Affordable and sustainable commuting options, including a cycle loan scheme, discounted bus travel, and season ticket loans. Access to a vibrant community through our social, cultural, and sports clubs. About the Role The Centre for Democratic Resilience is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the Trust, Participation, and Democratic Resilience (TRUST-D) project led by Professor Petra Schleiter. The project examines how participatory innovations, such as citizens' assemblies and participatory budgeting, influence long-term political trust. Young voters represent a key group of interest within the larger project. Working as part of a collaborative research team, the postholder will play a key role in designing and implementing field and survey experiments, analysing data, and contributing to high-quality academic and policy-focused outputs. The role also offers opportunities to engage with policymakers, practitioners and wider stakeholders, while being part of the vibrant research community at the Centre for Democratic Resilience. About You You will have experience of designing and delivering quantitative research, with a particular interest in political trust, democratic participation and political behaviour. You will contribute to the design, implementation and analysis of field and survey experiments, helping to generate new insights into the impact of participatory innovations such as citizens’ assemblies, mini-publics and participatory budgeting. Working closely with Professor Petra Schleiter and project collaborators, you will contribute to high-quality academic research, publications, conference presentations, policy briefs and stakeholder engagement activities. You will thrive in a collaborative research environment and be excited by the opportunity to join the Centre for Democratic Resilience's vibrant community of researchers, postdoctoral fellows and doctoral students. Furthermore, you must hold, or be close to completion of, a doctorate in political science or a related field. This position offers an exciting opportunity to join a vibrant community of researchers, postdoctoral fellows and doctoral students based at the Centre for Democratic Resilience (CeDR) and contribute to research on political trust and democratic resilience. This position is a full-time fixed term position available for 30 months from 16th November or as soon as possible thereafter. If you have any questions about the role, please contact the DPIR HR Team at vacancies@politics.ox.ac.uk . All enquiries will be treated in strict confidence and will not form part of the selection decision. Application Process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via the University’s job pages. You will be required to upload a recent CV and supporting statement as part of your online application. Only applications received before 12 noon (UK time) on Friday 23rd October can be considered. Please quote reference 189091 in all correspondence. Further information on the application process can be found here: How to apply | Oxford University Contact Person : DPIR HR Team Vacancy ID : 189091 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : vacancies@politics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £51,983 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188934": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Quantitative Political Science (TRUST-D Project)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Quantitative Political Science (TRUST-D Project) Department of Politics and International Relations, Manor Road Building, Oxford, OX1 3UQ About Us The Department of Politics and International Relations (DPIR) is one of Oxford’s largest and most internationally recognised departments, home to around 90 academic staff and a thriving community of researchers and visitors. Ranked the Number One University in the UK for Politics by The Guardian in 2024, the department delivers world‑class teaching and research supported by experienced Professional Services teams working across Courses, Research, Finance, HR and Communications. We are strongly committed to equality, diversity and inclusion, including work towards an Athena Swan Bronze Award, and are based in the Social Sciences Building on Manor Road alongside Economics, Criminology, Socio‑Legal Studies and the Social Science Library. What We Offer Working at the University of Oxford offers several exclusive benefits, such as: 38 days of annual leave (pro rata if part time and inclusive of public holidays) to support your wellbeing and additional leave after long service. One of the most generous family leave schemes in UK higher education, offering up to 26 weeks of full-pay maternity and adoption leave, plus 12 weeks of full-pay paternity/partner leave. A commitment to hybrid and flexible working to suit your lifestyle. An excellent contributory pension scheme. Affordable and sustainable commuting options, including a cycle loan scheme, discounted bus travel, and season ticket loans. Access to a vibrant community through our social, cultural, and sports clubs. About the Role The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will work with Prof Petra Schleiter on the externally funded Trust, Participation, and Democratic Resilience (TRUST-D) project, which examines whether participatory innovations such as participatory budgeting, citizens' assemblies, and mini-publics strengthen long-term political trust. The Fellow will lead quantitative analyses using advanced causal inference methods, including regression discontinuity designs, difference-in-differences, event study, instrumental variable, and related approaches. The role involves working with large-scale administrative, electoral, and survey datasets, including Polish participatory budgeting and local government data; and linking European comparative datasets such as the OECD Deliberative Democracy Database. European Social Survey data; and regional (NUTS-3 level) administrative data and indicators of political trust and participation. The Fellow will work closely with the PI and project collaborators on publications, conference presentations, policy briefs, and stakeholder engagement activities. They will be part of a vibrant community of researchers, postdoctoral fellows, and doctoral students based at the Centre for Democratic Resilience (CeDR) in the Department of Politics and International Relations. About You You will lead, develop and implement rigorous quantitative research designs to identify the causal effects of participatory innovations on political trust and democratic resilience. You will assemble, merge, manage and analyse complex administrative, electoral and survey datasets, and estimate and evaluate advanced statistical models using methods including difference-in-differences, event studies, regression discontinuity designs, instrumental variables and panel-data approaches. You will also conduct robustness, placebo, sensitivity and pre-trend analyses. You will contribute to the development of the project's theoretical framework and hypotheses concerning participatory innovations and political trust. Working closely with the PI and project collaborators, you will contribute to research dissemination through conference presentations, publications, policy briefs, and the preparation of replication datasets and code. The successful candidate will support engagement with policymakers, practitioners and civil society stakeholders, while also contributing to the administration of the project, including grant applications and workshop and conference organisation. You will manage your own research activities and workload effectively within the overall project framework. This position offers an exciting opportunity to join a vibrant community of researchers, postdoctoral fellows and doctoral students based at the Centre for Democratic Resilience (CeDR) and contribute to research on political trust and democratic resilience. This position is a full-time, fixed term position available for 30 months from 16th November or as soon as possible thereafter. If you have any questions about the role, please contact the DPIR HR Team at vacancies@politics.ox.ac.uk . All enquiries will be treated in strict confidence and will not form part of the selection decision. Application Process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via the University’s job pages. You will be required to upload a recent CV and supporting statement as part of your online application. Only applications received before 12 noon (UK time) on Friday 23rd October can be considered. Please quote reference 188934 in all correspondence. Further information on the application process can be found here: How to apply | Oxford University Contact Person : DPIR HR Team Vacancy ID : 188934 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : vacancies@politics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £51,983 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188929": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer - ATLAS",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Research Software Engineer - ATLAS MPLS Doctoral Training Centre, 1-4 Keble Road The Oxford Research Software Engineering Group (OxRSE - www.rse.ox.ac.uk ) is looking to recruit a Research Software Engineer (RSE) to join our team. We work with other research groups across the University of Oxford to create, improve and maintain software used for scientific research and translational projects, as well as providing consulting and training to other researchers on best practices in software development and reproducible research. This post is part of OxRSE’s embedded RSE group within the Department of Physics. This is an exciting opportunity to join a growing team and work as an RSE on world-leading and impactful projects. For the first two years, the RSE will work exclusively in the Department of Physics, on the expansion of the ATLAS project to include new scientific capability related to the Breakthrough Listen project. ATLAS is an asteroid impact early warning system developed by the University of Hawaii and funded by NASA. It consists of five telescope units (2 in Hawaii and 1 each in Chile, South Africa and Tenerife), which automatically scans the whole sky several times every night, looking for moving and transient objects. The University of Oxford is a key partner in the data management, data processing, and public data availability. The project has become the leading “all-sky every night” transient and a collaboration with the SETI Institute and Breakthrough Listen at Oxford has resulted in the construction of the sixth ATLAS unit at the HAT Creek Radio Observatory in California, USA. This will give further longitude coverage and weather redundancy and will also provide a fast readout capability for technosignature searches. The RSE will work on integrating the HCRO site data into the ATLAS network and develop software to enable data access and analysis for technosignatures. We offer RSEs the potential to advance their career and to make a significant contribution to the development of good software practices at a world-leading university. The successful candidate will, following the initial two year period, have the opportunity to work on a variety of research projects across the Physics subject area and the broader University of Oxford. They will also have the opportunity to deliver workshops and training in software development best practices, and further develop their own technical and research skills as well as those outside of software development, such as project management and consultancy. We are looking for candidates who hold a doctoral degree (or are close to completion, i.e., thesis submitted) with a significant software development component. We will also consider candidates without a doctoral degree but with equivalent experience of software development in a research environment. Candidates must be able to collaborate effectively with other researchers to design and implement reliable and maintainable software solutions to scientific questions and must be able to rapidly acquire skills in new programming languages, libraries and technologies. The successful candidate will have substantial experience, and proven efficiency across the majority of: Python and web frameworks (especially Django) programming; REST API development; Linux and shell scripting; implementation, deployment and management of large (multi-billion row) relational databases. This post is offered as an open-ended position subject to external funding. It is full time, but flexible options will be considered (e.g., part-time, job share). Applications for this vacancy are to be made online and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Your supporting statement must utilise the provided form. You will also be asked to provide a sample of your software engineering work which demonstrates your approach. The closing date for applications is 12 noon on 23rd October 2026. For further information, please contact Kaustubh Rajwade at kaustubh.rajwade@dtc.ox.ac.uk . Contact Person : Daniel Blanche Vacancy ID : 188929 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : daniel.blanche@dtc.ox.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188930": {
      "title": "Senior Research Software Engineer - Breakthrough Listen",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Senior Research Software Engineer - Breakthrough Listen MPLS Doctoral Training Centre, 1-4 Keble Road The Oxford Research Software Engineering Group (OxRSE - www.rse.ox.ac.uk ) is looking to recruit a Senior Research Software Engineer (RSE) to join our team. We work with other research groups across the University of Oxford to create, improve and maintain software used for scientific research and translational projects, as well as providing consulting and training to other researchers on best practices in software development and reproducible research. This post is part of OxRSE’s embedded RSE group within the Department of Physics. This is an exciting opportunity to join a growing team and work as a Senior RSE on world-leading and impactful projects. For at least the first two years, the RSE will work exclusively on the Breakthrough Listen project in the Department of Physics. Breakthrough Listen (BL) is the world’s most comprehensive Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence, operating wideband receivers on leading radio telescopes including the Green Bank Telescope, Parkes, MeerKAT, the Very Large Array, and the Allen Telescope Array. BL is collecting multi-petabyte datasets and developing state-of-the-art pipelines to detect technosignatures (indicators of technology as a proxy for the existence of extraterrestrial intelligence). A primary goal of BL is also to make the data accessible and usable by the broader astronomy and scientific communities, and by the general public. This is a high-impact role at the frontier of astronomy, machine learning, and large-scale scientific computing. We offer RSEs the potential to advance their career and to make a significant contribution to the development of good software practices at a world-leading university. The successful candidate will, following the initial two year period, have the opportunity to work on a variety of research projects across the Physics subject area and the broader University of Oxford. They will also have the opportunity to deliver workshops and training in software development best practices, and further develop their own technical and research skills as well as those outside of software development, such as project management and consultancy. As a Senior RSE, the postholder will also in time take on responsibility for the management of projects and the supervision of RSEs. We are looking for candidates who hold a doctoral degree with a significant software development component, with post-qualification research and software engineering experience. We will also consider candidates without a doctoral degree but with substantial experience of software engineering in a research environment. You must be able to collaborate effectively with other researchers to design and implement reliable and maintainable software solutions to scientific questions and must be able to rapidly acquire skills in new programming languages, libraries and technologies. The successful candidate will have substantial experience, and proven efficiency, across the majority of: Python and web frameworks (especially Django) programming; REST API development; Linux and shell scripting; implementation, deployment and management of large (multi-billion row) relational databases; containers and orchestration; AI-assisted software development; distributed systems (e.g. Slurm, Kubernetes) and high performance computing. This post is offered as an open-ended position subject to external funding. It is full time, but flexible options will be considered (e.g., part-time, job share). Applications for this vacancy are to be made online and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Your supporting statement must utilise the provided form. You will also be asked to provide a sample of your software engineering work which demonstrates your approach. The closing date for applications is 12 noon on 23rd October 2026. For further information, please contact Kaustubh Rajwade at kaustubh.rajwade@dtc.ox.ac.uk . Contact Person : Daniel Blanche Vacancy ID : 188930 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : daniel.blanche@dtc.ox.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189002": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Applied Mathematics and Computational Neuroscience",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Applied Mathematics and Computational Neuroscience Andrew Wiles Building, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG. We are currently inviting applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to work with Professor Lida Kanari at the Mathematical Institute, University of Oxford. This is a 1-year, fixed-term position, funded by a research grant from the UKRI, MRC. The starting date of this position is flexible with an earliest start date of 1st January 2027. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who are able to start between 1st January 2027 and 1st June 2027. The successful candidate will be part of a research group with responsibility for carrying out research in Applied Mathematics and Computational Neuroscience and related fields as part of the Medical Research Council, UKRI grant “Algebraic topology bridging the gap between single neurons and networks”. They will be expected to conduct research which falls within the remit of this large-scale project and will have the opportunity to do so in several ways including independently, collaboratively with other members of the group at Oxford and/or with other mathematicians internationally. They will contribute to the activities of the wider Oxford Centre for Industrial and Applied Mathematics (OCIAM) research group and write up the results of their work, either singly or with co-authors, for publication in refereed journals and proceedings. There will be opportunities to contribute a small amount of teaching to the department, of at most three hours a week during the academic terms. You will have, or be close to completing, a PhD in applied mathematics or a related discipline, and possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programmes. Excellent communication skills are essential, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings. We proudly hold a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award and an institutional Race Equality Charter Bronze Award, which guide our progress towards advancing racial and gender equality. As part of our strategic aim to improve staff equality and diversity, we would particularly welcome applications from women and BME candidates, who are currently under-represented in positions of this type within the department. Please direct informal enquiries to the Recruitment Coordinator (email: recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk), quoting vacancy reference 189002. Applicants will be selected for interview purely based on their ability to satisfy the selection criteria as outlined in full in the job description. You will be required to upload a statement setting out how you meet the selection criteria, a curriculum vitae including full list of publications, details of teaching experience, a statement of research interests, and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. (NOTE: Applicants are responsible for contacting two of their referees and making sure that their letters are sent to references@maths.ox.ac.uk by the closing date ). Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on Monday 26 October 2026 can be considered. Interviews are anticipated to take place early November 2026 Contact Person : Recruitment Coordinator Vacancy ID : 189002 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £41,636 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188772": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Molecular Optically Detected Magnetic Resonance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Molecular Optically Detected Magnetic Resonance Department of Materials, Parks Road, Oxford About this role and project Optically addressable solid-state electronic spins are central to emerging quantum technologies, such as optical quantum networks and quantum sensors. While spins associated with defects have received much attention, molecular spins are particularly promising due to their unique chemical tunability. However, there are few molecular systems where optically detected magnetic resonance has been demonstrated under ambient conditions. Our group is interested in forming an understanding of the molecular design rules that govern the excited state dynamics, optical properties, spin initialisation and spin coherence timescales for various molecules. This postdoctoral position will involve the study of excited and ground state molecular spins using a homebuilt optically detected magnetic resonance setup. In collaboration with researchers in Oxford and Cambridge, we will form a structure-function relationship for molecular spin qubit candidates. This work complements our research on spin defects in solid-state materials (hBN), and links with existing collaborations we have on molecular spins across the Departments of Chemistry and Physics in Oxford. We are looking for an excellent post-doctoral candidate with a PhD degree (or near completion) in molecular spin physics, EPR, ODMR, solid state quantum physics, or related areas. The successful candidate will be part of our research team and involved in designing and implementing experiments, disseminating our work via writing research articles and giving oral presentations at conferences and participating in the training and management of PhD students. The Project is due to start January - February 2027 (but flexible). How to Apply All applications must be made online using the Oxford University E-Recruitment system, no later than 12 noon on Monday 26 October 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a Supporting Statement as part of your application**. Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application.** Only applications received online by 12.00 midday (GMT/BST) on Monday 26 October 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled to take place at the Department of Materials on Friday 6 November 2026 and you must be available on this date, either by Teams or in person. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188772 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@materials.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 -£47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188755": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associates in Mathematical Modelling of Chronic Hepatitis B",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associates in Mathematical Modelling of Chronic Hepatitis B Andrew Wiles Building, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG We invite applications for two Postdoctoral Research Associate posts to work with Professor Helen Byrne at the Mathematical Institute, University of Oxford. These are three-year, fixed-term positions, funded by the GSK-Imperial-Oxford Modelling-Informed Medicine Centre (MiMeC). The starting dates of the positions are flexible. The successful candidates will be members of the Wolfson Centre for Mathematical Biology in the Mathematical Institute. They will be responsible for carrying out research involving mathematical modelling of hepatitis B virus (HBV) as part of the MiMeC chronic hepatitis B research theme. This theme is led by Professors Helen Byrne and Jane McKeating in Oxford in close collaboration with Drs Marissa Renardy and Ahmed Nader at GSK, supported by Professor Philip Maini and Dr Josh Moore (Oxford) and Dr Yaron Ben-Ami, Dr Rajat Desikan and Dr Anna Sher (GSK). The MiMeC HBV theme seeks to understand why patients with chronic hepatitis B respond differently to therapy, and how immune-modulatory treatments can be used to achieve functional cure. The project will integrate clinical, immunological, and spatial molecular data to develop mechanistic models linking liver tissue biology, immune responses, and viral dynamics. The two researchers will play complementary roles within the programme. One post will focus on the analysis of spatial omics, histological and imaging data, and the development of spatially resolved models (e.g. agent-based, partial differential equation, or hybrid models) of infection, immune responses, and the effect of treatments within the liver. The second post will develop whole-body quantitative systems pharmacology (QSP) models linking intrahepatic processes to peripheral biomarkers and clinical outcomes. The postdocs will work closely together to integrate spatial and QSP modelling frameworks, compare their predictive performance, and evaluate existing and emerging therapeutic strategies. The project is particularly well suited to researchers with expertise in mathematical modelling, computational biology, scientific computing, machine learning, immunology, or systems pharmacology, and offers the opportunity to contribute to clinically relevant research with the potential to inform personalised HBV therapies. The successful candidates will be expected to conduct research both independently and in collaboration with other members of the group at Oxford, and elsewhere, including Drs Renardy and Nader at GSK. They will also be expected to provide informal mentorship to doctoral students in the group, potentially including informal co-supervision of students working on MiMeC-related projects. In addition, they will work closely with virologists in the McKeating team within the Nuffield Department of Medicine at Oxford. These positions are ideally suited to researchers with strong modelling and computational skills and offer the opportunity to undertake research with the potential for substantial positive real-world impact. The postdoctoral researchers will contribute to the research and scholarly activities of the Wolfson Centre for Mathematical Biology and will be expected to publish the results of their work in leading peer-reviewed journals. There will also be opportunities to contribute a limited amount of teaching within the department, amounting to at most three hours per week during academic term time. We proudly hold a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award and an institutional Race Equality Charter Bronze Award. Please direct informal enquiries about the posts to either Professor Helen Byrne (email: helen.byrne@maths.ox.ac.uk) or Professor Jane McKeating (email: jane.mckeating@ndm.ox.ac.uk). The HR Administrator can also be contacted (email: recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk), quoting vacancy reference 188755. Please note: Applicants are responsible for asking two referees to send their reference letters directly to references@maths.ox.ac.uk by the closing date. Full details are contained in the job description). Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 26 October can be considered. Online interviews are anticipated to take place on the 13 November 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Coordinator Vacancy ID : 188755 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £41,636 - £46,049 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189271": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Single-Atom Alloy Catalysis (Modelling)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Single-Atom Alloy Catalysis (Modelling) Physical and Theoretical Chemistry Laboratory, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QZ Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Single-Atom Alloy Catalysis (Modelling) to work under the supervision of Professor Michail Stamatakis in the Department of Chemistry at the University of Oxford. About the role The project focuses on investigating the catalytic properties of single-atom alloys (SAAs) for the activation and conversion of hydrocarbons, such as methane, and oxygenates, including formic acid and methanol, in reactions relevant to energy and environmental sustainability. The research aims to advance the understanding of these materials and support the development of next-generation catalysts for natural gas and biomass valorisation. The successful candidate will contribute to the group's ongoing research by applying quantum chemistry approaches, particularly density functional theory (DFT), to investigate catalytic conversion pathways on promising SAAs. You will also employ statistical mechanics methods, including kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC), to predict the overall kinetics of catalytic reactions. The role will involve computational research using high-performance computing facilities, developing and applying scientific methods, analysing research findings and contributing to publications and presentations. You will work collaboratively within the research group, with opportunities for interaction with the ZERO Institute and potentially industry. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), fixed-term post for up to 12 months, based in the Physical and Theoretical Chemistry Laboratory, South Parks Road, Oxford. Find out more about the Stamatakis research group at: https://stamatakislab.org/ About you Applicants must hold a PhD in Chemistry, Physics, Engineering or related discipline (or be close to completion), prior to taking up the appointment. You will have expertise in statistical mechanics method development, with particular emphasis on kinetic Monte Carlo approaches, as well as expertise in software development, including version control systems such as Git or similar. You will be able to demonstrate competence and success in your previous research, together with a thorough understanding of your research area and evidence of independent intellectual and practical contributions to research projects. You will be a motivated, organised and proactive researcher, able to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, a willingness to develop new techniques, and the ability to communicate research findings clearly are essential. You will also be able to work supportively with other members of the research group, contributing to the exchange of knowledge and skills and providing guidance to junior colleagues where appropriate. Expertise in ab initio methods and kinetic Monte Carlo approaches specifically in the context of catalysis would be advantageous. Application Process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your application. In your supporting statement (<5 pages), please explain how you meet each of the selection criteria found in the job description. Certificates, references and research papers should not be provided at this stage. The closing date for applications is 12.00 midday, Tuesday 27 October 2026, UK time. Interviews will be held on as soon as possible thereafter. The University of Oxford and The Department of Chemistry are Silver Athena SWAN holders. Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and ethnic minority candidates, who are under-represented in Chemistry research posts in Oxford. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189271 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 27-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@chem.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum (inclusive of Oxford University Weighting)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189099": {
      "title": "Researcher in Agentic AI for Mechanistic Cancer Discovery",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Researcher in Agentic AI for Mechanistic Cancer Discovery Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford), Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF Oxford Population Health (the Nuffield Department of Population Health) provides an excellent environment for multi-disciplinary research and teaching and for professional and support staff. We work together to answer some of the most important questions about the causes, prevention and treatment of disease. We are seeking a Researcher to join Trey Ideker’s multidisciplinary research team at the Big Data Institute (BDI). You will contribute to a strategic research programme aimed at developing agentic artificial intelligence for biomedical discovery and genotype-to-phenotype modelling. You will formulate mechanistic biological hypotheses and analyse data from genetic, genomic, clinical, and functional evidence, design and implement multi-agent systems for scientific reasoning and develop agents capable of interrogating scientific literature, biomedical databases, genomic and multi-omics datasets. Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in bioinformatics, computational biology, systems biology, cancer biology, functional genomics, or another relevant discipline. You will have experience conducting hypothesis-driven biological research, a strong understanding of how causal and mechanistic knowledge is established in biology and demonstrated expertise in applying computational methods to biological discovery using genomic, functional-genomic, multi-omics, or other high-dimensional biomedical data. This post is full time (part time considered) and fixed term post for 3 years. The closing date for applications is noon on 27 October 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a cover letter as part of your online application. The cover letter should clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Contact Person : Recruitment Administrator Vacancy ID : 189099 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 27-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ndph.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39,424 - 47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189215": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher Department of Education, 15 Norham Gardens, Oxford, OX2 6PY. The Department of Education is seeking to appoint a full-time (1.0FTE) Postdoctoral Researcher to work on a Strategic Innovation-funded project with Dr Sara Ratner (PI), Professor Victoria Murphy and Assoc Professor Sandra Mathers for 22 months. The post will be available from 1 December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The successful candidate will work with PI to undertake research and support the establishment of the Oxford University Repository: Families, AI and Media (OUR FAM). This project builds on the work of the highly successful Learning in Families through Technology Project (LiFT) and AI in Education Oxford University (AIEOU) to develop an ecosystem approach to digital technologies in the early years and broader educational contexts. Candidates should: Hold a relevant PhD/Dphil, together with relevant experience Possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within the research project Ability to manage own academic research and associated activities Previous experience of contributing to publications/presentations Ability to contribute ideas for new research projects and research income generation Excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings A research focus on AI, expertise in co-design, participatory research methodologies, and experience working with young research participants and their families would be desirable. Information about the post, including details of how to apply, is available in the Further Particulars. Applications must be made online. Applicants will be asked to provide details of two referees and to upload a CV and a supporting statement explaining how they meet the selection criteria, using examples of their skills and experience. Only applications received before 12 noon (UK time) on Wednesday 28 October 2026 will be considered. The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. Contact Person : HR Department Vacancy ID : 189215 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@education.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 -£47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=187849": {
      "title": "Process Evaluation Social Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Process Evaluation Social Scientist Nuffield Department of Primary Care Health Sciences, Radcliffe Primary Care Building, Woodstock Road, Oxford Applications are invited for a Social Scientist with excellent knowledge of qualitative data collection and analysis methods to work with Dr Charlotte Albury on an NIHR SPCR-funded trial aiming to increase flu vaccination rates in adults. The purpose of this role is to work on the qualitative process evaluation to understand patients’ and practitioners’ experiences of the trial; and to generate learning for subsequent implementation. The role is embedded within the Communication and Language in Healthcare team, part of the thriving Health Behaviours theme ( https://www.phc.ox.ac.uk/research/research-themes/health-behaviours-theme/health-behaviours ) in the Nuffield Department of Primary Care Health Sciences (NDPCHS). The health behaviours theme focusses on interventions to prevent or treat ill-health, including a focus on diet, obesity, and smoking. The growing Communication and Language in Healthcare team, led by Associate Professor Charlotte Albury sits within this wider theme. The team aims to understand health communication and has a strong track record of applied research that can benefit patients and lead to changes in guidance and practice. We are committed to involving members of the public in all stages of our work. We offer generic training opportunities for researchers seeking a career in applied health sciences. We engage with a wide range of stakeholders and policymakers to facilitate the translation of research into practice. The successful applicant will have excellent knowledge of qualitative data collection and analysis methods, and knowledge of clinical trial process evaluations. Responsibilities will include carrying out the trial process evaluation, including undertaking a variety of qualitative data analysis methods including thematic analysis, and content analysis. You will also be responsible for conducting interviews and focus groups with patients and professionals, triangulating data and writing up reports, papers and blogs for the study. You will hold a PhD/DPhil in the field of social sciences or clinical trial process evaluations (or be about to obtain one in the next few months), and have qualitative experience including knowledge of process evaluations. You will be based in the Radcliffe Primary Care Building or Gibson Building of the Nuffield Department of Primary Care Health Sciences, Radcliffe Primary Care Building, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG as your normal place of work but there may be the opportunity for occasional remote working. The position is funded by the NIHR School for Primary Care Research until 29 February 2028, with the possibility for extension. For further information or discuss the post please contact Charlotte Albury charlotte.albury@phc.ox.ac.uk . “Committed to equality and valuing diversity” You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. The closing date for applications is 12:00 noon on Wednesday 28 October 2026. Interviews are expected to be held during the week commencing 23 November 2026. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 187849 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@phc.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189264": {
      "title": "Research and Innovation Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Research and Innovation Associate Department of Education, 15 Norham Gardens, Oxford, OX2 6PY. The Department of Education is seeking to appoint a part-time (0.6FTE) Postdoctoral Researcher to work on an ESRC-funded project with Dr Sara Ratner (PI). The post will be available from 1 November 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The successful candidate will work with PI to undertake research for the ESRC-funded project, Publicly Funded Pupil Data as a Strategic Asset , in collaboration with the UK Department for Education, Ofqual, and Oak National Academy. Candidates should have: A relevant PhD/Dphil, together with relevant experience Possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline Ability to manage own academic research and associated activities Previous experience of contributing to publications/presentations Ability to contribute ideas for new research projects and research income generation Excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings A research focus on AI or learning analytics, expertise in research methodologies and analytical strategies common in education, and an interest in multi-disciplinary collaboration would be desirable. Information about the post, including details of how to apply, is available in the Further Particulars. Applications must be made online. Applicants will be asked to provide details of two referees and to upload a CV and a supporting statement explaining how they meet the selection criteria, using examples of their skills and experience. Only applications received before 12 noon (UK time) on Wednesday 28 October 2026 will be considered. The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. Contact Person : HR Department Vacancy ID : 189264 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@education.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 -£47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189132": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Immunologist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Immunologist Department of Paediatrics, Oxford Vaccine Group, Churchill Hospital, Oxford, OX3 7LE About the role We have an exciting opportunity to join the Oxford Vaccine Group in the Department of Paediatrics as our Senior Postdoctoral Immunologist. You will play an important role in projects examining the immune response to vaccination funded by the MRC/UKRI, across the LEGACY studies (currently LEGACY01-LEGACY04) as part of the larger Lymph nodE single-cell Genomics AnCestrY and ageing (LEGACY) Network in Lymph Node Research UK. These projects involve profiling the intra-lymph node dynamics of vaccine responses in younger and older individuals compared with individuals on immunosuppression. You will be responsible for developing and pursuing appropriate immunological protocols and techniques to support the research, using sophisticated wet-lab immunology techniques including multi-parameter flow cytometry, cell culture, systems serology, ELISpot, ELISA and single-cell RNA sequencing (RNAseq). You will work independently, maintain excellent records and data management, contribute to manuscript-level reporting and writing, and support the training of junior team members. You will be working in a growing department within the Medical Sciences Division. The Department of Paediatrics is a world leader in child health research and hosts internationally renowned research programmes in drug development, gastroenterology, haematology, HIV, immunology, neuroimaging, neuromuscular diseases and vaccinology. You will join the group of Prof Katrina Pollock at the Oxford Vaccine Group and have the opportunity to interact with other members of the LEGACY Network and the Immune Memory and Mechanisms of Protection from Vaccines (IMMPROVE) consortium, led by Prof Teresa Lambe and Prof Paul Klenerman. This position is offered full-time on a fixed-term contract initially for 2 years, with the possibility to extend provided further external funding is available. This role may be eligible for sponsorship for a Global Talent visa under current UK and Immigration legislation. Further details of the chosen candidate will be required before this can be determined, so advice and assistance will be provided when/if they are chosen for the role. About the department The Department of Paediatrics is committed to equality and valuing diversity. The Department of Paediatrics has been honoured with the Athena Swan Gold award, a national gender equality charter, recognising the Department's innovative policies and practices. We are committed to the professional development of our staff by providing up to ten paid days annually for skill enhancement and allowing applications for additional training funding. By joining us, you will have the opportunity to contribute to a forward-thinking department. We welcome new staff with ideas who are willing to shape the future of the department that thinks about its staff and wellbeing. In addition to the University of Oxford healthcare and wellbeing resources, the Department of Paediatrics sponsors weekly exercise classes such as yoga and bootcamp sessions as well as a choir. For staff on work visas, we also offer financial assistance towards visa renewal fees. These activities are designed to promote physical and mental wellbeing among staff members. Please visit our website to find out more about the Department. About you You will have a relevant PhD/DPhil, supplemented by relevant post-qualification research experience and a strong publication record. You will have specialist knowledge in immunology and vaccinology, with proven technical skills including cell culture, ELISpot, ELISA, flow cytometry and single-cell RNA sequencing. You will have excellent analytical and quantitative skills, the ability to independently plan and manage a research project including a research budget, and the ability to raise research funds through grant applications. You will also have excellent interpersonal and communication skills and an ability to work effectively in a demanding research environment. Application Process You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your application will not be processed if you do not include both documents. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. To discuss the post in more detail, please contact Professor Katrina Pollock, using the contact details below. Only online applications received before 12.00 midday on 28 October 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : Katrina Pollock Vacancy ID : 189132 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : katrina.pollock@paediatrics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 49,119- 58,265 per annum (with a discretionary range to £63,489 per annum)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=187206": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Postdoctoral Scientist Big Data Institute, Li Ka Shing Centre for Health and Information Discovery, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF We are seeking a Postdoctoral Scientist to lead cutting-edge research into biomarkers and disease mechanisms to enhance therapeutic development for RNUopathies, including ReNU syndrome. Based within the Big Data Institute (BDI) and the Institute of Developmental & Regenerative Medicine (IDRM), you will join a multidisciplinary team investigating therapeutic approaches for rare neurodevelopmental disorders caused by variants in small nuclear RNA (snRNA) genes. You will lead bioinformatic and statistical analysis of large-scale genomic and transcriptomic datasets from human samples, cell lines, and mouse models; developing and implementing novel analytical approaches where appropriate; leading the research project; collaborating with internal and external researchers; contributing to the supervision of MSc and PhD students; maintaining high standards of data quality, integrity, and documentation; disseminating findings through publications, presentations, and conferences; engaging with patient advocacy groups; and supporting public engagement and outreach activities. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to pioneering rare disease research within a highly collaborative environment and to develop your own scientific profile within a world-leading therapeutic genomics programme. It is essential that you have a PhD/DPhil (or are close to completion) in a relevant field such as genomics, genetics, bioinformatics, biostatistics, statistics together with a track record of contributing to peer-reviewed publications and presenting research findings at seminars, conferences, and scientific meetings. Proficiency in programming languages such as R and/or Python is required, alongside the ability to independently manage day-to-day research activities, take ownership of projects, and deliver high-quality outputs to agreed deadlines. You should have experience working collaboratively within multidisciplinary teams and possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare publications, create clear and informative data visualisations, and present research to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for two years. Only applications received before 12 midday on Thursday 29 October 2026 will be considered. Please quote 187206 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Kristina Limbu Vacancy ID : 187206 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 29-Oct-2026 12:30 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188890": {
      "title": "OCTRU Medical Statistician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 OCTRU Medical Statistician 188890 Oxford Clinical Trial Research Unit (OCTRU) Medical Statistician Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 6: Salary in the range of £35,681 - £41,636 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full-time, fixed term position for 2 years. We have an exciting opportunity for a Medical Statistician (Grade 6) to join the Oxford Clinical Trials Research Unit (OCTRU) Statistics Team. OCTRU is one of the largest academic clinical trials units in the UK. (www.octru.ox.ac.uk). It undertakes trials of all phases across oncology, surgery, experimental medicine, orthopaedics and rheumatology. The Statistics Team led by Professor Jonathan Cook is looking for a recent post-graduate statistician to join an expanding statistical team. New team members will undergo a training programme in clinical trial statistics and related processes during their first year. The post is based within the Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS), University of Oxford. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. About the Role In this role, you will assist in the design and set-up of clinical trials within a multidisciplinary team including (but not limited to) the Chief Investigator and Trial Manager as well as provide written statistical input into protocols and ethics committee submissions. You will also provide advice on data collection, CRF development, data validation and data cleaning and data management. You will prepare statistical analysis plans (SAP) and produce statistical programs for analysis of study data, including generating, validating and monitoring randomisation schedules and performing interim and final analyses. The post is located at the Botnar Institute for Musculoskeletal Sciences, Oxford. This position is available now but would be suitable for a current MSc Student in Medical Statistics with a view to starting in the summer or autumn of 2027. In the latter instance there may be an opportunity to undertake a summer project with the team. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working after an initial full time office based period About You You must hold a postgraduate degree in statistics (or a closely related subject), a substantial part of which relates to medical statistics, or equivalent experience and be fluent in key aspects of study design and analysis, and aware of the latest statistical techniques. You must have good verbal and written communication skills including the ability to explain complex statistical issues to non-statisticians together with the ability to analyse problems, develop and test solutions, and make judgements. Additionally, you will have a proficient knowledge of statistical software (i.e., R or Stata). You will have good project management skills including the ability to plan and prioritise short and longer-term goals to ensure that all necessary deadlines are met simultaneously for around 4 substantive, and a number of smaller, projects. Proficiency in statistical programming and experience of producing statistical reports and contributing to peer-review publications are desirable. Application Process The University of Oxford is committed to equality and valuing diversity. All applicants will be judged on merit, according to the selection criteria. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and must include a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the selection criteria, using examples of your skills and experience. If you have any queries regarding the role, please email jonathan.cook@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Interviews will take place during the week commencing 9 November 2026 and will be face to face. Please quote reference 188890 in all correspondence. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188890 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £35,681-£41,636 per annum."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188693": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Neuropsychologist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Neuropsychologist Department of Paediatrics, Specialised Translational Research Oxford Neuromuscular Group (STRONG), John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford And Sanders Research Group, Institute of Developmental & Regenerative Medicine IDRM, Old Road Campus, Oxford About the role We have an exciting opportunity to join a collaborative programme between the University of Oxford and King’s College London, bringing together expertise across institutions to advance autism research and clinical trial readiness. The programme delivers large-scale clinical trials and longitudinal natural history studies incorporating deep phenotyping, translational genetics, advanced neuroimaging, and wearable technologies to inform therapeutic development. The Postdoctoral Research Psychologist, based within the Department of Paediatrics, will play a key role in administering behavioural and psychological assessments for the programme. The postholder will be responsible for coordinating and conducting neuropsychological and behavioural assessments in accordance with study protocols, ensuring the consistent and high-quality collection of research data. They will also contribute to the development and implementation of research studies, support the preparation of grant applications, and assist in the development of procedures for standardised assessments and other neuropsychological aspects of study delivery. You will be based within the Department of Paediatrics in the University of Oxford’s Medical Sciences Division, a world-leading centre for child health research with internationally recognised programmes, including neuromuscular diseases and vaccinology. The postholder will work closely with Professor Laurent Servais (John Radcliffe Hospital), and Professor Stephan Sanders (Institute of Developmental & Regenerative Medicine), collaborating across translational and clinical research teams. This position is offered full-time on a fixed-term contract for an initial period of five years or until 31/06/2032, whichever is earliest This role meets the criteria for a UK Global Talent Academic and Research visa route. About the department The Department of Paediatrics is committed to equality and valuing diversity. The Department of Paediatrics has been honoured with the Athena Swan Gold award, a national gender equality charter, recognising the Department's innovative policies and practices. We are committed to the professional development of our staff by providing up to ten paid days annually for skill enhancement and allowing applications for additional training funding. By joining us, you will have the opportunity to contribute to a forward-thinking department. We welcome new staff with ideas who are willing to shape the future of the department that thinks about its staff and wellbeing. In addition to the University of Oxford wellbeing resources , the Department of Paediatrics sponsors weekly exercise classes such as yoga and bootcamp sessions as well as a choir. For staff on work visas, we also offer financial assistance towards visa renewal fees. These activities are designed to promote physical and mental wellbeing among staff members. Please visit our website to find out more about the Department. About you You will hold a Doctorate in Psychology or related area and have significant postgraduate experience working with children and young people with complex neurodevelopmental conditions. You will possess excellent knowledge of neuropsychology, particularly in neurodevelopmental disorders, and a strong understanding of neuropsychological outcome measures used in clinical trials and longitudinal natural history studies. You will have a track record of research publications and strong academic writing skills, with experience contributing to research outputs and study development. You will be confident in administering and interpreting standardised neuropsychological assessments and communicating findings effectively. You will demonstrate excellent communication, interpersonal, organisational, and time-management skills, with the ability to work collaboratively within multidisciplinary teams and contribute positively to the research environment. Application Process You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your application will not be processed if you do not include both documents. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. To discuss the post in more detail, please contact Corinne Betts, the Programme Manager for Prof. Sanders, using the contact details below. Only online applications received before 12.00 midday on 02 November 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. The Department of Paediatrics offers an Interview Expense Reimbursement Policy in its efforts towards equality, diversity and inclusion. Should you be invited to interview, we will share further information about the policy at that stage. Contact Person : Corinne Betts Vacancy ID : 188693 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : corinne.betts@paediatrics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 38,674- £46,913 per annum (with a discretionary range to £51,059 per annum)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189077": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biomedical Image Analysis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biomedical Image Analysis Department of Engineering Science, Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Li Ka Shing Centre for Health Information and Discovery, Old Road Campus, Oxford, OX3 7LF We are seeking a full-time postdoctoral research scientist to join research groups of Professor Jens Rittscher and Professor Konstantinos Kamnitsas at the Department of Engineering Science, Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Headington. The post is funded by Wellcome Trust Bioimaging Technology Development Award. The initial contract is fixed-term for 18 months, with the possibility of an extension, subject to funding. You will apply and develop cutting-edge machine learning methods to integrate and analyse multi-omic data to identify disease phenotypes. A key aspect of the role is to bridge research and implementation. You will also develop novel algorithms using state-of-the-art computer vision and machine learning techniques (segmentation, multimodal AI, foundation models, agentic frameworks) to detect and analyse tissue architecture components and subtle disease related changes. The postdoctoral researchers will work closely with software engineers and data platform developers to translate research prototypes into practical tools, ensuring robustness, reproducibility, and usability of developed tools for wider adoption. You should hold a relevant PhD/DPhil (or be near completion) and possess strong technical expertise in deep learning, such as models for image segmentation, classification, multi-modal processing, foundation models, or agentic frameworks. An extensive background in computer vision and/or biomedical image analysis is essential, as well as the ability to manage own academic research and associated activities. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Jens Rittscher (jens.rittscher@eng.ox.ac.uk) and Konstantinos Kamnitsas (konstantinos.kamnitsas@eng.ox.ac.uk). For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on 2nd November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a cover letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Professor Jens Rittscher Vacancy ID : 189077 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : jens.rittscher@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum (Inclusive of Oxford University Weighting)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189233": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Multimodal Analysis to understand medical procedures.",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Multimodal Analysis to understand medical procedures. Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Old Road Campus, Headington, OX3 7DQ We are seeking a creative and highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to join the Turing AI World-Leading Fellowship research programme lead by Professor Alison Noble. Topics in the research programme include single- and multi-modal video-based human-AI collaboration, federated learning for healthcare collaboration and partnership, and applied research to understand human skill in healthcare settings and AI to support healthcare professional skills training. The appointee will be a technical researcher joining the AI to understand human skills strand of the research programme. This post is focused on bridging theory and application of medical video/multimodal analysis and medical procedure modelling. The research will involve building video/multimodal models that can analyse medical procedures and evaluate how usable they are. Evaluation may involve quantitative studies (model performance) and qualitative studies (human factors assessment). The appointee will be part of the Noble research group at the Institute of Biomedical Engineering, based at the Old Road Campus in Headington. The post is funded by UKRI and is fixed-term for 18 months. You will be working in a small, highly motivated interdisciplinary team working towards a shared goal. You will be responsible for the design and pilot testing of machine learning-based automated medical video/multimodal analysis models. The research will include work that aims to understand medical procedures using video/multimodal analysis. This requires solid technical experience in medical video/multimodal analysis and building efficient learning approaches from sparse data, as well as open-mindedness to co-develop blue-sky research questions with clinical collaborators from first principles. The work will involve both computational methods research and collaboration with experts in clinical medicine and human factors to design and evaluate models in simulated and real clinical scenarios. Evaluation may involve quantitative studies (model performance) and qualitative studies (human factors assessment). You should hold a relevant PhD/DPhil (or near completion) with publications in medical image/video analysis, computer-assisted intervention, or video analysis in computer vision. Knowledge of a specific medical procedure is not a requirement but a genuine interest in research at the interface of machine learning with clinical demand and the curiosity to develop blue-sky research questions is. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr Cheng Ouyang (email: cheng.ouyang@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on the 2nd November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Dr Cheng Ouyang Vacancy ID : 189233 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : cheng.ouyang@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum, including Oxford University Weighting (OUW)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189155": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher: AI Safety in Mental Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher: AI Safety in Mental Health Department of Psychiatry, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher to work on delivering state of the art AI safety work for the ARIADNE consortium, a Wellcome Trust-funded project developing a generative artificial intelligence system that conducts clinical interviews to assess risk of psychosis. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The post is available on flexible hybrid basis, with the minimum on-site attendance of three days per week. About the Role The post is full time and funded for 2 years. It is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. You will join the AI arm of the Computational and Molecular Neuroscience Laboratory. You will work closely with this group and with the wider ARIADNE team of clinicians, ethicists and lived-experience researchers across the Universities of Oxford, King's College London, Birmingham and Cambridge, working in partnership with Google DeepMind. Your research will focus on the safety of ARIADNE ahead of clinical evaluation. You will address the two principal safety risks identified through formal risk analysis (ISO 14971): inadvertent reinforcement of delusional beliefs, and inappropriate responses to suicidal symptoms. You will develop LLM “supervisor” modules that detect these events and flag them to the debriefing clinician, and will evaluate them in controlled testbeds in which LLM-simulated patients, representing five levels of symptom severity, complete ARIADNE interviews scored at scale by independent LLM evaluators and calibrated against human clinicians. About You You will have or be close to the completion of PhD/DPhil in machine learning, computer science, computational neuroscience, mathematics, or a related quantitative discipline and possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programme You will have proven experience developing and systematically evaluating LLM-based systems, including prompt-based, agentic or multi-agent approaches, using modern Python and LLM APIs. You will also have experience designing and analysing empirical evaluations of machine-learning systems, ideally including simulation-based methods, LLM-as-judge approaches, and validation of automated evaluations against human ratings. Experience with AI safety evaluation methods, such as red-teaming, guardrail architectures or model-based monitoring and of fine-tuning large language models with deep learning frameworks (e.g. PyTorch) or applying alignment techniques (e.g. reinforcement learning from human feedback, DPO) would be desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity, our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 12:00 midday on 4 November 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189155 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189018": {
      "title": "Quantitative Postdoctoral Researcher - Youth Participation and Mental Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Quantitative Postdoctoral Researcher - Youth Participation and Mental Health Department of Social Policy and Intervention, 32 Wellington Square, Oxford, OX1 2ER About the role The Department of Social Policy and Intervention is seeking a Quantitative Postdoctoral Researcher to join an interdisciplinary team across the UK and Brazil working on the ESRC-funded project ‘Becoming Protagonists: Effectiveness of a Digital Intervention to Promote Youth Participation for Mental Health in Brazil’. The project will evaluate ‘Cadê o Kauê?’, a digital intervention that equips students to support their peers’ mental health and take collective action to create more positive school environments, through a national trial in Brazilian state schools. The postholder will play a key role across the trial, from meeting regulatory and ethical requirements through to data management and analysis, while also contributing to other studies within the wider project. The role is fixed term for 26 months. The role is being advertised as full time however we will accept applications candidates wishing to work part-time (up to 80% FTE). About you You will hold, or be close to completion of, a PhD/DPhil in a relevant subject with expertise in evaluating interventions and at least one relevant area such as mental health, global health, adolescent, digital or school-based research. You will have the ability to independently manage academic research along with experience in writing academic publications and presenting findings to diverse audiences. You will have experience in navigating regulatory frameworks and knowledge of data management principles, with direct experience of managing large datasets and performing quantitative data analysis using statistical software (e.g. R, Stata, SAS). Strong communication and collaborative skills are essential, alongside the ability to contribute to new research ideas and funding opportunities. Desirable criteria include expertise in cluster randomised trials, qualitative or mixed methods research, patient and public involvement, and research in Brazil and/or other Global South contexts. The Department is committed to fostering an environment of equality, diversity and inclusion. The hours may be worked flexibly to complement caring responsibilities and applications are welcomed from part-time workers seeking a job share position - please state preferred hours and working pattern in your application. You will be based in Oxford as your normal place of work however there is opportunity for remote working (up to 40% FTE). The Department of Social Policy and Intervention holds a Bronze Athena Swan Award which demonstrates our commitment towards advancement of gender equality in higher education and research institutions: equal opportunities, representation and success for all. How to apply As part of your application you will be required to upload a CV and supporting statement , along with the name and contact details of two referees. The supporting statement should explain how you meet each of the essential selection criteria for the post using clear examples. This may include experience and skills gained in employment, education or during career breaks (such as time out for dependents). Please DO NOT attach any other documents to your application as these will not be considered. Further information, including how to apply, is available in the attached job description. Any further enquires may be directed to the email address below. Only online applications received by 12.00 noon on 4 November 2026 will be considered. Interviews are expected to take place during the week commencing 23 November 2026. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189018 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hrteam@spi.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424-£47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188955": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Transient Astrophysics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Transient Astrophysics Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Oxford Applications are invited for Postdoctoral Researcher in Transient Astrophysics. The University of Oxford Physics Department, together with Lancaster University, the University of Southampton and Queen’s University Belfast, is seeking to appoint one Postdoctoral Research Associate at each institution as part of the five-year STFC Large Award “TRANSCEND”. The TRANSCEND collaboration will use Vera Rubin/LSST transient data and the 4MOST Time-Domain Extragalactic Survey (TiDES) to address pressing questions in modern astrophysics: how stars explode and drive chemical enrichment, how supermassive black holes grow, and the true nature of Dark Energy. Each Postdoctoral Research Associate will lead one of the following areas: SNe Ia cosmology groundwork or rare transient follow-up and characterization (University of Oxford): use over 30,000 spectroscopically confirmed SNe Ia and 150,000 host-galaxy redshifts to improve SNe Ia standardisation and lay the groundwork for using SN cosmological measurements. Alternatively, focus on work on follow-up and modelling of rare transients. Host galaxies of transients (University of Southampton): derive host-galaxy properties from LSST+TiDES photometric and spectroscopic data, and use the unprecedented LSST+TiDES transient sample to study how transients correlate with their environments and measure rates of different classes of transient. Simulations and selection effects for SN Ia cosmology and beyond (Lancaster University): lead the forward-modelling of the combined LSST+TiDES sample, and characterize its associated sample selection effects. This project will include building galaxy population catalogues validated against state-of-the-art cosmological simulations (e.g. Illustris-TNG). This simulation effort will be the foundation underpinning every rate, host-galaxy and cosmological result in TRANSCEND. Transient physical properties and the black hole mass function of tidal disruption events (Queen’s University Belfast): measure key parameters such as luminosity and nickel masses for thousands of transients from TiDES + follow-up data to build population demographics, and use hundreds of live TDEs and thousands of host galaxies to derive scaling relations and occupation fractions for SMBHs. This advertisement is for the post at the University of Oxford specifically; separate applications are required to each university for the other three posts. The postholder has opportunity to teach. Applicants should possess, or close to obtaining a doctorate in physics or a related field and ideally a strong background in Physics or Astrophysics. Have relevant research experience and a proven record of research in time-domain astrophysics, supernovae, galaxy evolution, cosmology or survey science. Please direct enquiries about the role to Profs Maria Vincenzi maria.vincenzi@physics.ox.ac.uk and Stephen Smartt stephen.smartt@physics.ox.ac.uk This is a full-time post, fixed term for 5 years The closing date for applications is noon GMT time on Friday, 6 November 2026. As part of your online application, you will be required to upload a CV, publication list, and a 2-page research statement, and to provide details of two referees. Referees will only be contacted to submit their letters if you are shortlisted. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188955 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 06-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,474 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189267": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Associate in Agent-Based Climate Macroeconomics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Post Doctoral Research Associate in Agent-Based Climate Macroeconomics Smith School & Environmental Change Institute, School of Geography and the Environment, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QY The University of Oxford is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to research examining the macroeconomic impacts of UK climate mitigation policies. Based at the Institute for New Economic Thinking (INET), the successful candidate will join the MacroCB7 – Macroeconomics of the Seventh Carbon Budget project, working closely with the Department for Energy Security and Net Zero (DESNZ) to assess how climate policies affect economic growth, employment, inflation and inequality. Working with Professor Doyne Farmer, Tom Youngman and Dr Donal Brown, you will contribute to the development and application of an innovative agent-based macroeconomic model to evaluate the economic and distributional consequences of net zero policies. Research will explore areas including low-carbon heating technologies, government finances, alternative investment scenarios and the potential for social tipping points in climate transitions. The role combines quantitative research, software development and policy engagement. You will develop and implement new modelling approaches using Python, analyse results through sensitivity testing and simulation, contribute to academic publications, and present findings to government and policy partners. From June 2027, the research will expand to examine transformative net zero policies across energy, housing, transport and food, with a particular focus on reducing inequality alongside greenhouse gas emissions. We are seeking candidates who hold, or are close to completion of, a PhD/DPhil in a relevant discipline, with programming experience and a strong understanding of economic theory, particularly macroeconomics and complexity approaches. You should have excellent analytical and communication skills, experience contributing to research publications, and an interest in applying quantitative modelling to climate policy. Experience in agent-based modelling, collaborative coding or policy-engaged research would be advantageous. The role is based at INET's offices on Manor Road, Oxford. Flexible working arrangements may be considered, subject to discussion and approval, including part-time working at 0.5 FTE or above. A minimum of one day per week in the office during term time is expected. This position offers the opportunity to develop and apply innovative economic modelling methods to inform UK climate policy, working within an interdisciplinary research team with established government partnerships. This post is offered on a full-time, fixed-term basis for 18 months upon start date . (Part-time arrangements of 0.5 FTE or above may be considered) What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ well-being, and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter with a supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. In your supporting statement, please explain how you meet each of the selection criteria found in the job description, and why you would like to do this role. See guidance at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/cv-and-supporting-statement. Any technical questions related to this vacancy can be sent to: recruit@ouce.ox.ac.uk The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon (UK time) on 9 November 2026 . Only applications submitted by this time will be considered. Interviews are expected to take place on or around the 18 September 2026. The University of Oxford and The School of Geography and the Environment are Silver Athena SWAN holders. Applications are particularly welcome and encouraged from women, black, and minority ethnic candidates, who are under-represented in posts in The School of Geography and the Environment. The School of Geography and the Environment are committed to equality and values diversity Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189267 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ouce.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189244": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Immunology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Immunology 189244 – Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Immunology Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 7: Salary in the range of £39,424-£46,476 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full time (100% FTE), fixed term position for 3 years. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. The Kennedy Institute is a biomedical research centre uniquely bringing together discovery science and early-stage clinical research, to develop transformative new therapies for chronic inflammatory and musculoskeletal conditions. The Clarke group, based at the Kennedy Institute of Rheumatology, is focused on understanding how cellular metabolic processes shape the immune response in health and disease. A particular focus is on B cell metabolism, and more specifically the germinal centre reaction, which is responsible for the production of high-affinity antibodies and immune memory. More information can be found on the group website (https://www.kennedy.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/translational-immunometabolism). What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs About the Role We are offering an exciting and unique opportunity for a talented postdoctoral research assistant to become a member of the Clarke research group with responsibility for carrying out research in spatial immunometabolism, focused on the GC reaction. The lab is supported by the ERC and the post will also involve providing guidance to less experienced members of the research group, including postdocs, research assistants, technicians, and PhD and project students. You will adapt existing and develop new scientific techniques and experimental protocols as well as tests hypotheses and analyse scientific data from a variety of sources, reviewing and refining working hypotheses as appropriate. You will contribute ideas for new research projects and develop ideas for generating research income, and present detailed research proposals to senior researchers. About You You will hold a PhD (or be near completion) in Immunology or a related area together with relevant experience in standard techniques of immunology. You will have a demonstrable track record in immunology, evidenced by publications in high quality, peer-reviewed journals as well as an understanding of the role of cellular metabolism in the immune system. You will have a a personal licence to work with animals in the UK or be prepared to obtain such a licence via attendance of in-house courses. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please quote 189244 in all correspondence. The closing date for applications is 12pm 9 November 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189244 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £39,424-£46,476 per annum."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189012": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist in AI for Outbreak Response",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist in AI for Outbreak Response Pandemic Sciences Institute, Li Ka Shing Centre for Health and Information Discovery, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF Additionally, Pandemic Sciences Institute, Life and Minds Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL We are seeking to appoint a Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist in AI for Outbreak Response to join the Data Analytics and Epidemiology group at the Pandemic Sciences Institute. Working with Professor Moritz Kraemer, you will contribute to ORCAS (Outbreak Response and Control with AI Support), an international consortium developing AI-driven tools to enable more timely and cost-effective responses to infectious disease outbreaks in Nigeria and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The research will focus on disease forecasting, scenario modelling, and optimal decision making for public health interventions and surveillance. You will be responsible for developing and validating machine learning models for disease forecasting and simulation models for scenario exploration, utilising large-scale data across a range of modalities (e.g., epidemiological, genomic, behavioural). You will contribute to the development of decision-support tools that translate model outputs into actionable insights, and the integration of these tools into existing public health workflows. You will also work closely with public health partners in Nigeria and the DRC to understand their needs, adapt models to local contexts, and support their use through co-design workshops and training. It is essential that you hold a PhD/DPhil with post-qualification research experience in a relevant quantitative discipline, such as epidemiology, statistics, or computational biology. You should have experience developing and deploying machine learning models for disease forecasting and scenario modelling, strong proficiency in Python, R, and/or other programming languages, and experience building reproducible computational pipelines and software. Experience analysing large-scale data and evaluating machine learning models with uncertainty quantification are also essential. Experience with reinforcement learning, phylogenetics/phylodynamics, and developing user-facing dashboards or visualisations to communicate complex scientific findings to non-technical audience would also be advantageous. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 31 March 2028 and is funded by The Gates Foundation. Only applications received before 12 midday on 9 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189012 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Aruna Dhinakaran Vacancy ID : 189012 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 8: £49,119 - £58,265 with a discretionary range to £65,336 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189230": {
      "title": "Research Officer (Comparative Law)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Research Officer (Comparative Law) Oxford Department of International Development, Queen Elizabeth House, 3 Mansfield Road, Oxford OX1 3TB The Oxford Department of International Development (ODID) is seeking to recruit a Research Officer. Reporting to Professor Masooda Bano, the post-holder will join a research team funded by a European Research Council (ERC) Advanced Grant, Choosing Islamic Conservatism: Muslim Youth in Europe and the UK and the Question of Social Cohesion. Building on the earlier phases of the project, the team is now establishing the European Muslim Observatory: Comparative Research Infrastructure on Muslim Institutional Development in Europe. The Observatory aims to develop the first systematic comparative research infrastructure documenting the institutional development of Islam and Muslim communities across Europe. It will bring together legal, political, institutional and religious data to examine how Muslim institutions evolve in interaction with constitutional frameworks, state institutions and wider society, creating a lasting research resource for comparative scholarship on Islam in Europe. The Observatory aims to establish a lasting comparative database that will support future academic research, policy engagement and international collaboration beyond the lifetime of the current ERC grant. Successful applicants will become part of the founding research team responsible for designing, developing and implementing this new research infrastructure. This post is offered full-time for 18 months. The successful candidate will have a Master's degree (or equivalent) in Comparative Law, European Law, Public Law, or a closely related discipline. They will also possess excellent legal research and analytical skills. The ability to analyse complex legal and constitutional materials is essential to this role. At ODID we embrace and cherish our differences, and endeavour to be considerate and welcoming of all. You are most welcome at ODID, without the need to hide any part of who you are. We acknowledge societal inequalities and how these affect us, and those around us, personally and professionally. We hold an Athena Swan bronze award in recognition of our efforts to introduce organisational and cultural changes which promote equality, cherish diversity and create a better working environment for all. We are also taking active steps to promote race equality and reduce the risk of bias and discrimination. We work together to enrich, fortify and grow our community and dedicate our combined efforts to teaching and research to increase our impact and influence in development debates at the national and global level. We encourage all eligible candidates to apply for our vacancies and join us in pursuing our goals. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a CV and supporting statement as part of your online application. Please note that only the CV and supporting statement will be considered; any other documents uploaded will not be reviewed as part of the shortlisting process. Only applications received before 12 midday on Wednesday, 11 November 2026. can be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189230 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@qeh.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - 47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188851": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance Oxford-Man Institute of Quantitative Finance (OMI), Eagle House, Walton Well Road, Oxford Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance (2 Posts) Research Topic - Financial Economics: Asset Pricing and Portfolio Theory This post is fixed-term until Fixed-term for up to 3 years The post-holders will engage in advanced study and academic research and will contribute to the full range of research and academic life within the Oxford-Man Institute (OMI). Reporting to the Director of the OMI, the post-holders will help ensure a healthy and vibrant research environment within the OMI. This will involve leading, devising, coordinating and supervising research projects in this area, including the work involved in the collaborations with project partners, guidance to students, participating in and organising seminars and conferences, and winning further funding to underpin the research. If appropriate, the role may involve acting as a formal co-supervisor for research students. The post holders will hold a relevant PhD/DPhil or be near completion* in one of the following: Finance, Mathematics, Statistics, Econometrics, Computer Science, or a closely related subject (preference will be given to those with a strong focus on data driven models), they will also possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programmes. Experience of supervising academic work is a desirable We proudly hold a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award, which guide our progress towards advancing racial and gender equality. As part of our commitment to openness, inclusivity and transparency, we would particularly welcome applications from women and black and minority ethnic candidates, who are currently under-represented in positions of this type at Oxford. Applicants will be selected for interview purely based on their ability to satisfy the selection criteria as outlined in full in the job description. You will be required to upload a statement setting out how you meet the selection criteria, a curriculum vitae, further details/other documents e.g. publications list and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. Please note that applicants are responsible for contacting their referees and making sure that their letters are sent to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk directly by the closing date quoting the the vacancy reference 188851. Please direct informal enquiries about the post to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 188851 Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 19 November 2026 can be considered. Interviews are anticipated to be held on the first or second week of December 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188851 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@stats.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £41,636- £47,779 779 per annum, with a discretionary range to £51,983 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188853": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance Oxford-Man Institute of Quantitative Finance (OMI), Eagle House, Walton Well Road, Oxford Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantitative Finance (2 posts) Research Topic - Large Language Models, Artificial Intelligence, and Financial Economics This post is fixed-term until Fixed-term for up to 3 years The post-holders will engage in advanced study and academic research on large language models, artificial intelligence, and their applications to economic and financial problems. The successful candidate will contribute to the full range of research and academic life within the Oxford-Man Institute (OMI). Reporting to the Director of the OMI, the post-holder will help ensure a healthy and vibrant research environment within the Institute. This will involve leading, devising, coordinating, and contributing to research projects involving LLMs and related AI systems, including collaborations with project partners, guidance to students, participation in and organisation of seminars and conferences, and development of further research funding. If appropriate, the role may involve acting as a formal co-supervisor for research students. The post holders will hold a relevant PhD/DPhil, or be near completion, in Statistics, Computer Science, Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence, Engineering, Mathematics, Operations Research, Economics, Finance, or a closely related subject. Preference will be given to candidates with strong expertise in large language models, foundation models, or modern AI systems and they will possess sufficient specialist knowledge of LLMs, foundation models, deep learning, NLP, mechanistic interpretability, model evaluation, or related AI methods to work within established and emerging research programmes. Research expertise in mechanistic interpretability of LLMs or foundation models is a desirable. We proudly hold a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award, which guide our progress towards advancing racial and gender equality. As part of our commitment to openness, inclusivity and transparency, we would particularly welcome applications from women and black and minority ethnic candidates, who are currently under-represented in positions of this type at Oxford. Applicants will be selected for interview purely based on their ability to satisfy the selection criteria as outlined in full in the job description. You will be required to upload a statement setting out how you meet the selection criteria, a curriculum vitae, further details/other documents e.g. publications list and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. Please note that applicants are responsible for contacting their referees and making sure that their letters are sent to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk directly by the closing date quoting the the vacancy reference 188853. Please direct informal enquiries about the post to hr@stats.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 188853 Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on 19 November 2026 can be considered. Interviews are anticipated to be held on the first or second week of December 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 188853 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 19-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@stats.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £41,636- £47,779 per annum, with a discretionary range to £53,483 per annum, including the Oxford University Weighting (OUW) and the possibility of a market pay supplement"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188956": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistance in Supernova Cosmology (2 posts)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistance in Supernova Cosmology (2 posts) Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Oxford Applications are invited for postdoctoral positions in supernovae cosmology and time-domain astrophysics with Rubin, 4MOST and Euclid. The candidates will lead research projects related to Type Ia Supernova cosmology, with a focus on data from Rubin, 4MOST and Euclid data. Projects may span a broad range of topics, including improving supernova modelling and standardization, developing and implementing novel data analysis and statistical methods for Type Ia supernova cosmology, and contributing to data acquisition and spectroscopic follow-up of upcoming large transient surveys (e.g. Rubin/ATLAS) using 4MOST and other spectroscopic facilities. The candidates will benefit from the vibrant Astrophysics community at Oxford, which hosts both the Hintze Centre for Astrophysical Surveys and the Beecroft Institute for Particle Astrophysics and Cosmology. The candidates have the opportunity to assist with the supervision of graduate students and outreach activities. Successful candidates should hold PhD (or be close to completion) in a relevant area of astrophysics or physics, and should have experience in Type Ia supernova cosmology (supernova modelling, simulations, data analysis and statistical methods) and/or transient data acquisition. Candidates must demonstrate excellent communication skills. In addition, they may take advantage of opportunities for tutorial-based teaching and may benefit from a college association. Evidence of a capacity to work independently and have sufficient specialist knowledge and technical skills to work within established research programmes are required. Please direct enquiries about the role to Prof. Maria Vincenzi ( Maria.Vincenzi@physics.ox.ac.uk ). This is a full-time post, fixed term until 30 June 2028 . Extension may be possible depending on funding applications. Only applications received before midday on Friday, 20 November 2026 will be considered. You will be required to upload a CV (maximum three pages, including a publication list or a NASA ADS link) and a 2-page supporting statement of research interests and experience. The supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. You should arrange for your 2 referees to send your reference to leanne.odonnell@physics.ox.ac.uk before the closing date. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188956 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 20-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,474 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189048": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Earthquake Nucleation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Earthquake Nucleation Department of Earth Sciences, South Parks Road, Oxford Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Earthquake Nucleation This post is fixed term for 24 months The researcher in this position will join a collaborative team to investigate how earthquakes nucleate. In this NERC-funded project, we are combining experimental, modelling, and seismological investigations to probe the mechanics and conditions of earthquake nucleation. The role here is to take the lead on the seismological and modelling components of the project, with supervision from project co-Lead Jessical Hawthorne as well as Project Lead Stefan Nielsen and Project co-Lead Nicola De Paola at Durham University. The post holder will use a range of seismological analysis tools to identify features that reveal the processes preceding earthquake nucleation. They will, for instance, stack long-period signals from multiple earthquakes and apply novel methods to estimate radiated energy, as well as exploring additional approaches as interest allows. The post holder will use the observations to investigate how nucleation processes vary across different tectonic settings and physical conditions. The post holder will also undertake fault-slip modelling to understand observed nucleation behaviours: both seismic and experimental. They will work closely with all members of the project and use the models to help bridge observations across laboratory and natural scales, and to identify the physical conditions that determine different nucleation behaviours. Applicants should be motivated to think deeply about seismological observations, experiments, and modelling to explore nucleation. They should have experience in seismic data analysis and a track record of robust scientific analysis. For further details of the role please see the job description. The deadline for applications is midday on 23.11.2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a Supporting Statement as part of your application. Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and ethnic minority candidates, who are under-represented in research posts in the Department. Interviews will be held on the 3rd December 2026 Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189048 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : personnel@earth.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £40,514 - £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=187293": {
      "title": "Beecroft Fellow in Cosmology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Beecroft Fellow in Cosmology Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Oxford Applications are invited for a Beecroft Fellowship in Cosmology. The post holder(s) will report to the group leader Prof. Pedro G. Ferreira and other members of the Beecroft Institute of Particle Astrophysics and Cosmology. The post holder will be a member of a disparate research network working independently to carry out research in cosmic microwave background physics or one or more of the following fields: cosmology, galaxy formation, numerical astrophysics, survey science tied to the up and coming large scale surveys, early universe and gravitational physics. The primary focus will be on deploying new machine learning methods, from deep learning to symbolic methods, to extract new physics from up and coming cosmological surveys. The successful candidate will be required to develop a personal research programme in theoretical cosmology (which may include numerical modelling and/or data analysis) and gravitational waves, interacting with faculty members. The candidate will be required to demonstrate the ability to manage their own academic research and administrative activities which may involve small scale project management, to co-ordinate multiple aspects of work to meet deadlines. The candidate will take part in the academic life of BIPAC and the astrophysics group at the Physics Department, and in particular attending and taking part in seminars, colloquia and journal clubs. Candidates should have experience of contributing to publications and/or presentations and be able to contribute new ideas for research projects and research income generation. Applicants should possess, or close to obtaining a doctorate in physics or a related field and ideally a strong background in Physics or Astrophysics. Previous experience in the field of Cosmology, Computational Astrophysics, Early Universe or Gravitational Physics will be an advantage. The post-holder will have the opportunity to teach. This is a full time, fixed term post for 36 months. Please direct enquiries about the role to pedro.ferreira@physics.ox.ac.uk Only applications received before midday Monday, 30 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a supporting statement, CV and details of two referees as part of your online application. Your referees should be asked to send their letters, before the application deadline, to leanne.odonnell@physics.ox.ac.uk. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 187293 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,424 - £ 47, 779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=187767": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantum Simulation with Dipolar Gases",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Quantum Simulation with Dipolar Gases Contact Person : Vacancy ID : 187767 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189234": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in mRNA Vaccine Manufatoring",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in mRNA Vaccine Manufatoring Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ The Nuffield Department of Medicine is recruiting a Postdoctoral Researcher in mRNA Vaccine Manufacturing to join the Centre for Immuno-Oncology and contribute to the UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project. You will work within a national programme developing personalised neoantigen mRNA cancer vaccines, bringing together AI-led target discovery, advanced immunology, automated laboratories, near-patient manufacturing and NHS clinical trials. You will contribute to the programme’s manufacturing workstream, taking individualised mRNA vaccine candidates from construct design and in vitro transcription through formulation, analytical characterisation and release testing, and preparing the process for transfer into GMP manufacture for a first-in-human trial. You will develop and optimise lipid nanoparticle or lipoplex formulations, establish assays for RNA identity, integrity, purity and potency, and produce the quality and regulatory documentation required to support clinical translation. You will also analyse experimental, genomic and clinical data, contribute to publications and presentations, and act as a source of expertise on RNA handling, assay development and manufacturing workflows across the wider group. You should hold a PhD/DPhil, or be close to completion, in molecular biology, biochemistry, immunology or a related discipline, with relevant research experience. You will need practical experience in the design, development or prototyping of mRNA vaccines or other RNA-based medicines, together with strong experimental biology skills including mammalian cell culture, fluorescence microscopy, sequencing and flow cytometry/FACS. You should be able to work independently to develop and validate new assays and protocols, integrate genomic, experimental and clinical information, manage your own research activity and communicate effectively across laboratory, computational and clinical teams. Experience in mRNA manufacturing workflows, AI or automation, clinical research, or evidence of national scientific or academic recognition would be advantageous. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 September 2030 and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on 22 October 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189234 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 189234 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 22-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189245": {
      "title": "Senior Researcher in Agentic AI for Cancer Vaccine Development",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Senior Researcher in Agentic AI for Cancer Vaccine Development Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ The Nuffield Department of Medicine is recruiting a Senior Researcher in Agentic AI for Cancer Vaccines to join the Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology and take a leading role in the UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project. This government-supported national programme brings together sovereign AI supercomputing, AI Scientists, automated laboratories, advanced immunology and near-patient manufacturing to accelerate the development of personalised cancer vaccines for NHS patients. Reporting to Dr Lennard Lee, you will serve as Research Group Leader / Senior Researcher within the programme, leading a significant interdisciplinary research project and helping shape its scientific direction, technical delivery and wider strategy. You will provide specialist leadership across artificial intelligence, agentic AI, computational biology, scientific software and research computing, including development of the programme’s AI Scientist and its integration with frontier and open-source AI models, sovereign supercomputing and autonomous laboratory technologies. You will also manage research staff and resources, contribute to funding applications, produce significant research outputs and build collaborations across AI, cancer biology, research computing and laboratory automation. You should hold a relevant PhD/DPhil with substantial post-qualification research experience and have a recognised national research reputation in artificial intelligence, computational biology, AI-enabled science or a related area. You will need a strong publication or technical research record, experience of leading complex interdisciplinary projects and managing research or technical staff, and demonstrable experience of designing and building agentic AI systems or comparable AI-enabled scientific software. Practical experience with frontier AI systems, open-source foundation models, AI or research-computing infrastructure and integration with laboratory automation is essential, together with significant knowledge or research experience in cancer biology, genomics, immunology or a closely related field. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 September 2030 and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on 22 October 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189245 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 189245 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 22-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 9 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 9: £56,623 - £65,336 with a discretionary range to £73,296 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189324": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Computer Vision for Sport Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Computer Vision for Sport Medicine Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher, to work within the Podium Institute for Sports Medicine and Technology at the University of Oxford. Established in October 2022 the Podium Institute sits within the Institute of Biomedical Engineering (IBME) in the University’s Department of Engineering Science. It constitutes a world-unique ecosystem within which to develop and validate new technologies for the diagnosis, prevention, and management of sport injuries, with emphasis on safety rather than performance in the community, youth (11-18 years of age) and women’s sport. This full-time post is initially fixed-term for 18 months with possible extension. You will work at the intersection of computer vision, artificial intelligence and sports medicine, collaborating closely with researchers within The Podium Institute, the Visual Geometry Group (VGG), the wider Department of Engineering Science, and clinical and biomedical departments across the University. You will join a highly interdisciplinary team of computer vision, machine learning, engineering, neuroscience, clinical and sports medicine researchers working together to develop innovative technologies for understanding athlete movement, identifying injury mechanisms, assessing injury risk and supporting evidence-based injury prevention strategies. A particular focus of the role is the 3D reconstruction of athletes and their interactions from monocular, multi-view and broadcast video, including in contact sports such as rugby, where dense contact, occlusion and close interaction between players make injury mechanisms difficult to capture. You should hold, or be near completion of, a PhD/DPhil in Computer Vision, Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence, Engineering Science, Computer Science, Robotics, Biomedical Engineering, or a related discipline. You should have strong programming skills in relevant scientific computing frameworks and languages (e.g. Python, PyTorch, TensorFlow or equivalent), and have experience developing, implementing and evaluating machine learning or computer vision models using real-world image and video datasets, including in-the-wild or sports footage. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Christian Rupprecht (email: christian.rupprecht@cs.ox.ac.uk). For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on the 26th October 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Professor Christian Rupprecht Vacancy ID : 189324 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : christian.rupprecht@cs.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum (Inclusive of Oxford University Weighting)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189246": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Cancer Vaccine Epitope Discovery & Future mRNA Manufacturing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Research Assistant in Cancer Vaccine Epitope Discovery & Future mRNA Manufacturing Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a Research Assistant (AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology) to join the Nuffield Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, working on an state of the art project funded by Advanced Research & Innovation Agency. Reporting to Dr Lennard Lee, Programme Director, the post holder will serve as a Cancer Vaccine Epitope Discovery & Future mRNA Manufacturing Research Assistant for the UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project. The UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project is a government-supported national flagship research initiative hosted by the University of Oxford. The project brings together advanced immunology, automated laboratories, sovereign AI supercomputing, and AI Scientists to develop and manufacture safer, more precise and more effective personalised cancer vaccines. Experimental findings are rapidly fed back to the AI scientist, enabling increasingly accurate predictions and accelerating translation of scientific discoveries towards NHS patient benefit. You will provide experimental research support across cancer vaccine epitope discovery and the development of future approaches to personalised mRNA vaccine manufacturing. They will contribute to the experimental validation of AI-predicted cancer vaccine targets using immunological assays and support the development, manufacture and characterisation of experimental mRNA vaccine candidates. They will work closely with immunologists, computational scientists, AI researchers and autonomous laboratory scientists, helping connect AI-led target discovery with experimental validation and vaccine manufacturing. The project has been recognised as one of the UK’s leading strategic research initiatives, having been referenced within both the Government’s AI for Science Strategy and the 10-Year National Cancer Plan. It is supported by major UK research funders and government organisations, including ARIA, the Medical Research Council, UK Research and Innovation, Cancer Research UK and the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology, alongside a consortium of more than 2,500 scientists, clinicians, engineers, patient representatives and technology experts. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative multidisciplinary team working at the interface of artificial intelligence, computational biology, cancer immunology, robotics, software engineering and clinical research, contributing to research that aims to establish new approaches for the discovery and development of personalised cancer vaccines. You will provide computational research support within the programme, working with large-scale cancer, genomic and immunological datasets and applying established and emerging artificial intelligence approaches to cancer vaccine research. They will use frontier and open-source AI models and agentic coding tools, contribute to the development and evaluation of AI models and scientific agents, and support computational analyses alongside cancer biologists, immunologists and AI researchers. The post holder will manage their own research and administrative activities within agreed guidelines and contribute to project planning, data analysis, research outputs and collaborative research. It is essential that you hold a degree in immunology, biomedical sciences, molecular biology, biochemistry, cancer biology or a related discipline and possess sufficient specialist knowledge of immunology, cancer biology, molecular biology or a related discipline to work within established research programmes. With experience analysing biological, genomic, biomedical or other large-scale research datasets using computational methods, you will have the ability to manage own research, laboratory and administrative activities within agreed guidelines. Practical experience of laboratory research, with experience in one or more relevant techniques such as mammalian cell culture, immunological assays, flow cytometry, fluorescence microscopy, sequencing or molecular biology is also essential. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 September 2030 and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on Monday, 26 October 2026 will be considered. Please quote **189246 **on all correspondence. Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 189246 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 6: £35,681- £41,636 with a discretionary range to £46,476 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189278": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist Department of Physiology, Anatomy & Genetics, Institute of Developmental and Regenerative Medicine (IDRM), IMS-Tetsuya Nakamura Building, Old Road Campus, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7TY About us: At the Department of Physiology Anatomy & Genetics (DPAG) we undertake discovery science where we reassemble physiological processes at the molecular, cellular, tissue and systems level of organisation. In so doing we provide a bridge to translational medicine, and interface between physical and life sciences. We are committed not only to innovative research and the highest standard of teaching, but also to creating an inclusive and supportive working environment. Overview of the role: We are seeking three Postdoctoral Research Associates to investigate an exciting and emerging area of tissue biology: how the extracellular matrix (ECM) acts as an essential signalling network in the establishment and maintenance of tissue function throughout the lifespan. The ECM is a critical but relatively understudied component of tissue biology. Not only is its healthy regulation essential for tissue function, but its dysfunction drives chronic diseases. Understanding its role requires a highly interdisciplinary approach that integrates principles from biology, physics and engineering. All projects will be undertaken in close collaboration with research groups across the University of Oxford. One position will focus on the role of ECM and basement membrane in development, in collaboration with Shankar Srinivas’s laboratory; one will investigate the role of ECM regulation in joint degeneration and regeneration in osteoarthritis, working with Tonia Vincent’s laboratory; and one will explore ECM-mediated mechanisms of tissue repair in spiny mouse, a model system of scarless wound healing, in collaboration with Paul Riley’s laboratory. The ideal candidate will hold or be close to completion of a PhD/DPhil with sufficient specialist knowledge in mechanobiology, tissue biology, inflammatory disease, or developmental biology to work within established research programmes. This post is available from 01st November 2026 until 26th August 2027 in the first instance. Key responsibilities: Manage own academic research and administrative activities. This involves small scale project management, to co-ordinate multiple aspects of work to meet deadlines. Adapt existing and develop new scientific techniques and experimental protocols. Test hypotheses and analyse scientific data from a variety of sources, reviewing and refining working hypotheses as appropriate. Contribute ideas for new research projects. Collaborate in the preparation of scientific reports and journal articles and occasionally present papers and posters. Use specialist scientific equipment in a laboratory environment. Act as a source of information and advice to other members of the group on scientific protocols and experimental techniques. Represent the research group at external meetings/seminars, either with other members of the group or alone. Carry out collaborative projects with colleagues in partner institutions, and research groups. Contribute to discussions and share research findings with colleagues in partner institutions, and research groups. Selection criteria: Hold, or be close to completion of, a PhD/DPhil in the biological sciences or biomedical engineering, together with relevant experience. Possess sufficient specialist knowledge in mechanobiology, tissue biology, inflammatory disease, or developmental biology to work within established research programmes. Ability to manage own academic research and associated activities. Previous experience of contributing to publications/presentations. Ability to contribute ideas for new research projects and research income generation. Excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings. Experience of creating collaborative and inclusive environments with people from different backgrounds. What we offer: Your wellbeing at work matters, so we offer a range of family friendly and financial benefits including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle and electric car loan schemes Employee Assistance Programme Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans While this is a full-time role, we welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. How to apply: Please provide a supporting statement outlining how you meet the selection criteria along with your CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is 12 noon on 29th October 2026 . Interviews are likely to take place during the week commencing 9th November 2026, and will be face to face. Applications are particularly welcome from women, black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. Follow us: Stay connected with us on LinkedIn , Bluesky and Instagram to learn more about our work and culture. Informal enquiries about the role may be directed to the DPAG HR Team: hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk DPAG’s Statement of Inclusion: We, as a Department and Community, will be considerate and welcoming of all people, regardless of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, and socio-economic background. We acknowledge societal inequalities and how these impact us, and those around us, personally and professionally. Our policies, practices and Respectful Behaviours Framework underpin this commitment. DPAG and Sustainability: We have signed up to The Laboratory Efficiency Assessment Framework (LEAF) and Green Impact, actively implementing and encouraging eco-friendly practices that reduce waste, promote energy efficiency, and promote bio-diversity. See the job description for more detail. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189278 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 29-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188942": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in frequency and bandwidth conversion of single photons for distributed entanglement networks",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in frequency and bandwidth conversion of single photons for distributed entanglement networks Department of Materials About this role The Department of Materials at the University of Oxford is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to the Integrated Quantum Networks Hub (IQN Hub), a major UK initiative developing the next generation of quantum networking technologies. This exciting role focuses on frequency and bandwidth conversion of single photons for distributed entanglement networks, supporting the development of practical quantum networking demonstrators. The successful candidate will undertake a comprehensive review of existing wavelength and bandwidth conversion technologies and help define strategies for integrating quantum light sources and quantum memories into future network architectures. You will: Lead a technical review of wavelength and bandwidth conversion methods for quantum networks. Develop a strategic roadmap for implementation within the IQN Hub. Collaborate with leading academic and industry partners, including Covesion Ltd . Contribute to publications and written reports. Present findings and engage with researchers across the Hub network. About You We are looking for a talented and motivated researcher with a PhD/DPhil in Physics and a strong background in quantum optics. You will have excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication and present complex scientific ideas clearly to a range of audiences. Strong project management and organisational skills are essential, together with experience contributing to research publications and presentations. The successful candidate will also be willing to travel within the UK to collaborate with partners across the Integrated Quantum Networks Hub. Desirable experience includes working with single-photon frequency conversion technologies and contributing to collaborative research publications. About the Project This project forms part of the Integrated Quantum Networks Hub and focuses on one of the key challenges in the development of future quantum communication systems: the frequency and bandwidth conversion of single photons for distributed entanglement networks. The successful candidate will undertake a comprehensive review of existing approaches and technologies for wavelength and bandwidth conversion, assessing their suitability for connecting quantum light sources and quantum memory platforms within practical quantum networks. Working closely with academic collaborators across the Hub and industry partner Covesion Ltd, the postholder will help develop strategies for integrating these technologies into network demonstrators, ranging from long-distance optical fibre links of approximately 100 km to shorter-range heterogeneous quantum networks. The outcomes of this work will help shape the future architecture of scalable quantum networks and support the UK's ambitions in quantum technology research and innovation. How to Apply All applications must be made online using the Oxford University E-Recruitment system, no later than 12 noon on 30 October 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a Supporting Statement as part of your application . Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. Only applications received online by 12.00 midday (GMT) on 30 October 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled to take place at the Department of Materials on Friday 6 November 2026 and you must be available on this date, either by Teams or in person. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 188942 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@materials.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189265": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biomechanical Modelling and Wearable Robotics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biomechanical Modelling and Wearable Robotics central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant to join RADlab (www.radlab-oxford.com) at the Department of Engineering Science (central Oxford). The post is funded by UKRI and is fixed-term to 31st May 2028. You will develop computational models to guide the design of wearable robots that deliver assistive forces and joint torques. The research will examine how robotic assistance affects human movement, muscle activity and joint loading, supporting the development of effective soft and hybrid wearable devices. You will be responsible for: Developing musculoskeletal models of human movement to predict muscle forces, joint loads and the effects of wearable robotic assistance. Simulating human-robot interaction to determine the force, torque and timing requirements of assistive devices during relevant movements, including upper-body tasks and/or gait cycles. Using simulation and optimisation to guide actuator placement, device geometry and assistance strategies, accounting for the mechanical behaviour of soft actuators and their attachment to the body. Collaborating with experimental researchers to validate models against motion, force and, where available, EMG measurements, and to inform successive device designs. You should possess: A PhD, or be near completion of a PhD, in Engineering, Biomechanics, Robotics, Computational Mechanics or a closely related discipline. Research experience in musculoskeletal modelling or the simulation of human movement, including upper-body biomechanics and/or gait analysis. A strong understanding of human movement mechanics, including inverse dynamics, muscle recruitment and the interpretation of joint forces and torques. Proficiency in relevant modelling tools, such as OpenSim, AnyBody or equivalent software, and in Python or MATLAB for numerical analysis and optimisation. Experience in wearable robotics, soft robotics, exoskeletons or closely related human-robot interaction research. Experience with rigid or hybrid exoskeletons is also relevant. A record of peer-reviewed publications appropriate to your career stage, strong communication skills and the ability to work independently and collaboratively to meet research milestones. Experience in EMG processing, wearable sensor data analysis, assistive control strategies or finite-element modelling of soft devices and body interfaces would be advantageous. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Prof Antonio Elia Forte (email: antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on 2nd November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Modelling, Robotics, Wearable-Robotics, Exoskeleton, Simulation Contact Person : Antonio Forte Vacancy ID : 189265 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189266": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Wearable Robotics and Experimental Biomechanics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Wearable Robotics and Experimental Biomechanics central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant to join RADlab (www.radlab-oxford.com) at the Department of Engineering Science (central Oxford). The post is funded by UKRI and is fixed-term to 31st May 2028. You will develop wearable robotic devices that deliver assistive forces and joint torques, with a particular focus on upper-body movement. The research will combine soft robotic actuation, device fabrication and experimental biomechanics. You will be responsible for: 1. Designing and fabricating wearable soft robotic devices that provide mechanical assistance during upper-body movements. 2. Developing experimental protocols to characterise actuator force, joint assistance, range of motion and the transfer of loads between the device and the body. 3. Evaluating and optimising device performance, repeatability, durability and comfort through mechanical testing and human movement measurements. 4. Collaborating with researchers in biomechanical modelling and robotics to translate experimental findings into improved designs. You should possess: 1. A PhD, or be near completion of a PhD, in Engineering, Biomechanics, Robotics or a closely related discipline. 2. Experience in experimental biomechanics, preferably involving the upper body, including the shoulder, arm or trunk. 3. Strong practical expertise in the design, fabrication and testing of robotic devices, including relevant techniques such as moulding, casting, additive manufacturing or textile fabrication. 4. Research experience in soft robotics, wearable robotics or exoskeletons. Experience with rigid or hybrid exoskeletons is also relevant. 5. Experience in actuator characterisation, instrumentation and data acquisition, with proficiency in Python or MATLAB for experimental data analysis. 6. A record of peer-reviewed publications appropriate to your career stage, strong communication skills and the ability to work independently and collaboratively to meet research milestones. Experience in human movement analysis, motion capture, wearable sensing, EMG or the integration of robotic control systems would be advantageous. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Prof Antonio Elia Forte (email: antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on 2nd November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Soft-Robotics, Robotics, Wearable-Robotics, Exoskeleton, Mechanics Contact Person : Antonio Forte Vacancy ID : 189266 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188879": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist in Molecular Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist in Molecular Biology Centre for Human Genetics, Building for Genomic Medicine, Roosevelt Drive, Oxford, OX3 7BN We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Scientist in Molecular Biology to work on synthetic genome DNA assembly in the University of Oxford. This is an exciting opportunity arising from a major new grant of £6.7 million from the Advanced Research and Invention Agency (ARIA) – a UK government funding agency. You will work with Professor Chris O'Callaghan and Dr Nathali Flores-Fernández on further development and application of our DNA assembly technology to create synthetic organelle genomes. This synthetic biology project aims to move rapidly to translation with real-world testing of synthetic chloroplast genomes in relevant crop plants including potato ( Solanum tuberosum ) and wheat ( Triticum aestivum ) with Professors Steve Kelly and Francesco Licausi. Synthetic genomes will incorporate multiple variants that have arisen independently in nature and have potential to improve desirable properties, such as resistance to disease and environmental/ climate stress. This is a great opportunity to develop skills in state-of-the-art techniques while working on a project with major potential for high impact. Your primary role will be to assist in planning and to carry out experiments and analyse the results within Professor Chris O’Callaghan’s laboratory (https://www.chg.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/ocallaghan-group-1). This will involve molecular biology and biochemistry to undertake DNA assembly including generating constructs and testing new approaches. This is a translational project to further develop and apply technology that we have invented to assemble large DNA molecules of any sequence with ease. Using this technology, it is possible to rapidly and simply assemble DNA molecules up to hundreds of thousands of base-pairs in length. We have progressively improved and simplified this technology which uses recombinant DNA methylases as published in PubMed 40548934, PubMed 41858718, and PubMed 38270650. There may also be some expression and purification of recombinant enzymes such as methylases. There is also scope for some bioinformatic/ computational analysis but this is primarily a laboratory-based post. The laboratory is well equipped for this work and for testing the function of assembled DNA molecules. The project will enjoy a high level of funding and if successful there is potential for a further 2 or 3-year phase of funding to be awarded. The project collaborators include Professor Francesco Licausi and Professor Steve Kelly. You will be responsible for managing your own academic research and administrative activities, adapting existing and developing new scientific techniques and experimental protocols. It is essential that you hold a PhD/DPhil (or be close to completion) in a relevant discipline such as molecular/synthetic biology, biochemistry, biotechnology or related subject. You will also have proven experience and confidence in molecular cloning and DNA assembly, working with and extraction of long DNA molecules, bacterial cultures, and recombinant protein production and purification. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 31 May 2028 and is funded by the Advanced Research and Innovation Agency (ARIA). Only applications received before 12 midday on 4 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 188879 on all correspondence. Informal enquiries should be addressed to Prof Chris O’Callaghan (chris.ocallaghan@ndm.ox.ac.uk). Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 188879 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £43,984. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189339": {
      "title": "Clinical Trial Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Clinical Trial Manager 189339 Clinical Trial Manager Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 7: Salary in the range of £39,424-£47,779 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full time (part-time at minimum of 80% FTE will be considered), fixed term position for 3 years. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. The Kadoorie Institute of Trauma, Emergency and Critical Care brings together interdisciplinary research across trauma and rehabilitation, emergency care and critical care, with a focus on improving outcomes for patients with life-threatening illness and injury. The Institute integrates expertise across these closely connected specialties within a single research environment. Based at the John Radcliffe Hospital, the centre of urgent and emergency care in Oxford, the Institute supports more than 100 multidisciplinary researchers working across the breadth of trauma, emergency and critical care research. Its research spans the use of routinely collected healthcare data and early- and late-phase clinical trials, enabling discoveries to be translated into improvements in clinical practice and patient care. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working About the Role In this role, you will work autonomously as a Trial Manager, taking overall responsibility for the day-to-day management of multi-centre randomised clinical trials. You will assist in finalising the protocol, study materials, training packages for collaborating sites and trial manuals, and prepare trial specific instructions. You will work to design data collection forms and associated documentation as required in collaboration with the trial statistician and Chief Investigator – then work with the trial programmer to assist in the design and testing of the trial database systems for randomisation, data entry, validation and report generation. This post involves obtaining and maintaining overall and site-specific ethical and R&D approvals for all sites and ensure Clinical Trial Agreements are in place before the research commences as well as providing line management and training for administrative trial staff (e.g. trial administrators, data entry clerks). About You You must hold a degree in a biomedical or associated subject, or other relevant qualification, or proven experience in clinical research. You will have experience of overseeing the conduct of multi-centre clinical trials and working to a high standard of Good Clinical Practice and according to the research governance framework. Experience and understanding of techniques for the management of medical research data are essential. Previous involvement in development of clinical reporting forms, as well as experience working with REDCap, is desirable. Application Process The University of Oxford is committed to equality and valuing diversity. All applicants will be judged on merit, according to the selection criteria. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and must include a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the selection criteria, using examples of your skills and experience. Interviews will take place during the week commencing 16 November and will be face to face/online. Please quote reference 189339 in all correspondence. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189339 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £39,424-£47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189248": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher - AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher - AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Researcher (AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology) to join the Nuffield Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, working on an state of the art project funded by Advanced Research & Innovation Agency. The UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project is a government-supported national flagship research initiative hosted by the University of Oxford. The project brings together sovereign AI supercomputing, AI Scientists, automated laboratories, and immunology to develop and manufacture safer, more precise and more effective personalised cancer vaccines. To accelerate the development of personalised cancer vaccines for NHS patients, we seek to create an AI scientist capable of reviewing scientific discovery, generating hypotheses, designing experiments, controlling laboratory workflows, and analysing results. By integrating advanced machine learning with robotic laboratory automation, experimental findings are rapidly fed back to the AI scientist, enabling increasingly accurate predictions and accelerating the pace at which new scientific discoveries can be translated towards patient benefit. The project has been recognised as one of the UK’s leading strategic research initiatives, having been referenced within both the Government’s AI for Science Strategy and the 10-Year National Cancer Plan. It is supported by major UK research funders and government organisations, including ARIA, the Medical Research Council, UK Research and Innovation, Cancer Research UK and the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology, alongside a consortium of more than 2,500 scientists, clinicians, engineers, patient representatives and technology experts. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative multidisciplinary team working at the interface of artificial intelligence, computational biology, cancer immunology, robotics, software engineering and clinical research, contributing to research that aims to establish new approaches for the discovery and development of personalised cancer vaccines. You will take responsibility for a discrete area of computational research within the programme, developing and applying artificial intelligence and computational biology approaches to large-scale cancer datasets. They will use frontier and open-source AI models, develop AI models and agentic scientific systems, and integrate genomic, immunological and experimental data to improve cancer vaccine target discovery and prediction. They will work closely with AI researchers, cancer biologists, immunologists and autonomous laboratory scientists and provide guidance to junior members of the research group. It is essential that you hold a relevant PhD/DPhil (or be close to completion) in artificial intelligence, computational biology, bioinformatics, cancer biology, genomics or a closely related discipline. You will possess strong computational research skills and demonstrable experience analysing large-scale biological, genomic or biomedical datasets. Similarly, with practical experience using frontier AI models and AI coding or agent systems, for example Codex or comparable agentic coding tools, you will demonstrate sufficient specialist knowledge of cancer biology, cancer genomics or tumour immunology to work effectively within established cancer research programmes. An understanding of cancer vaccines, neoantigen discovery, tumour antigens or cancer immunology and their application to personalised medicine and ability to manage own academic research and independently deliver a discrete area of computational research is essential. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 September 2030 and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on Thursday, 5 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189248 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 189248 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53, 483p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189315": {
      "title": "Policy and Research Specialist (What Works Hub for Global Education)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Policy and Research Specialist (What Works Hub for Global Education) Blavatnik School of Government, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford OX26GG About the Blavatnik School of Government Our vision is of a world better led, a world better served and a world better governed. We are a global school committed to improving the quality of government and public policymaking worldwide, through three routes: teaching current and future leaders ; generating research with impact ; and engaging with governments and practitioners . What we offer The Blavatnik School of Government is a collaborative, friendly, and dynamic department based in an award-winning building in Oxford. We host regular events with leading global figures, all of which are open to staff. On the social side, we have regular staff coffee/cake mornings, a family-friendly Halloween event, and Christmas and summer parties. As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing, and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days’ annual leave Family leave schemes and a comprehensive range of childcare services Cycle loan scheme and discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs Opportunities for remote working. More information about working at the School can be found on our jobs page. About the role The post-holder will manage and deliver applied research across multiple workstreams within the What Works Hub for Global Education (WWHGE), contributing to evidence-informed education policy and reform. They will design and apply qualitative and quantitative research methods, analyse evidence from diverse sources and produce high-quality research outputs, including reports, policy briefs and publications. Working collaboratively with researchers, governments and international partners, the post-holder will translate research findings into practical policy recommendations and contribute to technical assistance, capacity-strengthening activities and policy engagement with governments and implementers. They will also support the development of new research studies, coordinate complex projects and contribute to programme planning, stakeholder engagement and the wider dissemination of research findings. About you You will hold a Master's degree in a relevant discipline, with a PhD/DPhil preferred, and have specialist knowledge of international education, public policy, implementation research or a related field. You will have experience managing research projects, applying qualitative and/or quantitative methodologies, analysing complex evidence and producing high-quality research outputs for academic and policy audiences. You will have excellent analytical, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to present findings clearly to diverse audiences. You will also have experience working across interdisciplinary and international partnerships, developing evidence-informed policy and delivery plans, and supporting capacity-building activities in low- and middle-income country partner organisations and / or governments. Strong organisational skills, the ability to manage competing priorities and experience contributing to research proposals and funding opportunities are also essential. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement, your CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is 12 noon (UK time) on Monday, 9 November 2026 . We anticipate that interviews will take place during week commencing 16th or 23rd November 2026. Contact Person : HR team Vacancy ID : 189315 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@bsg.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 (with a discretionary range to £49,119) per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189340": {
      "title": "Senior Researcher",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Senior Researcher Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Oxford The Department of Physics at the University of Oxford invites applications for the position of Senior Researcher in Technosignature Research within Breakthrough Listen, the world’s leading scientific programme dedicated to the search for extraterrestrial intelligence (SETI). This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to pioneering research at the intersection of radio astronomy, signal processing, machine learning, and large-scale scientific data analysis. The successful candidate will play a leading role in the strategic and operational development of Breakthrough Listen, shaping its research priorities, managing stakeholder relationships, and driving interdisciplinary research at the forefront of SETI science. Key responsibilities include: Lead Research Strategy and Vision: Establish and develop a dedicated research group, initiate long-term research programmes, secure research funding, and chair key committees. Oversee Advanced Technical Computing: Lead the management and development of Breakthrough Listen’s computing infrastructure and assets within the SKA Lab, drawing on specialist expertise in high-performance computing (HPC), CUDA, and field-programmable gate array (FPGA) technologies. Represent Breakthrough Listen Internationally: Serve as a leading representative of SETI research to industry, research councils, and international academic institutions; present findings at major international conferences; and build and strengthen global research collaborations. The candidate will bring scientific and technical expertise, together with an international research profile, commensurate with that expected of a senior academic. They will also demonstrate a clear and ambitious vision for the future development of Breakthrough Listen and its associated research programmes at Oxford and internationally. The candidate must hold a PhD/DPhil. Substantial experience in developing and implementing research strategies, recruiting and managing staff, overseeing budgets, securing research funding, and producing high-quality proposals, reports, and scientific publications is essential for this post. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential, together with extensive experience of engaging with and balancing the needs of diverse international stakeholders, including funders, collaborators, human resources professionals, observatory directors, policymakers, early-career researchers, and the wider public. For informal queries, please contact Therese Docherty therese.docherty@physics.ox.ac.uk The post is full time and fixed term for 2 years with possibility of extension. The closing date for applications is 12 PM, Monday, 9 November 2026. As part of your application you will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement and to provide details of two referees. Your referees should be asked to send their letter, to Leanne O’Donnell ( leanne.odonnell@physics.ox.ac.uk ) by the closing date. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189340 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 10 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 10RS: £65,336 - £ 75,439 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189277": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Statistical Machine Learning for Genomics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Statistical Machine Learning for Genomics Statistics, 24-29 St Giles', Oxford, OX1 3LB We invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Statistical Machine Learning for Genomics to join the Palamara Group at the University of Oxford, funded by the European Research Council (ERC). Working with Professor Pier Palamara, you will develop novel statistical and machine learning methods for large-scale genomic, phenotypic and health data to address questions in human genetics and evolution. The research programme brings together statistical inference, machine learning and AI to study the genetic basis of complex traits and how evolution shapes their genetic architectures. Possible directions include genomic language models, multimodal learning integrating genomic and health data with functional, genealogical and evolutionary information, federated learning, and multi-trait and multi-ancestry methods. Applications include association testing, fine-mapping and polygenic prediction. You will develop scalable methods, implement reproducible research software and apply them to biobank-scale datasets, rigorously evaluating their statistical properties and predictive performance. You will prepare publications, present findings, collaborate with project researchers and provide guidance to junior researchers. You should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in machine learning, statistics, computer science, computational biology, statistical genetics or another relevant quantitative discipline. You should have a strong foundation in statistical inference and machine learning, with experience developing novel methods and evaluating their statistical and empirical performance. Strong research software skills and experience with large-scale or complex quantitative data are also essential. Previous experience in genomics or evolutionary biology is desirable but not essential. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Pier Palamara at palamara@stats.ox.ac.uk. Further information about the group is available at https://palamaralab.github.io/. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 36 months and is funded by the European Research Council. Only applications received before 12 midday (UK time) on 10 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote the vacancy reference 189277 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Vacancy ID : 189277 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : palamara@stats.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: salary in range £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189274": {
      "title": "Senior Researcher in Probabilistic Machine Learning for AI Governence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Senior Researcher in Probabilistic Machine Learning for AI Governence central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Senior Researcher to join the Bayesian Governance Lab, led by Professor Maike Osborne, in the Department of Engineering Science (central Oxford), working in close collaboration with the Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative. The post is fixed-term for two years. The AI systems that most demand assurance learn, act and change by the week, so evaluating them is a problem of sequential Bayesian inference and experimental design rather than of static benchmarking. You will lead a strand of research on Bayesian methods for the evaluation and monitoring of AI systems, publish your findings, lead the lab’s open-source software, translate results into policy-facing outputs, and guide research assistants and students. You should hold a PhD/DPhil in machine learning, statistics or a closely related discipline, with post-qualification research experience and a strong publication record, and have specialist knowledge of Bayesian machine learning or applied probability (for example Gaussian processes, sequential inference or Bayesian optimisation). Familiarity with the evaluation or governance of AI systems is desirable. Whilst the role is a grade 8 £49,119 - £59,955 per annum, we would be willing to consider candidates with potential but less experience or qualification who are seeking a development opportunity, for which an initial appointment would be at grade 7 £39,424 - £47,779 per annum where the individual will undertake all the duties but with increased supervision and/or less responsibility. Where a post is underfilled, a development plan will be put in place stating clear milestones which must be met by the individual in order to be promoted to the substantive grade. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Maike Osborne (maike.osborne@eng.ox.ac.uk). Only applications received before midday on 10 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a CV, a supporting statement and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award in recognition of its commitment to gender equality. Keywords: Bayesian inference; Gaussian processes; machine learning; AI governance; AI evaluation Contact Person : Maike Osborne Vacancy ID : 189274 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : maike.osborne@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 8 £49,119 - £59,955 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189220": {
      "title": "Live Well Implementation Senior Post Doctoral Researcher :  NIHR Applied Research Collaboration (ARC) Thames Valley",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Live Well Implementation Senior Post Doctoral Researcher : NIHR Applied Research Collaboration (ARC) Thames Valley Department of Psychiatry, University of Oxford, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. This is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the TV ARC programme under the Live Well theme which addresses Social and Commercial Determinants of Health. Our work is organised across three thematic areas- Start Well, Live Well, Age Well- along with five cross cutting themes: digital; health tech and data; community, social, and home care; knowledge mobilisation, implementation, and partnerships; research inclusion and health inequalities; planning and delivering social care. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The post is available on a flexible hybrid basis. The minimum on-site time is 3 days/week for a full-time post. About the Role The post is funded for 48 months and is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. You will develop research questions within the livewell theme , conduct individual research, analysing detailed and complex qualitative and/or quantitative data from a variety of sources, and generate original ideas by building on existing concepts, demonstrating independence and high-level expertise as part of the ARC. You will lead in research implementation, including study setup; recruitment; consent processes; data collection, monitoring and analysis; and publication, working closely with the senior research team About You You will hold a relevant Ph.D/D.Phil with post-qualification research experience. You will have a strong publication record and familiarity with the existing literature and research in the field. Experience of supervising research internships or other student activity is desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity Our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 11 November 2026. Contact Person : HR Administrator Vacancy ID : 189220 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £49119 -£58265 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188876": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant Centre for Human Genetics, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Oxford, OX3 7BN We are seeking a highly motivated and skilled researcher to join our team and contribute to an exciting programme of immunological and infectious disease research. The successful candidate will develop research questions within a defined scientific context, undertake independent research and analyse complex qualitative and quantitative data from a range of sources. You will have the opportunity to generate original ideas, build on existing concepts and contribute to the development of new research projects and funding proposals, while working collaboratively with colleagues across the Department and partner institutions. You will be responsible for managing your own research and laboratory activities, planning and coordinating experimental work to support the successful delivery of defined projects. You will develop, optimise and apply analytical protocols and experimental techniques, using specialist knowledge to test hypotheses and refine research approaches. As a Postdoctoral Research Assistant, you will use scientific laboratory equipment, analysis and interpret experimental data, and collaborate with colleagues to provide advice on research methods and techniques. You will contribute to research publications, including peer-reviewed journal articles, book chapters and reviews, and communicate findings through national conferences and seminars. You will also support collaborative projects, organise and delegate activities where appropriate. Coaching colleagues in specialist methodologies and contribute to the Department and University’s public engagement and widening access activities is also a key part to the role. It is essential that you hold a relevant PhD/DPhil, or equivalent qualification in viral immunology, cellular immunology or a related subject. Having demonstrable experience of conducting immunological or infectious disease research in a research laboratory is a must. It is essential to have sufficient specialist knowledge to develop, optimise and apply experimental research methodologies. You must also hold the ability to independently plan and organise experimental work and contribute effectively to the delivery of a research project. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 24 months and is funded by UKRI - MRC. Only applications received before 12 midday on 11 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 188876 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 188876 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188917": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Epitranscriptomics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Epitranscriptomics Target Discovery Institute, NDM Research Building, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7FZ We are seeking to appoint two Postdoctoral Research Scientists in Epitranscriptomics to join Dr Chunxiao Song’s research group at the Target Discovery Institute, Nuffield Department of Medicine. You will contribute to research investigating the role of pseudouridine and pseudouridine synthases (PUS) in cancer, alongside exploring the clinical applications of pseudouridine in circulating cell-free RNA for cancer diagnostics. The group develops cutting-edge technologies for high-resolution mapping of DNA and RNA modifications, translating discoveries in epigenetics and epitranscriptomics into diagnostic and therapeutic opportunities. You will be responsible for managing and leading research projects, developing and adapting experimental techniques, testing hypotheses and analysing scientific data. The role includes contributing to research development and funding proposals, preparing scientific reports and journal articles, presenting research findings and providing specialist advice on experimental protocols and techniques. You will also collaborate with researchers and partner institutions, contributing to the development of innovative approaches in RNA biology and epitranscriptomics. It is essential that you hold, or are close to completing, a relevant PhD/DPhil in Molecular Biology, Biochemistry, Cell Biology, Chemistry or a related scientific discipline, together with relevant research experience. You should have strong practical skills in molecular biology, biochemistry or cell biology, specialist knowledge of RNA biology or epitranscriptomics, and the ability to independently manage and lead research projects. Excellent communication skills and experience contributing to scientific publications are also required. Experience with next-generation sequencing, bioinformatic data analysis, liquid biopsy or cell-free RNA sequencing, supervising researchers or students, and first-author publications in a relevant field would be advantageous. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 24 months and is funded by Cancer Research UK. Only applications received before 12 midday on 11 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 188917 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Aruna Dhinakaran Vacancy ID : 188917 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: Salary in range £39,424 - £47,779 per annum (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189272": {
      "title": "Translational Research Scientist: Experimental Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Translational Research Scientist: Experimental Medicine 189272 Translational Research Scientist: Experimental Medicine Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) Grade 8: Salary in the range of £49,119-£58,265 per annum. This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year. This is a full time, fixed term position for 24 months. The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Nuffield Department of Orthopaedics, Rheumatology and Musculoskeletal Sciences (NDORMS) is part of the Medical Sciences Division and is the largest European academic department in its field, running a globally competitive programme of research and teaching. The Kennedy Institute is a biomedical research centre uniquely bringing together discovery science and early-stage clinical research, to develop transformative new therapies for chronic inflammatory and musculoskeletal conditions. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs About the Role We are offering an exciting and unique opportunity for an experienced researcher to work as a Translational Research Scientist at the interface of academia and industry, joining a large multidisciplinary team working on Experimental Medicine. The team are carrying out histopathological and omics analyses of samples from clinical studies sponsored by industry partners, which aim to understand the molecular basis disease pathways and potential new treatment targets. The Translational Research Scientist will contribute to the set-up, delivery and continuity of the research projects and programmes by providing advanced technical and scientific expertise in support. Unlike a traditional Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) role, this position is not primarily aligned with a standard academic career progression pathway. Instead, the postholder will focus on collaborative research, the application of specialist knowledge, and the development, optimisation, and maintenance of methodologies, facilities, or shared resources. The role emphasises long-term contribution, technical leadership, and support for team-based research objectives, rather than the pursuit of independent academic advancement or principal investigator status. About You You must hold a PhD/DPhil in immunology or related area, together with post-qualification research experience. You will have experience of independently managing a research project and evidence of having collaborated with both wet and dry lab researchers to complete a project. Demonstrated experience in standard laboratory-based immunology techniques, such as human tissue handling and culture, PCR, flow cytometry, is essential. Proficiency in conducting 10X single-cell RNAseq and spatial transcriptomics is desirable. Application Process The University of Oxford is committed to equality and valuing diversity. All applicants will be judged on merit, according to the selection criteria. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and must include a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the selection criteria, using examples of your skills and experience. Interviews will take place during the week commencing 23 November and will be face to face/online. Please quote reference 189272 in all correspondence. Contact Person : NDORMS Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189272 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Salary in the range of £49,119-£58,265 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188948": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Clinical Research Fellow Centre for Human Genetics, Building for Genomic Medicine, Roosevelt Drive, Oxford, OX3 7BN The Nuffield Department of Medicine is recruiting a Clinical Research Fellow in Gastroenterology to join a multidisciplinary team investigating the molecular basis of pathogenic autoantibodies in treatment-refractory inflammatory bowel disease and identifying potential new treatment targets. Based at the Centre for Human Genetics in Oxford, you will work under the supervision of Professor Holm Uhlig and Professor Simon Travis, with close collaboration across clinical, laboratory and computational research teams. In this role, you will help deliver interventional gastroenterology studies including identifying and recruiting patients, providing clinical care in accordance with trial protocols, and collecting and processing blood and tissue samples for downstream single-cell and bulk RNA analysis, transcriptomics and proteomics. You will work closely with wet-lab scientists, bioinformaticians, clinical colleagues and the industry partners to generate insights as the trial progresses. You will also contribute to data analysis, research papers, conference abstracts and grant applications, while supporting the accurate documentation and regulatory standards required for clinical research. It is essential that you hold a Bachelor’s degree in Medicine and be a UK GMC-registered medical practitioner with experience working full time as a doctor within the NHS. You will need excellent communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, together with the ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. A methodical approach to data collection, an understanding of data protection and relevant professional guidelines, and the ability to manage multiple projects are also essential. Experience in gastroenterology, clinical trials or research, MRCP or equivalent, and an understanding of research methodology would be advantageous. The post also offers significant scope for research and professional development. You will be encouraged to develop an area of your own interest and may have the opportunity to work towards a higher degree, such as a DPhil or MSc, with potential funding available through the industry collaboration. Training and research opportunities within the Clinical Trials Unit and wider translational research programme will further support your development during the three-year appointment. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 3 years and is funded by Johnson and Johnson. Only applications received before 12 midday on 12 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 188948 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 188948 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 12-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : CLINICAL RESEARCHER (NEW SCALE) (E63) Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade E63: £40,190 - £76,582 per annum dependent on qualifications and experience, in line with current pay protection and translational arrangements in the NHS."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189191": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Immunologist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Postdoctoral Immunologist Location IMS-Tetsuya Nakamura Building, Old Road Campus, Roosevelt Dr, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7TY We are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Immunologist to join Professor Dame Sarah Gilbert’s Outbreak Pathogens Vaccine Group at the University of Oxford. You will investigate T- and B-cell immune responses to adenoviral-vectored and RNA vaccine antigens, helping to advance novel vaccines against emerging and outbreak pathogens towards clinical development. Working within the preclinical team, you will evaluate vaccine immunogenicity with in vivo models, analyse and present experimental data, adapt laboratory protocols and contribute to the development of new approaches. You will also collaborate with internal and external researchers, contribute to scientific discussions, reports and publications, and support the training and supervision of junior team members. Reporting to Senior Preclinical Immunologist Barbara Dema Jimenez, you will be a highly organised and motivated researcher with experience in immunological assessment and a strong commitment to high-quality, safe laboratory practice. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a highly collaborative and translational vaccine research programme with a strong track record in developing vaccines against globally important pathogens. It is essential that you have a PhD/DPhil (or close to completion) in a relevant biological sciences subject. You must hold a UK Home Office Personal Licence. Practical experience with cellular immunology assays is a must. It is essential that you are self-motivated, technically competent and capable of working independently and methodologically in a laboratory. The ability to interpret scientific data, analyse and research complex issues and write factual reports is also essential. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 November 2028 and is funded by The Calleva Foundation. Only applications received before 12 midday on 12 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189191 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 189191 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 12-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 with a discretionary range to £53,483 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189133": {
      "title": "Bioinformatician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Bioinformatician Department of Paediatrics, Sanders Research Group, Institute of Developmental & Regenerative Medicine IDRM, Old Road Campus, Oxford About the role We have an exciting opportunity to join the Sanders Group and the Medical Research Council Centre of Research Excellence (MRC CoRE) in Therapeutic Genomics. The programme aims to advance the understanding, diagnosis, and treatment of rare genetic neurodevelopmental disorders including autism, through genomic research and the development of genome-targeted therapies, such as CRISPR-based editing and antisense oligonucleotides (ASOs). This three-year position aims to revolutionise how therapies are identified, developed, and brought to the clinic for rare genetic disorders. The Senior Bioinformatician, based within the Department of Paediatrics, will provide critical technical and analytical support for a range of different projects across the Sanders Group and MRC CoRE. The postholder will be responsible for the analysis, management, and interpretation of large-scale genomic datasets, ensuring the development and maintenance of robust, reproducible bioinformatics pipelines. Working across both the Sanders Group and the MRC CoRE, you will collaborate with researchers to define analytical strategies, provide expert statistical input, and identify new projects that contribute to the overall goals of the research programme. The role is based within the Department of Paediatrics, part of the University of Oxford’s Medical Sciences Division, and is located at the Institute of Developmental & Regenerative Medicine (IDRM). The postholder will work closely with Professor Stephan Sanders and a multidisciplinary team including data managers, developers, and international colleagues at the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF). You will be part of a team of outstanding international experts and industry partners committed to priming the clinical landscape for the next generation of genetic medicines. This position is offered full-time on a fixed-term contract for an initial period of two years. About the department The Department of Paediatrics is committed to equality and valuing diversity. The Department of Paediatrics has been honoured with the Athena Swan Gold award, a national gender equality charter, recognising the Department's innovative policies and practices. We are committed to the professional development of our staff by providing up to ten paid days annually for skill enhancement and allowing applications for additional training funding. By joining us, you will have the opportunity to contribute to a forward-thinking department. We welcome new staff with ideas who are willing to shape the future of the department that thinks about its staff and wellbeing. In addition to the University of Oxford wellbeing resources , the Department of Paediatrics sponsors weekly exercise classes such as yoga and bootcamp sessions as well as a choir. For staff on work visas, we also offer financial assistance towards visa renewal fees. These activities are designed to promote physical and mental wellbeing among staff members. Please visit our website to find out more about the Department. About you You will be educated to degree level in Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Genomics, Computer Science, or a related discipline, or possess equivalent relevant experience. You will have substantial experience analysing and interpreting large-scale genomic datasets, including high-throughput sequencing data, using established bioinformatics tools and workflows. You will demonstrate expertise in developing and maintaining reproducible bioinformatics pipelines, with proficiency in scripting languages such as Python and/or R, workflow management systems (e.g. Snakemake, Nextflow or Cromwell), and collaborative software development practices using version control. Experience working with large genomic datasets in cloud or high-performance computing environments, together with a strong understanding of data quality, integrity, and ethical handling of human genomic data, is essential. You will have excellent analytical, problem-solving, organisational, and communication skills, with the ability to manage multiple concurrent projects, collaborate effectively across multidisciplinary research teams, contribute to scientific publications and grant applications, and deliver high-quality outputs to demanding deadlines. Application Process You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your application will not be processed if you do not include both documents. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. To discuss the post in more detail, please contact Corinne Betts, the Programme Manager for Prof. Sanders, using the contact details below. Only online applications received before 12.00 midday on Friday 13th November will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. The Department of Paediatrics offers an Interview Expense Reimbursement Policy in its efforts towards equality, diversity and inclusion. Should you be invited to interview, we will share further information about the policy at that stage. Contact Person : Corinne Betts Vacancy ID : 189133 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 8 Contact Email : corinne.betts@paediatrics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 8: £49,119- £58,265 per annum (with a discretionary range to £63,489 per annum)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189273": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department of Economics 189273 - Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department of Economics, Manor Road Building, Manor Road, Oxford Salary: Research Grade 7, £39,424–£47,779 per annum Hours: Full-time (37.5 hours per week) Contract: Fixed term, from summer 2027 (with some flexibility on start date), for 3 years The Department of Economics is seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Fellow. The successful candidate will join the ERC-funded research project GENTALENT: Gender Specialisation in the Future of Work, led by Professor Barbara Petrongolo at the University of Oxford. The project investigates how workplace organisation, family dynamics, and social norms shape gender specialization, and how these patterns affect careers, parental mental health, and child development. The research will also explore the dynamics of gender specialization in a historical perspective. The postholder will play a central role in developing and implementing the project's research agenda, working closely with the Principal Investigator and other collaborators. The successful candidate will have opportunities to develop independent research ideas, co-author papers intended for leading international journals and contribute to the supervision of junior researchers. Professor Barbara Petrongolo is a member of the Applied Microeconomics Group within the Department of Economics ( https://www.economics.ox.ac.uk/research-group/applied-microeconomics ) and the postholder will join that group. The Department provides a vibrant environment for postdoctoral researchers and excellent training and development opportunities: over 20 researchers currently hold postdoctoral positions within the Department. Many previous fellows have moved on to permanent faculty positions at leading universities in Europe and North America. The closing date for applications is midnight (UTC) on 27 November 2026. Interviews for shortlisted candidates will be held online shortly after the application closing date. Applications must be submitted online via EconJobMarket (EJM): EJM - Econ Job Market. The University of Oxford is committed to fostering an inclusive working environment and welcomes applications from suitably qualified candidates regardless of background. Selection for appointment will be based solely on merit, assessed against the criteria for the role and the ability to undertake its duties. The University is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion, alongside its commitment to academic freedom and freedom of speech, as set out in its Equality Policy and Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Strategic Plan. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189273 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 27-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : personnel@economics.ox.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189341": {
      "title": "Part-time Research Assistant;   Biological Ageing in Psychosis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Part-time Research Assistant; Biological Ageing in Psychosis Department of Psychiatry, University of Oxford, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. Reporting to the Dr Chloe Yap, the post holder is a member of the Immuno-Metabolism & Severe Mental Illness research group with responsibility for the provision of research support for a project investigating the role of accelerated biological aging in psychosis. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working About the Role The post is a part-time, 7.5 hours per week (20.13%FTE), funded to 31 January 2028, and based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. You will analyse large multi-modal datasets relevant to psychosis, including proteomic, metabolomic, neuroimaging and genomic data, and generate and apply biological ageing clocks across a range of omics modalities. You will select, adapt and develop appropriate analytical methodologies, as well as review and synthesise literature on biological ageing and undertake systematic literature reviews. You will contribute to research publications and conference presentations and support wider project planning and day-to-day project administration. About You You will hold a first degree, for example in biomedical sciences, psychology or psychiatry, and/or have relevant experience, and be working towards a doctorate in psychosis. You will have experience using command line tools for reproducible data analysis, alongside skills in statistics, machine learning, data cleaning and analysis pipelines. You will also have experience working with large multi-modal datasets relevant to psychiatry and sufficient specialist knowledge of biological ageing to work within established research programmes. You will be able to communicate research findings clearly, including writing for publication, presenting data at conferences and representing the research group at meetings. Experience of contributing to research publications and working collaboratively within a research team, including contributing ideas for new research projects, would be desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity Our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 28 October 2026. Contact Person : HR Administrator Vacancy ID : 189341 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35681 - £40514 pro rata part time"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189312": {
      "title": "Research Assistant – VLP Vaccine Development",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Research Assistant – VLP Vaccine Development Jenner Institute, Old Road Campus Research Building, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a Research Assistant in VLP Vaccine Development to work with Associate Professor Sandy Douglas’ group. This is a fantastic opportunity to work with a team of scientists developing vaccines and vaccine platforms, as well as advancing knowledge on a range of infections. The scope of the Douglas group’s work covers pre-clinical development, vaccine manufacturing and clinical trial, and adenovirus genetic engineering. You will be mainly focused on manufacturing: the researcher will receive training and contribute to multiple projects on viral vaccine manufacturing and analytical assay development, particularly within a project on VLP vaccine platforms and EBV. You will have responsibility for producing high quality data using a range of modern laboratory techniques, such as cell culture, recombinant protein production and purification, flow cytometry and analytical size exclusion chromatography. You will develop your skills in experimental design, planning and analysis, while making a meaningful contribution to global health through developing new vaccines. You will be based at the Jenner Institute, Old Road Campus Research Building, Headington. You will have the opportunity to develop a diverse range of knowledge and skills. You will develop your career with mentoring from a senior team comprising Associate Prof. Sandy Douglas (the Group Leader), Dr Catherine Cherry (day-to-day supervisor), and members of the research team. The post would be particularly suited if you are enthusiastic about pursuing graduate studies in medical science, as the exposure to a range of projects and techniques will provide rich development opportunities. It is essential that you hold a MSc degree in biological science, biochemistry or a related discipline. Practical experience of biology, medical science or similar laboratory work is a must. You must have knowledge of vaccinology, infectious disease and molecular biology. It is essential that you have experience of mammalian cell culture, recombinant protein production, ELISAs, size exclusion chromatography and viral neutralisation assays. Ability to work within established research programmes, with the potential to contribute ideas for new and improved experiments is a must. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 9 months and is funded by SpyBiotech. Only applications received before 12 midday on Wednesday, 28 October 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189312 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 189312 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 28-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 6: Salary in range £35,681 - £41,636 per annum (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189438": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in MRI-Microstructure Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in MRI-Microstructure Modelling Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN). FMRIB Centre, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford, OX3 9DU The post will be based within the Oxford Centre for Integrative Neuroimaging (OxCIN), reporting to Profs Saad Jbabdi and Karla Miller. Our group develops, validates and applies novel MRI techniques for basic and clinical neuroscience. This project involves the development of machine learning methods for mapping the relationships between multimodal MRI (e.g. diffusion MRI, relaxometry) and microscopy (including phase-sensitive OCT, light sheet, spatial transcriptomics, and immunohistochemistry) in the same tissue samples. The postholder will use datasets from a Wellcome-funded Biomedical imaging project (PI Jason Lerch) and a large NIH-funded project based in the US (PI Sarah Heilbroner) to develop methods for jointly analysing MRI and microscopy data. The post holder will be expected to take an independent and proactive role in managing the research process, maintaining the collaboration with partners, and generating new research opportunities. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the role, responsibilities, and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. For any queries regarding visa sponsorship, HR and the University's Staff Immigration Team will provide advice and assistance when the chosen candidate for the role has been identified. This post is full time, fixed term until 31st August 2028 in the first instance. Only applications received before midday 12:00 on Friday 30th October 2026 will be considered. Contact Person : NDCN Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189438 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £49,119 - £58,265 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189450": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Research Technician Oxford Centre for Diabetes Endocrinology and Metabolism (OCDEM), Churchill Hospital, Old Road, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LE Grade 5: £32,108 - £37,338 per annum, pro rata We are seeking an enthusiastic Part-time Research Assistant to join a vibrant research team led by David Ray at the Oxford Centre for Diabetes Endocrinology and Metabolism (OCDEM) on studies related to circadian and sleep population genetics. The work will analyse Kupffer cell circadian function. You will use in vitro models and in vivo studies with genetically modified mice. You will analyse gene expression at bulk and single-cell level. You will analyse histological sections and fixed, purified cells using conventional, immune, and fluorophore-tagged proteins. You will run FACS analyses on immune cell preparations from the liver. Prof Ray will provide day-to-day support. The successful applicant will join an established multidisciplinary research team, undertaking and directing the research day to day whilst also supervising both technical staff and graduate research students attached to the project. The successful applicant must hold, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in human population genetics and Mendelian randomisation. They must have peer-reviewed publications. They will possess sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to work within established research programmes. The position is part-time for 20 hours per week and fixed-term for 12 months. The position will be based at OCDEM, Churchill Hospital, Headington, Oxford. For informal enquiries, please contact Ruby Ramsamy at ocdem.personnel@ndm.ox.ac.uk . Please quote reference 189450 on all correspondence. As part of your formal online application, you will be required to upload a CV and supporting statement. Only applications received before midday on Friday, 30 October 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled for the w/c 16 November 2026 . Contact Person : Ruby Ramsamy Vacancy ID : 189450 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 5 Contact Email : ocdem.personnel@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £32,108 - £37,338"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189476": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Neuroscientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Neuroscientist Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN) - John Radcliffe Hospital, Headington, Oxford, OX3 9DU This post is an exciting opportunity provided by funding from the Adelson Medical Research Foundation. The aim of this post is to develop human cellular models of peripheral neuropathy to test novel therapeutic avenues. These will include rare inherited neuropathies and diabetic peripheral neuropathy using sensory neurons differentiated from induced pluripotent stem cells and to model them using advanced culture techniques (such as microfluidic chambers) and cellular stressors (which may be metabolic, drug induced including activators of SARM a key component of the Wallerian degeneration pathway or traumatic neuropathies). We will investigate the downstream molecular pathways mediating cell death and axonal destruction using both targeted approaches but also genome-wide CRISPR screen. The post will therefore suit an applicant who has an interest in the response to neural injury, the Wallerian degeneration pathways, iPSC models and some expertise in molecular biology. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the responsibilities and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. For any queries regarding visa sponsorship, HR and the University's Staff Immigration Team will provide advice and assistance when the chosen candidate for the role has been identified. The post is full time for a fixed term until 30th of September 2028. Only applications received before 12.00 midday on Monday the 2nd of November 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN HR Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189476 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum A less experienced candidate may be appointed at Grade 6 (£35,681– £41,636 per annum), with a commensurate adjustment in either the essential criteria, responsibilities or duties."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189475": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Laboratory-based Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Laboratory-based Sciences Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Old Road Campus We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Laboratory-based Sciences to join the Institute of Biomedical Engineering at the Department of Engineering Science (Headington). The post is funded by the UKRI-EPSRC Programme Grant and is fixed-term to 30 September 2027. The post holder will work under the EPSRC-funded Beyond Antibiotics research programme and report to project Co-Investigator Dr. Dario Carugo. They will work collaboratively with other researchers in the programme, to specifically evaluate the effect of clinically viable physical stimuli (primarily biomedical ultrasound, with/without ultrasound-responsive agents) on the antimicrobial/antibiofilm efficacy of antibiotics and on the host tissue physiology. To achieve this, they will integrate physical instrumentation (such as ultrasound probes) with complex in-vitro biological models already developed within the research programme, and they’ll perform bio-analytical assays on the treated biological models. The programme has a specific focus on the development of human-relevant models of urinary tract and wound-associated infections, which can be employed to evaluate or develop more efficacious and safer alternatives to conventional antibiotic treatments. You will be a member of a research group with responsibility for carrying out research for a discrete area of a large project. You will also provide guidance to less experienced members of the research group, including postdocs, research assistants, technicians, and/or doctoral and project students. You should hold a PhD/DPhil in the areas of bioengineering, biochemistry, biophysics, biotechnology, and/or other related discipline, and be experienced in the development and/or use of biological models for therapy evaluation purposes, such as in-vitro or ex-vivo tissue models. The post also requires experience in the use of ultrasound generation/detection probes, and the associated electronic instrumentation and signal/data analysis techniques. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr Dario Carugo dario.carugo@ndorms.ox.ac.uk. For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on 02 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of three referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Dr Dario Carugo Vacancy ID : 189475 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : dario.carugo@ndorms.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum, including Oxford University Weighting (OUW)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189227": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Postdoctoral Scientist Centre for Medicines Discovery, NDM Research Building, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7FZ We are seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher to join Professor Alex Bullock’s Growth Factor Signalling and Ubiquitination research group. You will investigate proteins genetically linked to childhood brain tumours, including medulloblastoma and glioma, contributing to research aimed at improving our understanding of the molecular mechanisms underlying these cancers. You will develop and perform cellular assays to characterise disease-associated genetic mutants and small molecule modulators, with a particular focus on protein degradation and kinase signalling. You will analyse and interpret experimental data, contribute new research ideas, prepare publications and presentations, collaborate with other researchers, and provide guidance to research assistants and PhD students. You will be responsible for managing your own academic research and administrative duties involving small scale project management, to co-ordinate multiple aspects of work to meet deadlines, as well as maintaining accurate records in an electronic lab notebook. You will be adapting, developing and performing cell assay protocols in protein degradation and kinase signalling to characterise genetic mutants linked to childhood brain tumours. It is essential that you hold a relevant PhD/DPhil in Molecular Biology or closely related subject. You must have proven technical experience in cellular assays for protein degradation and kinase signalling, including work with cancer mutants and small molecule chemical probes. A good scientific publication record is a must. It is essential that you have competent computer skills including knowledge of graphpad and electronic lab notebooks. It is also essential that you have excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered part time on a fixed term contract until 31 March 2027 and is funded by both the Department and Cancer Research UK. Only applications received before 12 midday on Monday, 2 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189227 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Luke Gundry Vacancy ID : 189227 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: Salary in range £39,424 - £46,476 per annum (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188912": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Research Assistant Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a Research Assistant to join the Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology within the Nuffield Department of Medicine. Working with Dr Ignacio Melero, you will contribute to research investigating anti-tumour immune responses and the development of new strategies to improve cancer immunotherapy. The research focuses on antigen presentation, interactions between dendritic cells and T cells, and inflammatory signalling within the tumour microenvironment, using preclinical models and patient-derived samples to identify approaches that could enhance responses to immune checkpoint blockade and other immunotherapies. You will be responsible for providing high-quality technical and research support, carrying out laboratory experiments including cell culture, flow cytometry, molecular biology and biological sample processing, as well as in vivo experiments using mouse models. The role involves adapting and optimising experimental protocols, troubleshooting technical problems, collecting and analysing data, contributing to experimental design and research planning, and communicating findings with collaborators. Responsibilities also include providing practical training to researchers and students and supporting the day-to-day running of the laboratory, including equipment maintenance, stock control and ordering. It is essential that you hold a degree in a relevant biological science subject, together with experience working in a biological research laboratory. You should have practical experience of cell culture and flow cytometry, good organisational and communication skills, and the ability to follow and adapt experimental protocols and troubleshoot technical problems. Experience of, or a willingness to undertake, in vivo work using mouse models is required, along with the ability to obtain and maintain a Personal Licence and work within a Biological Services Unit. Experience in immunology, cancer biology or tumour immunology, preclinical mouse models, multicolour flow cytometry or FACS, and working with primary immune cells, tumour tissue or patient-derived samples would be advantageous. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 24 months and is funded by Cancer Research UK. Only applications received before 12 midday on 2 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 188912 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Aruna Dhinakaran Vacancy ID : 188912 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 6: £35,681 - £41,636 This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189354": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Cardiovascular Nanomedicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Research Assistant in Cardiovascular Nanomedicine RDM Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford, OX3 9DU Research Assistant in Cardiovascular Nanomedicine An exciting opportunity has arisen for a highly motivated and enthusiastic Research Assistant in Cardiovascular Nanomedicine to join the internationally recognised research teams within the Division of Cardiovascular Medicine (CVM), University of Oxford. CVM is renowned for its excellence in translational and clinical cardiovascular research, delivering innovative studies that improve patient care and outcomes worldwide. About the role As Research Assistant, you play a key role in supporting Professor Keith Channon and Dr Tanveer Tabish to investigate nitric oxide-releasing nanomedicines for cardiovascular therapeutics. The work will focus on cell culture, functional and molecular assays, microscopy and ex-vivo vascular studies. Responsibilities will include measuring nitric oxide-dependent signalling using cGMP assays and downstream protein readouts such as VASP phosphorylation, in addition to performing qPCR, ELISA, Western blotting, and RNA and protein extraction to investigate molecular responses and inflammatory activity. The postholder will work closely with laboratory researchers within a multidisciplinary environment. About you The successful Research Assistant must hold a relevant degree in biomedical sciences, cell biology, pharmacology, physiology or a related discipline, alongside relevant laboratory research experience. Experience in handling biological samples or tissues, with an understanding of sample traceability and the procedures required for safe and responsible laboratory work is also essential. Experience in primary endothelial cell culture, including HUVECs or other vascular cell models will be an advantage. This is a full-time appointment on a fixed-term contract until 3rd October 2027. The Research Assistant will be based at the Centre for Human Genetics, OX3 7BN. How to apply Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via the University website; you will be required to upload your CV and a supporting statement that explains how you meet the selection criteria for the post. CVs alone will not be considered. Only applications received before midday on 2nd November 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled to take place shortly thereafter. For informal enquiries about this position, please contact – Dr Tanveer Tabish – tanveer.tabish@cardiov.ox.ac.uk Georgia Broom – cvm_personnel@cardiov.ox.ac.uk We are committed to ensuring that our recruitment processes are inclusive and accessible. If you require the job description or any other materials in an alternative format, or if you would like to request any adjustments to support you through the application or interview process, please contact the recruitment team at cvm_personnel@cardiov.ox.ac.uk. The University of Oxford offers a comprehensive range of competitive benefits available to all members of staff designed to support both professional and personal wellbeing. Benefits include: • Generous family leave schemes , including maternity, paternity, adoption, and carers’ leave • 38 days of annual leave , plus additional leave for continuous service • Membership of a highly competitive pension scheme • Oxford Living Wage assurance • Annual cost-of-living reviews and automatic salary increments up to the top of the grade • Extensive training and professional development opportunities • Travel support schemes and a wide range of transport discounts , including bus, train, and bicycle travel Committed to equality and valuing diversity We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or member of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Contact Person : Dr Tanveer Tabish Vacancy ID : 189354 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : tanveer.tabish@cardiov.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189241": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Molecular Virology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Research Assistant in Molecular Virology CAMS Oxford Institute, Building for Molecular Physiology, Roosevelt Drive, Old Road Campus, Oxford, OX3 7BN We are seeking to appoint a Research Assistant in Molecular Virology to join the Molecular Virology group at the CAMS Oxford Institute, Nuffield Department of Medicine. Working under the supervision of Dr Peter Wing, you will contribute to research investigating the complex interactions between viruses and host cells, with a particular focus on the relationship between cellular oxygen sensing and viral replication. The project aims to identify new host-directed pathways to combat respiratory viral infections. You will be responsible for growing and maintaining viral cultures in containment level 2 and 3 laboratories, performing molecular biology, immunological and biochemical techniques, and conducting siRNA screens in cellular infection models. Your role will include maintaining human and mammalian cell lines, generating knockout and knockdown cell lines using CRISPR/siRNA, performing live-cell imaging, and collecting and analysing scientific data. You will also contribute to experimental design, scientific publications, collaborative research projects and the day-to-day management of the laboratory. It is essential that you hold a degree in biology, biochemistry, chemistry, biotechnology or a related discipline, with specialist knowledge of molecular virology and experience in viral propagation, cell culture and gene knockout/knockdown techniques. You should have experience using hypoxia workstations, molecular cloning, bacterial culture, confocal microscopy, immunofluorescence and RNA-FISH, alongside excellent organisational, analytical and communication skills. The ability to work independently, adapt experimental protocols and maintain accurate research records is essential. Experience working in containment level 2/3 laboratories, handling primary tissue, and using data analysis software such as R, Python, FIJI or Prism would be advantageous. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract for 12 months, with the possibility of extension and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on 2 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189241 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Aruna Dhinakaran Vacancy ID : 189241 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 6: Salary in range £35,681 - £37,388 per annum (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189379": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Development of the Lithium-Air Battery",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Development of the Lithium-Air Battery Department of Materials About the role As part of a collaborative project, the PDRA will carry out world-leading research on the development of the lithium-air battery. The Li-air battery represents a step-change in battery technology, with a theoretical energy up to ten times that of current lithium-ion cells. This role is part of an exciting project that brings together scientists and engineers to tackle the key challenges preventing commercialisation. The position will involve lab work in our recently furbished state-of-the-art laboratories, collaborating with other groups, presenting to key stakeholders and preparing manuscripts for publication. About you We are looking to recruit an outstanding PDRA and we encourage applications from candidates with a background in chemistry or materials science. We are keen to hear from people with expertise in inorganic/organic chemistry (including synthesis), electrochemistry and physical chemistry (including spectroscopy, modelling and reaction dynamics). You will be a well-organised and self-motivated scientist who enjoys cutting edge research in a fast-paced and intellectually stimulating environment. The role requires a relevant PhD(DPhil), excellent communication skills and a desire to take this technology to the next level. How to Apply All applications must be made online using the Oxford University E-Recruitment system, no later than 12 noon on 3 November 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a Supporting Statement as part of your application . Please do not attach any manuscripts, papers, transcripts, mark sheets or certificates as these will not be considered as part of your application. Only applications received online by 12.00 midday (GMT) on 3 November 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled to take place at the Department of Materials and you must be available either by Teams or in person. The interview date is to be confirmed. Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189379 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 03-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@materials.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189454": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist – Biomaterials and Biofabrication",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist – Biomaterials and Biofabrication Department of Physiology, Anatomy and Genetics, Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin Building, Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QU Contract and job type: Full-time, fixed term for 18 months About us: The Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery (Kavli INsD), established in March 2021, brings together over 30 faculty and 450 researchers across disciplines to tackle global health challenges. By fostering interdisciplinary collaboration and providing cutting-edge facilities, it encourages innovation and shared discovery, and benefits from the close proximity of the scientific departments as well as advanced imaging and characterisation facilities and state-of-the-art-instrumentation. At the Department of Physiology Anatomy & Genetics (DPAG) we undertake discovery science where we reassemble physiological processes at the molecular, cellular, tissue and systems level of organisation. In so doing we provide a bridge to translational medicine, and interface between physical and life sciences. We are committed not only to innovative research and the highest standard of teaching, but also to creating an inclusive and supportive working environment. Overview of the role: We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Scientist to join Professor Dame Molly Stevens’s lab. The Stevens Group operates at the intersection of multiple disciplines, enabling highly collaborative and cross-field research. The lab is based at the Kavli Institute of Nanoscience Discovery and is strategically affiliated with the Department of Physiology, Anatomy and Genetics, as well as the Institute of Biomedical Engineering within the Department of Engineering Science. We are looking for a talented biomaterial scientist with broad expertise on the design and fabrication of advanced biomaterial for therapeutics and regenerative medicine applications. The role is highly multidisciplinary involving collaboration with researchers working within the fields of materials science, bioengineering, chemistry and biology. Successful candidates will engage with researchers both within the lab and across partner institutions, requiring strong organizational abilities and project management expertise to navigate complex, multi-team projects. This post is available immediately for a fixed-term of 18 months, with the possibility of part-time appointments being considered. Key responsibilities: Develop and optimise micro-architectured constructs using state-of-the-art bioprinting techniques. Investigate how cells interact with biomaterials, including mechanobiological responses to physical and biochemical cues, and translate insights into improved material designs. Plan and perform cell culture experiments to evaluate the bio-material interfaces. Support the operation and management of shared biofabrication and synthesis facilities, providing training, technical expertise and guidance to other laboratory users as required. Selection criteria: Hold, or be near to completion of, a PhD/DPhil in materials science, bioengineering or relevant field. Experience in the development of bioinks for fabrication of micro/nano-structured biomaterials. Experience in light-based 3D-bioprinting (e.g. SLA/DLP/nanoscribe). Experience in a wide range of material and biointerface characterisation techniques (including microscopy, spectroscopy, rheological and mechanical properties, cell assays, etc.) Strong background in cell culture and in vitro tissue development. Previous experience of contributing to publications/presentations, with a proven track record of first author publications in peer reviewed journals. Please refer to the Job Description for a full list of responsibilities and selection criteria. What we offer: Your wellbeing at work matters, so we offer a range of family friendly and financial benefits including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle and electric car loan schemes Employee Assistance Programme Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans While this is a full-time role, we welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. How to apply: Please provide a supporting statement outlining how you meet the selection criteria along with your CV, and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is 12 noon on Tuesday 03rd November 2026. Interviews are likely to take place during the week commencing 16th November, and will be held on Microsoft Teams. Applications are particularly welcome from women, black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. Follow us: Stay connected with us on LinkedIn , Bluesky and Instagram to learn more about our work and culture. Informal enquiries about the role may be directed to the DPAG HR Team: hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk . DPAG’s Statement of Inclusion: We, as a Department and Community, will be considerate and welcoming of all people, regardless of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, and socio-economic background. We acknowledge societal inequalities and how these impact us, and those around us, personally and professionally. Our policies, practices and Respectful Behaviours Framework underpin this commitment. DPAG and Sustainability: We have signed up to The Laboratory Efficiency Assessment Framework (LEAF) and Green Impact, actively implementing and encouraging eco-friendly practices that reduce waste, promote energy efficiency, and promote bio-diversity. See the job description for more detail. Contact Person : DPAG HR Team Vacancy ID : 189454 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 03-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@dpag.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39,424 - 47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189437": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Sleep, Memory and Neuroimaging",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Sleep, Memory and Neuroimaging Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 7: £39,424-£47,779 per annum The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Experimental Psychology is located on South Parks Road at the new, purpose-built Life and Mind Building, a multidisciplinary research and teaching facility that opened in the summer of 2025. We are seeking a motivated and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Sleep and Memory research group, led by Professor Bernhard Staresina. The post is funded by the Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council and will examine how memories spanning recent to remote time periods change in quality and neural representation. The successful candidate will coordinate and deliver a longitudinal, multi-session human study combining behavioural memory testing, overnight sleep EEG and fMRI. The study will include repeated encoding sessions, overnight sleep recordings, an fMRI retrieval session and detailed post-scan memory assessment over approximately one month. You will develop and refine experimental methods, coordinate participant recruitment and testing, process and analyse behavioural, sleep EEG and MRI/fMRI data, and contribute to publications, conference presentations and research funding proposals. You will also develop reproducible study materials, task code, data-management procedures and analysis workflows; work collaboratively with colleagues and partner institutions; provide advice on scientific protocols and experimental techniques; and contribute to the wider intellectual activities of the research group. The successful candidate will hold a relevant PhD/DPhil in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, neuroscience or a closely related discipline, together with relevant post-qualification research experience. You will have specialist knowledge and hands-on experience of sleep and memory consolidation, including human sleep research and overnight sleep studies, and experience of designing or implementing behavioural and/or neuroimaging experiments. Experience of EEG data acquisition and analysis, MRI data acquisition and strong quantitative data-analysis skills using software such as R, Python and/or MATLAB are also essential. You will have a record of contributing to peer-reviewed publications and presenting research findings, alongside excellent organisational and communication skills and the ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary team. The post is full time and fixed term for 1 year. The research involves overnight sleep recordings; any flexible working arrangements will be agreed in accordance with the needs of the study. What We Offer: As an employer, we genuinely care about your happiness and wellbeing at work and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement detailing how you meet the selection criteria, your CV including a list of publications (if any), and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 4 November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189437 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189451": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Research Assistant Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 6: £35,681 - £41,636 per annum The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Experimental Psychology is located on South Parks Road at the new, purpose-built Life and Mind Building, a multidisciplinary research and teaching facility that opened in the summer of 2025. We are seeking a motivated and experienced part-time Research Assistant to support a Wellcome-funded project focused on improving help-seeking for obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) among UK-based ethnic minority adolescents. The project is led by Dr Nina Higson-Sweeney within the Oxford Centre for Emerging Minds Research. The successful applicant will support the day-to-day delivery of the project. This will include assisting with recruitment, data collection and qualitative analysis; contributing to screening, data extraction, quality appraisal and synthesis for a mixed-methods systematic review; supporting patient and public involvement activities; and contributing to research publications, presentations and other research outputs. You will be based within Dr Polly Waite’s PANDA research group (Psychological interventions for ANxiety and related Disorders in Adolescence) and will contribute to the wider intellectual activities of the group and Centre, including research meetings, lab meetings, journal clubs and workshops. The successful candidate will hold an undergraduate degree in Psychology or a related discipline, together with relevant experience of research with ethnic minority young people in a UK context. You will have experience of completing qualitative research with UK-based young people, including semi-structured interviews, focus groups and reflexive thematic analysis, and experience of mixed-methods systematic reviews. You will also have experience of co-production or participatory research with young people and relevant stakeholders. Excellent communication, organisation, record-keeping and IT skills are essential, together with the ability to manage your own research and administrative activities and work effectively as part of a team. The post is part time (0.2 FTE) and is fixed-term until 31 January 2028. The role is available on a remote basis, with flexibility for occasional on-site working. What We Offer: As an employer, we genuinely care about your happiness and wellbeing at work and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave (pro rata) A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement detailing how you meet the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 4 November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189451 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681 - £41,636 per annum pro rata"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188788": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Immune-Metabolism and Multi-omics of Symptom \nDimensions",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Immune-Metabolism and Multi-omics of Symptom Dimensions Department of Psychiatry, University of Oxford, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. This is an exciting opportunity for a postdoctoral researcher to lead the computational work package of the Franco-British project “Immuno-metabolic profile of anhedonia in severe mental illness”, within the Immuno-metabolism and severe mental illness research group. Combining new UK and French cohort data with existing large-scale datasets, the project aims to identify and validate immune-metabolic signatures of anhedonia and other transdiagnostic symptoms across major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder and psychosis. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs This role also offers the opportunity for hybrid working. About the Role This part-time (0.6 FTE) post is fixed-term for 12 months and is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital, reporting to Dr Fabiana Corsi-Zuelli. You will lead the integration of genomic and phenotypic data from large external cohorts with the project's multi-omics datasets (proteomic, metabolomic and cellular immunophenotyping) to support biomarker discovery and validation, developing reproducible analysis pipelines. You will manage your own research, work closely with the UK and French partner teams, and contribute to project reporting, publications and conference presentations. About You You will have, or be close to completing, a relevant PhD/DPhil in neuroscience, bioinformatics, statistical genetics, computational biology, epidemiology, or a related quantitative discipline, together with relevant research experience. You will possess sufficient specialist knowledge in analysing and integrating high-dimensional multi-omics data with clinical or phenotypic data, knowledge of statistical genetic approaches using large-scale resources, strong programming and reproducible research skills, and excellent communication skills for multi-site international collaboration. Experience with UK Biobank, ALSPAC, or other population databases is desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity Our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/application-process The closing date for applications is 5 November 2026. Contact Person : HR Administrator Vacancy ID : 188788 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39424 -£41636 per annum pro rata part-time"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189338": {
      "title": "Researcher in Health Data Science and Infectious Disease Epidemiology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Researcher in Health Data Science and Infectious Disease Epidemiology Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF Researcher in Health Data Science and Infectious Disease Epidemiology Nuffield Department of Population Health, Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum (including Oxford University weighting) This is an exciting opportunity for a postdoctoral scientist to conduct primary research with multimodal data from a low-income setting. You will be a member of an international partnership that is employing cutting-edge big data methodologies for the study of a disease of poverty. The candidate will join the Big Data Institute and Nuffield Department of Population Health, both of which are home to world-renowned research groups applying quantitative methods to key global health issues. For this post, we are looking to appoint a highly motivated scientist to conduct research on doppler and transient elastrography readings from point-of-care ultrasound to build morbidity models for intestinal schistosomiasis. Your responsibilities and duties will include to support and collaborate with other group/project members and Ugandan researchers. You will publish research articles for leading peer-reviewed journals and present papers at flagship conferences or meetings, as well as participate in community/public engagement activities. You will hold or be close to completion of a PhD/DPhil in health data science. You will have demonstrated the ability to manage your own academic research and associated activities. A strong track record of advanced statistical skills and machine learning with health data is required. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online and require a supporting statement and CV. The supporting statement must address the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Further particulars, including details of how to apply, can be obtained from the document below. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will be required to upload a CV and supporting statement as part of your application. Only applications received by 12.00 midday on 29th October will be considered. Contact Person : NDPH Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189338 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ndph.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189425": {
      "title": "Clinical Trials Administrator with Play",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Clinical Trials Administrator with Play Department of Paediatrics, Oxford Vaccine Group, Churchill Hospital, Oxford About the role We are seeking to appoint a Clinical Trials Administrator (with Play) to join the Oxford Vaccine Group, Department of Paediatrics. Within the Oxford Vaccine Group the Clinical Trials Administrator (with Play) is responsible for the effective and efficient day to day administration of research studies to enable the academic research staff to fulfil their responsibilities. The Clinical Trials Administrator (with Play) will carry out varied office duties and specific study related tasks and acts as a pivotal point of contact for the clinical trial team. The also role includes assisting the paediatric study team by providing distraction to babies and children on visits within the community. This position is offered full-time on a fixed-term contract initially for one year with the possibility to extend provided further external funding is available. The role requires on site working five days per week, remote working cannot be facilitated due to the nature of the role. You will be working in a growing department within the Medical Sciences Division. The Department of Paediatrics is a world leader in child health research and hosts internationally renowned research programmes in drug development, gastroenterology, haematology, HIV, immunology, neuroimaging, neuromuscular diseases and vaccinology. About the department The Department of Paediatrics is committed to equality and valuing diversity. The Department of Paediatrics has been honoured with the Athena Swan Gold award, a national gender equality charter, recognising the Department's innovative policies and practices. We are committed to the professional development of our staff by providing up to ten paid days annually for skill enhancement and allowing applications for additional training funding. By joining us, you will have the opportunity to contribute to a forward-thinking department. We welcome new staff with ideas who are willing to shape the future of the department that thinks about its staff and wellbeing. In addition to the University of Oxford healthcare and wellbeing resources , the Department of Paediatrics sponsors weekly exercise classes such as yoga and bootcamp sessions as well as a choir. For staff on work visas, we also offer financial assistance towards visa renewal fees. These activities are designed to promote physical and mental wellbeing among staff members. Please visit our website to find out more about the Department. About you You will have experience in research clinical trials administration including working knowledge of regulatory and governance requirements for clinical trials. You will possess demonstrable experience in an administrative role working in an office environment, with excellent written and oral communication skills. Application process You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your application will not be processed if you do not include both documents. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. To discuss the post in more detail, please contact Conor Whelan, Senior Clinical Trial Manager, using the contact details below. Only online applications received before 12.00 midday on Friday 6th November 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. The Department of Paediatrics offers an Interview Expense Reimbursement Policy in its efforts towards equality, diversity and inclusion. Should you be invited to interview, we will share further information about the policy at that stage. Contact Person : Conor Whelan Vacancy ID : 189425 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 06-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 4 Contact Email : conor.whelan@paediatrics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 28,437 - 32,108 per annum (with a discretionary range to £34,732 per annum)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189414": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biotechnology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Biotechnology Department of Engineering Science, Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3PJ About the role This is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Researcher to join a project developing robotic tools for long term directed evolution of microbes. The project team includes experts in biotechnology, mechanical engineering, software, and robotics. The candidate will have a broad remit to take leadership roles in the research team, including by co-designing new research questions and defining application domains. They will also have opportunities to provide guidance to other members of the project group (such as PhD and collaborating Postdoctoral Researchers), and to present their work at conferences and other meetings. About you We are seeking ambitious candidates that hold a PhD/DPhil (or are near completion) in fields relevant to the project. To succeed in the role, it is essential that candidates have direct experience in the study and engineering of magnetic field effects in engineered biological systems, as well as experimental development of robotic tools and microscopy in the area. What we offer At the University of Oxford your happiness and wellbeing at work is important to us. We have a number of generous benefits including 38 days' annual leave (inclusive of bank holidays and fixed closure days), generous pension schemes and free access to the Oxford colleges. The other benefits include: Travel - We have several travel schemes available for staff which promote sustainable modes of transport. Family friendly benefits - We aim to be a family-friendly employer and offer a range of flexible working options. We have some of the most generous family leave provision in the Higher Education sector. Sports – We have several schemes to help you stay fit and healthy, including discounted membership of the university sports centre on the Iffley Road Personal and professional development - We actively encourage all staff to participate in planning their personal and professional development and provide a broad range of opportunities to enable them to reach their full potential. University club - All staff are entitled to free membership of the University Club. It offers social and hospitality facilities, a small gym and exercise classes. Staff discounts - University staff can access shopping discounts in several ways; by purchasing a TOTUM card, by showing an employee pass in selected local stores, or online by using any of the listed discount codes Childcare services - We offer excellent childcare services including five University nurseries as well as University-supported places at other local private nurseries. How to apply Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system and should include the covering letter/supporting statement (describing how you meet the criteria), a CV, and names of two academic references. The position is a fixed-term post available for 4 years. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on Friday 06 November 2026. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Harrison Steel: harrison.steel@eng.ox.ac.uk Further information can be found at http://www.eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/home Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and minority ethnic candidates who are under-represented in academic posts in Oxford. The Department is committed to equality and valuing diversity and holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting gender equality in academia. The University is a Living Wage Employer, holds an Athena Swan Silver Award, an HR Excellence in Research and a Race Equality Charter Bronze Award and is a Stonewall Diversity Champion. Our staff and students come from all over the world, and we seek to create a friendly and inclusive culture. Diversity is positively encouraged, through our EDI Committee, working groups and networks, for example eng.ox.ac.uk/women-in-engineering , as well as a number of family friendly policies . Contact Person : Professor Harrison Steel Vacancy ID : 189414 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 06-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : harrison.steel@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189501": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Computational Mechanics and Multistable Metamaterials",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Computational Mechanics and Multistable Metamaterials central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant to join RADlab (www.radlab-oxford.com) at the Department of Engineering Science (central Oxford). The post is funded by UKRI and is fixed-term (1st March 2027 to 31st August 2027). You will develop mechanical models of metamaterials based on inflatable and multistable structures, investigating how geometry, pressure and interactions between individual units govern their collective mechanical response. The research will combine nonlinear mechanics, numerical simulation and design optimisation to create materials with programmable shape, stiffness and load-bearing capacity, including systems that switch between and retain distinct stable configurations. You should possess A PhD, or be near completion of a PhD, in Mechanical Engineering, Computational Mechanics, Applied Mathematics or a closely related discipline, with the possibility to underfill at Grade 6 (£28,098 - £33,518 p.a.) if candidate holds a relevant degree/Masters and is working on PhD/DPhil, together with relevant experience You will be responsible for: 1. Developing computational models of inflatable and multistable structures and their assemblies, accounting for large deformations, material nonlinearity, contact and mechanical instabilities. 2. Investigating how unit geometry, internal pressure and assembly architecture influence deployment, stiffness, strength and transitions between stable configurations. 3. Characterising energy landscapes, snap-through transitions and hysteresis to establish design principles for mechanical metamaterials with controlled switching and shape retention. 4. Using numerical simulation and optimisation to design structures with prescribed mechanical responses, in collaboration with experimental researchers who will fabricate and test the designs and provide measurements for model validation. You should possess: 1. A PhD, or be near completion of a PhD, in Mechanical Engineering, Computational Mechanics, Applied Mathematics or a closely related discipline. 2. A strong background in solid mechanics, particularly nonlinear deformation, structural stability and the mechanics of soft or slender structures. 3. Experience in finite-element modelling using Abaqus, COMSOL, ANSYS or equivalent software, including nonlinear analysis and contact. 4. Research experience in mechanical metamaterials, multistable systems, inflatable structures, soft robotics or closely related areas. 5. Proficiency in Python or MATLAB for numerical modelling, simulation automation, data analysis and optimisation. 6. A record of peer-reviewed publications appropriate to your career stage, strong communication skills and the ability to work independently and collaboratively to meet research milestones. Experience in modelling buckling, snap-through and post-buckling behaviour, analysing energy barriers and stability, shell or membrane mechanics, pressure-volume coupling, or inverse design would be advantageous. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Prof Antonio Elia Forte (email: antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on 6th November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Soft-Robotics, Robotics, Mechanical Metamaterials, Inflatables, Mechanics Contact Person : Professor Antonio Forte Vacancy ID : 189501 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 06-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : antonio.forte@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189364": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Structural Mass Spectrometry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Structural Mass Spectrometry Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin Building, Biochemistry Department, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QU Postdoctoral Research Associate in Structural Mass Spectrometry Department of Biochemistry Pay Scale: STANDARD RESEARCH GRADE 7 Salary (£) : £42,794 - £47,779 per annum Location: Department of Biochemistry, South Parks Road, Oxford Contract type: Fixed Term Contract for up to 12 months in the first instance Hours: Full Time (37.5 hours per week) About the role Reporting to Professor Simon Newstead (Principal Investigator) and Dr Jo Parker (Senior Research Scientist), the post holder will be a member of the Newstead group in the Department of Biochemistry and Kavli Institute for Nanoscience, which studies the structure, mechanism and cellular biology of solute carrier (SLC) transporters. The post holder will lead the group's native mass spectrometry research, a discrete area within a larger programme on how SLC transporters are folded, modified, trafficked and regulated in mammalian cells. This full-time fixed-term post is funded by the Wellcome Trust for up to 12 months in the first instance. The actual starting salary offered will be based on qualifications and relevant skills acquired and will also be determined by the funding available. About you You will hold, or be near completion of, a PhD/DPhil in biophysical chemistry, analytical chemistry, structural biology or a related discipline, with substantial experience in native top-down mass spectrometry using high-resolution hybrid Orbitrap instrumentation, including Thermo Fisher Orbitrap UHMR and Ascend platforms. You will demonstrate expertise in native mass spectrometry sample preparation, advanced fragmentation techniques, quantitative analysis of protein modifications and lipid interactions, alongside experience in proteomics, lipidomics and membrane protein expression and purification. You will also possess strong computational and data-analysis skills, including scientific software development, ideally using Python. Experience in SLC transporter research, glycoproteomics and open-source software development, together with a strong publication record, would be advantageous. What We Offer Your happiness and wellbeing at work matters to us, so we offer a range of family-friendly and financial benefits including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans The University also runs a large number of social groups and sports clubs for those looking for more than just a great place to work. Application Process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via www.recruit.ox.ac.uk and Vacancy ID 189364 . You will be required to upload your curriculum vitae and a supporting statement, setting out how you meet the selection criteria for the post, using examples of your skills and experience. Click here for information and advice on writing an effective Supporting Statement. As part of your application, you will be asked to provide details of two referees and indicate whether we can contact them now. For further details, please refer to the How to apply section of the job description. Any further enquires may be directed to recruitment@bioch.ox.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on Monday 9th November 2026 . Only applications received before this time can be considered. Interviews for shortlisted candidates to be held as soon as possible thereafter. Committed to equality and valuing diversity Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189364 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@bioch.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £42,794 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189488": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical) Oxford Martin School, 34 Broad Street, Oxford, OX1 3BD We have an exciting opportunity to join the Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative (AIGI Oxford) as a Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical). The Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative seeks to improve the impact of AI on global societal outcomes through impactful research grounded in the social and computational sciences, decision-maker education campaigns, and training the next generation of technology governance leaders. It is one of the few organisations in the world to focus on the governance of AI from both technical and policy perspective. The successful candidate will join a grant-funded research project within a wider research programme. The project focuses on technical and political approaches to verifying that computational rules are being followed in AI data centres. It aims to provide states with viable, privacy-preserving and attack-resistant verification options for future international AI agreements. The post holder will pursue research in conjunction with other members of the team, with the aim of producing agenda-setting publications and policy reports. They will contribute ideas toward the strategic direction for the project, help sustain its research agenda, and provide day-to-day supervision of research assistants. The role requires specialisation, at doctoral level or equivalent, in the technical side of artificial intelligence, such as machine learning. This specialisation would ideally be combined with substantial practical or policy experience in domains related to AI verification such as technology policy, arms control, AI governance, and artificial intelligence. The post is a full-time, fixed-term appointment for two years. The Oxford Martin School supports the University’s commitment to equal opportunity and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. You will be required to upload a CV and a supporting statement as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Both documents must be submitted for your application to be considered. Only applications received before 12:00 noon (GMT) on Monday 9th November 2026 can be considered. We expect to hold interviews on Monday 16th November 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Administrator Vacancy ID : 189488 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@oxfordmartin.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £40,178 - £48,700 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189247": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Research Assistant - AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology Oxford Centre for Immuno-Oncology, Old Road Campus Research Building, Roosevelt Drive, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ We are seeking to appoint a Research Assistant (AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology) to join the Nuffield Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, working on an state of the art project funded by Advanced Research & Innovation Agency. Reporting to Dr Lennard Lee, Programme Director, you will serve as an AI Scientist & Computational Cancer Biology Research Assistant for the UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project. The UK Cancer Vaccine AI Scientist & Supercomputing Project is a government-supported national flagship research initiative hosted by the University of Oxford. The project brings together sovereign AI supercomputing, AI Scientists, automated laboratories, and immunology to develop and manufacture safer, more precise and more effective personalised cancer vaccines. To accelerate the development of personalised cancer vaccines for NHS patients, we seek to create an AI scientist capable of reviewing scientific discovery, generating hypotheses, designing experiments, controlling laboratory workflows, and analysing results. By integrating advanced machine learning with robotic laboratory automation, experimental findings are rapidly fed back to the AI scientist, enabling increasingly accurate predictions and accelerating the pace at which new scientific discoveries can be translated towards patient benefit. The project has been recognised as one of the UK’s leading strategic research initiatives, having been referenced within both the Government’s AI for Science Strategy and the 10-Year National Cancer Plan. It is supported by major UK research funders and government organisations, including ARIA, the Medical Research Council, UK Research and Innovation, Cancer Research UK and the Department for Science, Innovation and Technology, alongside a consortium of more than 2,500 scientists, clinicians, engineers, patient representatives and technology experts. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative multidisciplinary team working at the interface of artificial intelligence, computational biology, cancer immunology, robotics, software engineering and clinical research, contributing to research that aims to establish new approaches for the discovery and development of personalised cancer vaccines. You will provide computational research support within the programme, working with large-scale cancer, genomic and immunological datasets and applying established and emerging artificial intelligence approaches to cancer vaccine research. They will use frontier and open-source AI models and agentic coding tools, contribute to the development and evaluation of AI models and scientific agents, and support computational analyses alongside cancer biologists, immunologists and AI researchers. The post holder will manage their own research and administrative activities within agreed guidelines and contribute to project planning, data analysis, research outputs and collaborative research . It is essential that you hold a degree in computer science, computational biology, bioinformatics, genomics, cancer biology or a related discipline and possess sufficient specialist knowledge in computational biology, bioinformatics, artificial intelligence or data science to work within established research programmes. You will have experience analysing biological, genomic, biomedical or other large-scale research datasets using computational methods, practical experience using modern AI models or AI-assisted coding tools, with an ability to apply them appropriately to research tasks and some experience developing, adapting or evaluating computational models, machine-learning approaches, AI agents or reproducible computational workflows. Applications for this vacancy should be made online and you will need to upload a supporting statement and CV. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Please restrict your documentation to your CV and supporting statement only. Any other documents will be requested at a later date. This position is offered full time on a fixed term contract until 30 September 2030 and is funded by ARIA. Only applications received before 12 midday on Monday, 9 November 2026 will be considered. Please quote 189247 on all correspondence. Contact Person : Bogdan Tiritelnicu Vacancy ID : 189247 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 6: £35,681- £41,636 with a discretionary range to £46,476 p.a. (pro rata). This is inclusive of a pensionable Oxford University Weighting of £1,730 per year (pro rata)."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189489": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical) Oxford Martin School, 34 Broad Street, Oxford, OX1 3BD We have an exciting opportunity to join the Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative (AIGI Oxford) as a Senior Postdoctoral Researcher in AI Verification (Technical). The Oxford Martin AI Governance Initiative (AIGI Oxford) seeks to improve the impact of AI on global societal outcomes through impactful research that is rigorously grounded in the social and computational sciences, decision-maker education campaigns, and training the next generations of technology governance leaders. It is one of the few organisations in the world to focus on the governance of AI from both technical and policy perspectives. The successful candidate will play a leading role in a grant-funded research project focused on technical and political pathways for verifying compliance with computational rules in AI data centres. The project aims to develop viable, privacy-preserving, and attack-resistant verification mechanisms to support future international AI agreements. The postholder will pursue an independent research agenda, produce agenda-setting academic publications and policy reports, and contribute to the strategic direction of the project. They will supervise research assistants, support the development of future research activities, and build relationships across academia, government, industry, and civil society. The post is a full-time fixed-term appointment for two years. The Oxford Martin School supports the University's commitment to equal opportunity and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. You will be required to upload a CV and a supporting statement as part of your online application. Your supporting statement should explain how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Both documents must be submitted to be considered. Only applications received before 12:00 noon (GMT) on Monday 9th November 2026 can be considered. We expect to hold interviews on Monday 16th November 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Administrator Vacancy ID : 189489 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@oxfordmartin.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £50,067 - £59,396 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189348": {
      "title": "Computational Biologist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Computational Biologist Computational Biologist Oxford Centre for Diabetes Endocrinology and Metabolism (OCDEM), Churchill Hospital, Old Road, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LE Grade 7: £39,424 - £47,779 per annum An exciting opportunity has arisen for a Computational Biologist to lead the core computational analyses for a British Heart Foundation-funded project investigating how RSPO3 regulates human body fat distribution and cardiometabolic risk. The study combines recall-by-genotype human phenotyping with single-nucleus RNA sequencing of abdominal and gluteal adipose tissue, large-scale proteomic datasets and genetic analyses. You will analyse and integrate these data to define the cellular and molecular effects of RSPO3 and identify pathways linking adipose tissue biology to cardiometabolic disease. We are seeking a highly motivated Bioinformatician to lead the core computational analyses for a British Heart Foundation-funded three-year project investigating how RSPO3 regulates human body fat distribution and cardiometabolic risk. The study combines recall-by-genotype human phenotyping with single-nucleus RNA sequencing of abdominal and gluteal adipose tissue, large-scale proteomic datasets and genetic analyses. The postholder will analyse and integrate these data to define the cellular and molecular effects of RSPO3 and identify pathways linking adipose tissue biology to cardiometabolic disease. The appointee will bring strong computational and statistical skills and work closely with experts in adipose tissue biology, single-cell genomics and statistical genetics. The role offers substantial scope for intellectual contribution, method development, first-author publications and presentation of findings at national and international meetings. The position is full time and fixed term for 3 years in the first instance. The position will be based at OCDEM, Churchill Hospital, Oxford. For informal enquiries, please contact Ruby Ramsamy at ocdem.personnel@ndm.ox.ac.uk Please quote reference 189348 on all correspondence. As part of your formal online application, you will be required to upload a CV and supporting statement. Only applications received before midday on Tuesday, 10 November 2026 can be considered. Interviews are scheduled for the W/C 30 November 2026. Contact Person : Ruby Ramsamy Vacancy ID : 189348 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 7 Contact Email : ocdem.personnel@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424- £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189467": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Research Assistant Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, New Biochemistry Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QU About the role Applications are invited for a Research Assistant in Chemistry to work under the supervision of Professor Justin Benesch for a period of up to 10 months. The project involves assisting in key research of the Benesch research group and is funded by the BBSRC. About you Applicants must hold an undergraduate science degree, prior to taking up your appointment. The research requires experience in optical microscopy, mass photometry and extensive knowledge of python as well as machine learning based data analysis to support our project. The post will be based in the Department of Chemistry, and Kavli Institute for Nanoscience Discovery, and is available from 1st of January 2027. The position is for 0,6FTE. Application process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your application. Your supporting statement must explain how you meet each of the selection criteria for the post using examples of your skills and experience. Certificates, references and research papers should not be provided at this stage. To apply for this role and for further details, including a job description and selection criteria, please click on the link below. The closing date for applications is 12.00 midday, Tuesday 10 November 2026. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. The University of Oxford and The Department of Chemistry are Silver Athena SWAN holders. Applications are particularly welcome from women and black and ethnic minority candidates, who are under-represented in Chemistry research posts in Oxford. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189467 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@chem.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681 - £41,636 per annum (pro rata for part-time appointments) (inclusive of Oxford University Weighting)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189123": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Natural Product Chemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate in Natural Product Chemistry Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road and Department of Pharmacology, Mansfield Road, Oxford About the role The Oxford Martin Programme on Safer Antifungals aims to unlock a new generation of naturally inspired products to combat fungal diseases in agriculture and medicine. You will lead a biochemical research project exploiting a novel source of natural antifungal products. The work centres on microscopic freshwater animals called bdelloid rotifers, which naturally protect themselves from fungi using genes acquired through horizontal DNA transfer. You will join an interdisciplinary team translating this recent discovery into a platform for identifying safer antifungal compounds bringing together expertise from Biology, Pharmacology, Chemistry, Medicine, the Ineos Oxford Institute and Oxford University Innovation. Your work will include bioactivity-guided extraction and purification of antifungal compounds, elucidation of their structure and mechanism of action, investigation of biosynthetic origins and candidate pathways, chemical or biosynthetic diversification of hit compounds, performing assays for biological activity and toxicity, and writing up and communicating results in scientific publications and for future research translation. About you The successful applicant will have strong research expertise and specialist knowledge in chemistry, biochemistry, biosynthetic chemistry or related area of chemical biology, including a PhD in a relevant discipline. They will have prior relevant laboratory experience in one or more of the following areas: purifying chemicals from complex biological mixtures; elucidation of chemical structures for biologically active molecules; or genetic and synthetic-biology approaches to natural product synthesis, including biosynthetic pathway elucidation and reconstruction of bioactive molecules. They will show initiative in developing research ideas and methodologies, have excellent written and verbal communication skills, and work effectively as part of a multidisciplinary team and in advising and supervising other team members. Strong interest or experience in research translation and innovation is also desirable. How to apply The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. This post is full time (although applications to work >50% of full time are welcome) and available from 1 January 2027 for 36 months (or until 31 December 2029). The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on Wednesday 11 November 2026, interviews are likely to be scheduled for mid-November 2026. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Contact Person : HR Vacancy ID : 189123 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@biology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £43,984 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189435": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Graduate Research Assistant Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 6: £35,681-£41,636 per annum Applications are invited for a Graduate Research Assistant to join the Brain, Speech & Language Research Group led by Professor Kate Watkins in the Department of Experimental Psychology. The post is funded by a European Research Council grant for the STUTTER project, which investigates the neural mechanisms underlying stuttering across the lifespan. The successful applicant will assist with a longitudinal study of children who stutter and children who do not stutter, including participant recruitment, behavioural and neuroimaging assessments, and follow-up testing. Working closely with the Principal Investigator and postdoctoral researcher, the postholder will contribute to participant testing, data collection and analysis, and the preparation of publications, reports and conference presentations. The post is full-time and fixed term for 2 years in the first instance, with the possibility of extension subject to funding. Flexible working, including evenings, weekends and school holidays, will be required to facilitate participant testing and MRI scanning sessions. The role is based in the Life and Mind Building and involves work at the Oxford Centre for Human Brain Activity in Headington, Oxford. Applicants should hold a good first degree (at least a 2:1) in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, speech and language sciences, or a related discipline. Experience of experimental research methods, behavioural assessment and/or neuroimaging research is essential. Experience of working with children, speech and language disorders, MRI or MEG would be advantageous. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 13 November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189435 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681 - £41,636"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189436": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Graduate Research Assistant Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 6: £35,681-£41,636 per annum Applications are invited for a Graduate Research Assistant to join the Brain, Speech & Language Research Group led by Professor Kate Watkins within the Department of Experimental Psychology. The post is funded by a European Research Council grant for the STUTTER project, which investigates the neural mechanisms underlying stuttering across the lifespan. The successful applicant will assist with a randomised controlled trial investigating the effects of non-invasive brain stimulation combined with speech fluency training in adults who stutter. Working closely with the Principal Investigator and postdoctoral researcher, the postholder will contribute to participant recruitment, delivery of experimental procedures, data collection and analysis, and the preparation of publications, reports and conference presentations. Outcomes will be evaluated using speech recordings, MRI and MEG. The post is full-time and fixed term for 2 years in the first instance, with the possibility of extension subject to funding. Flexible working, including evenings and weekends, may occasionally be required. The role is based in the Life and Mind Building and involves work at the Oxford Centre for Human Brain Activity in Headington, Oxford. Applicants should hold a good first degree (at least a 2:1) in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, speech and language sciences, or a related discipline. Experience of experimental research methods, behavioural assessment and/or neuroimaging research is essential. Experience of MRI, MEG or non-invasive brain stimulation techniques would be advantageous. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 13 November 2026 only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189436 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £35,681-£41,636 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189420": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 7: £39,424-£47,779 per annum Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Brain, Speech & Language Research Group led by Kate Watkins within the Department of Experimental Psychology. The post is funded by the European Research Council for the STUTTER project, which investigates the neural mechanisms underlying stuttering across the lifespan. The successful applicant will lead a randomised controlled trial investigating the effects of non-invasive brain stimulation combined with speech fluency training in adults who stutter. The postholder will be responsible for participant recruitment, delivery of experimental procedures and evaluation of outcomes using speech recordings, MRI and MEG. They will also contribute to publications, conference presentations and other research outputs arising from the project and provide day-to-day supervision of a graduate research assistant. The project team comprises the Principal Investigator, two postdoctoral researchers and two graduate research assistants and benefits from expertise and infrastructure at the Oxford Centre for Integrative Neuroimaging (OxCIN). The post is full-time and fixed term for 2 years in the first instance, with the possibility of extension subject to funding. Flexible working, including evenings and weekends, may occasionally be required. The role is based in the Life and Mind Building and involves work at the Oxford Centre for Human Brain Activity in Headington, Oxford. Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, speech and language sciences, or a related discipline. Experience of human participant research and behavioural assessment, neuroimaging or neurostimulation methods is essential. Experience of MRI, MEG or non-invasive brain stimulation techniques would be advantageous. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 13 November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189420 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189417": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL Grade 7: £39,424-£47,779 per annum Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Brain, Speech & Language Research Group led by Kate Watkins within the Department of Experimental Psychology. The post is funded by the European Research Council for the STUTTER project, which investigates the neural mechanisms underlying stuttering across the lifespan. The successful applicant will lead a longitudinal study of children who stutter and children who do not stutter, using behavioural assessments and brain imaging methods to investigate why stuttering persists in some children but resolves in others. The postholder will be responsible for participant recruitment, behavioural and neuroimaging assessments, data collection and management, and will contribute to publications, conference presentations and other research outputs arising from the project. The post is full-time and fixed term for 2 years in the first instance, with the possibility of extension subject to funding. Flexible working, including evenings, weekends and school holidays, may occasionally be required. The role is based in the Life and Mind Building and involves work at the Oxford Centre for Human Brain Activity in Headington, Oxford. Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, speech and language sciences, or a related discipline. Experience of human participant research and behavioural and/or neuroimaging methods is essential. Experience of research involving children, speech and language disorders, MRI or MEG would be advantageous. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 13 November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189417 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189393": {
      "title": "Medical Statistician / Statistical Programmer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Medical Statistician / Statistical Programmer Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford),Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF We are seeking a Medical Statistician/Statistical Programmer to join the Cancer Epidemiology Unit (CEU), within the Nuffield Department of Population Health at the University of Oxford. You will work closely with statisticians, epidemiologists and other colleagues on large prospective cohort studies investigating lifestyle, dietary, hormonal, molecular and genetic factors in relation to cancer and other common non-communicable diseases. The post focuses on linking and managing large epidemiological datasets, preparing data for analysis, and carrying out statistical analyses. You will work particularly with the EPIC study and EPIC-Oxford, which links cohort data with NHS records of cancer registrations, deaths and hospital admissions. You will also support EPIC-Oxford 2, a new study building on the cohort. Your work will include managing research databases; incorporating electronic health records, questionnaire data and laboratory results; deriving disease outcomes from coded health data; and preparing datasets for cohort and nested case-control studies. You will analyse data, mainly using survival and logistic regression, and prepare tables, figures and reports for research projects and publications. There may also be opportunities to work with biomarker and omics data. You will hold a graduate degree, or have equivalent experience, in medical statistics, statistics, health data science or another quantitative subject, with training in statistical analysis and epidemiological study design. You will have experience working with routinely collected electronic health records and preparing epidemiological data for analysis, including survival analysis and logistic regression. You will also be proficient in Stata and/or R, and have experience using SQL and a relational database system to manage large datasets. Excellent attention to detail, clear and well-documented coding, and the ability to work independently and collaboratively are essential, as are strong communication and organisational skills. An understanding of epidemiological concepts and study designs, experience working with NHS-linked data under information governance requirements, or experience with biobank, sample-tracking, biomarker, dietary or omics data would be an advantage. Experience contributing to peer-reviewed publications and designing databases would also be beneficial. The post is full-time (although part-time is considered) and fixed term for 2 years. The closing date for applications is noon on 16 November 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a cover letter as part of your online application. The cover letter should clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Contact Person : NDPH HR Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189393 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : STANDARD GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ndph.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189368": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral researcher in aerosols, clouds and their interactions with focus on km-scale modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Post-doctoral researcher in aerosols, clouds and their interactions with focus on km-scale modelling Department of Physics, AOPP, Oxford Applications are invited for a Post-doctoral researcher in aerosols, clouds and their interactions with focus on km-scale modelling. This position, aligned with the EU Horizon Europe project Next-Generation Earth System Modelling for Realistic Projections of Rapid Climate Evolution in the Coming Decades (RealESM) with partners across Europe, will advance the representation of aerosols, clouds and their interaction in next-generation global km-scale Earth System Models (ESMs) and apply them to assess of aerosol effects on effective radiative forcing, precipitation and extreme events, with a particular focus on convective systems. Successful applicants will work closely with our national and international collaborators and are expected to develop innovative research approaches. The role also includes the possibility to co-advise doctoral students. Results should be presented at national and international meetings as well as published in leading subject and high-impact publications. The post-holder will have the opportunity to teach. Applicants should hold a PhD/DPhil (or be close to completion) in atmospheric physics, or related fields. Strong background and track record in atmospheric physics is essential as well as strong computing skills, including the knowledge of UNIX/Linux, Fortran, Python, or other high-level languages. The post is full time and fixed term for 30 months. The closing date for applications is noon GMT on Monday, 16 November 2026 You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement and details of two referees, as part of your online application. The Supporting Statement should include a cover letter and should also clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Referees are also required to submit their references to Heather Waller: aopp-admin@physics.ox.ac.uk ** by the closing date.** Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189368 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7RS: £39, 424 -£47, 779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=188987": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Machine Learning",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Machine Learning central Oxford We are seeking two full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistants in Machine Learning to join the Visual Geometry Group (VGG), in central Oxford. The positions are associated with two separate research projects funded by Toyota Motor Europe and Amazon, respectively. Each successful candidate will be assigned to one of these projects and will work under the supervision of Dr. João Henriques. Both posts are available for 12 months in the first instance. The Toyota Motor Europe project, RoboSim, involves research in deep learning, at the intersection of reinforcement learning and computer vision, to train effective robotic agents in simulation. The research will focus on developing robust visuomotor policies that can transfer to real robots, in collaboration with researchers at the Oxford Robotics Institute and Toyota Motor Europe. The Amazon project involves research on robotic manipulation using computer vision and vision-language-action models. The research will investigate how representations of 3D geometry and motion can help robots manipulate a wide range of objects, including deformable objects, and generalise to new settings. The methods will be evaluated on robotic manipulation tasks, including experiments on real robots. You should hold a relevant PhD/DPhil (or near completion*) in Machine Learning, Computer Vision, Robotics or a related field. You should have a strong publication record at leading international conferences in these areas, together with sufficient theoretical and practical knowledge of deep learning methodologies. Research experience in reinforcement learning, computer vision or robotics would be desirable. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr. João Henriques (email: joao@robots.ox.ac.uk). For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only online applications received before midday on the closing date stated in the online advert can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised positions), a CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Applicants may indicate whether they have a preference for either project. * “Near completion” means that you must have submitted your PhD thesis. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze Award, recognising its commitment to advancing gender equality and supporting an inclusive working environment. Machine learning; Computer vision; Robotics; Reinforcement learning; Robotic manipulation Contact Person : Joao Henriques Vacancy ID : 188987 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : joao.henriques@eng.ox.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189512": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Modelling of Quantum Computing Control Systems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Modelling of Quantum Computing Control Systems central Oxford We are seeking a motivated and Talented experimentalist for a full-time Research Assistant in Quantum Computing Control Systems within Professor Ares’ research group at the Department of Engineering Science (Central Oxford). The post is externally funded 8 months fixed-term. The development of large-scale quantum computers will require a deep understanding of the classical infrastructure that supports them, including analog control systems. As quantum devices scale toward the million-qubit regime, modeling these complex systems becomes essential. This project will focus on building machine learning models and comprehensive digital twins of quantum devices to investigate how scaling impacts the control and stability of quantum operations. The researcher will create models of the physical and control architecture, enabling the identification of design bottlenecks and optimization strategies. The digital twins and simulators will serve as a testbed for evaluating engineering trade-offs and guiding future hardware development. The appointed researcher will collaborate with academic teams, industrial partners, and national laboratories, contributing to the broader QCI3 initiative aimed at advancing scalable and interconnected quantum computing technologies. Reporting to the Principal Investigator, you will help ensure a healthy and vibrant research environment within Natalia Ares’ research group. You should possess a PhD/DPhil and the ability to develop new research projects and raise research funds through making grant applications. Excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and results, and represent the research group at meetings is essential. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr Natalia Ares (email: natalia.ares@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only applications received before midday on the 16 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, (describing how past experience fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Contact Person : Professor Natalia Ares Vacancy ID : 189512 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : natalia.ares@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189396": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Climate Policy and Regulation (Oxford Climate Policy Hub)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Climate Policy and Regulation (Oxford Climate Policy Hub) Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford OX2 6GG About the Blavatnik School of Government Our vision is of a world better led, a world better served and a world better governed. We are a global school committed to improving the quality of government and public policymaking worldwide, through three routes: teaching current and future leaders ; generating research with impact ; and engaging with governments and practitioners . What we offer The School is a collaborative, friendly, and dynamic department based in an award-winning building in Oxford. We host regular events and talks with well-known guest speakers – all of which are open to staff. On the social side, we have a regular events calendar that are designed to appeal to all, including our Christmas and summer parties. As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days’ annual leave • Family leave schemes and a comprehensive range of childcare services • Cycle loan scheme and discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Opportunities for hybrid working ( typically 60% of working hours on-site and 40% remotely) More information about working at the School can be found on our jobs page . About the Role You will contribute to the Hub’s research on climate policy, regulation and governance, working collaboratively to develop innovative research across sectors and jurisdictions. You will design and adapt research methodologies, analyse qualitative and quantitative evidence, and explore how climate policies and regulations are designed, interact and spread across different contexts. You will help shape new research projects and funding proposals, contribute to academic publications and book chapters, including comparative research using the Climate Policy Monitor, and present findings at conferences and other events. Working with colleagues and partner institutions, you will manage your own research activities, share methodological expertise and engage with policymakers, regulators and other stakeholders. You may also contribute to developing new areas of the Hub’s research and Climate Policy Monitor. About You You will hold, or be close to completing, a relevant PhD/DPhil in public policy, political science, law, economics, international relations or a related discipline, alongside relevant research experience. You will have strong specialist knowledge of climate policy, regulation or governance in one or more sectors or jurisdictions, with the ability to contribute to comparative research across different contexts and develop innovative research ideas. You will have effective organisational and project management skills, with the ability to manage your own research and meet deadlines. You will bring experience of contributing to research publications or presentations, alongside the ability to develop new research ideas and contribute to funding proposals. Excellent written and verbal communication skills are essential, including the ability to write for publication, present research proposals and findings, and represent the research group at meetings. Application process Please upload a supporting statement that outlines how you meet the selection criteria in your own words , along with your CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is at 12 noon (UK time) on Monday 16 November 2026 Interviews are expected to take place during week commencing 7 December 2026 and will be online. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189396 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@bsg.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 to £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189513": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Electrification and Power Sector Decarbonisation (Oxford Climate Policy Monitor)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Electrification and Power Sector Decarbonisation (Oxford Climate Policy Monitor) Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford OX2 6GG About the Blavatnik School of Government Our vision is of a world better led, a world better served and a world better governed. We are a global school committed to improving the quality of government and public policymaking worldwide, through three routes: teaching current and future leaders ; generating research with impact ; and engaging with governments and practitioners . What we offer The School is a collaborative, friendly, and dynamic department based in an award-winning building in Oxford. We host regular events and talks with well-known guest speakers – all of which are open to staff. On the social side, we have a regular events calendar that are designed to appeal to all, including our Christmas and summer parties. As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days’ annual leave • Family leave schemes and a comprehensive range of childcare services • Cycle loan scheme and discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Opportunities for hybrid working ( typically 60% of working hours on-site and 40% remotely) More information about working at the School can be found on our jobs page . About the Role You will play a key role in developing new electrification-related domains of the Climate Policy Monitor, working with researchers, developers and external partners to shape their scope, methodology and integration into the tool. You will oversee data collection and validation, co-ordinate research activities and manage the Hub’s contributions to the Oxford Electrification Accelerator, working closely with colleagues across the University. Your research will focus on electricity market design, regulation and power-sector decarbonisation, developing methodologies and analysing complex policy and regulatory data to inform new research and publications. You will contribute to research proposals and funding opportunities, collaborate with partner institutions, and communicate findings through publications, conferences and external meetings. You will also provide specialist advice to colleagues and engage with policymakers, regulators, system operators and industry stakeholders. About You You will hold, or be close to completing, a relevant PhD/DPhil in economics, public policy, law, engineering or a related discipline, with relevant experience focused on energy, electricity or climate. You will bring specialist knowledge of electricity systems, energy and electricity market regulation and/or power-sector decarbonisation, including an understanding of different jurisdictions relevant to the Monitor’s global scope. You will be confident working with large, complex policy and regulatory datasets and have strong organisational and project management skills, including the ability to manage your own research and competing priorities. You will have experience contributing to research publications or presentations, alongside excellent written and verbal communication skills. You will be able to present research proposals and findings clearly, represent the research group at meetings and build effective working relationships with external experts, partners and data users. You will also be able to develop ideas for new research projects and contribute to identifying opportunities for research funding. Application process Please upload a supporting statement that outlines how you meet the selection criteria in your own words , along with your CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is at 12 noon (UK time) on Monday 16 November 2026 Interviews are expected to take place during the week commencing 7 December 2026 and will be online. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189513 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@bsg.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 to £43,984 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189333": {
      "title": "Hooke Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Hooke Research Fellow Andrew Wiles Building, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG We are seeking talented postdoctoral researchers to take up exciting Fellowship opportunities at the Mathematical Institute. The prestigious Hooke Research Fellowships provide an ideal opportunity for candidates to pursue an independent research programme in applied mathematics, and to join a large existing cohort of leading researchers on Fellowship programmes at the Mathematical Institute . The Department is also advertising a similar fellowship scheme for Titchmarsh Research Fellows in pure mathematics ( vacancy 189327 ). The Fellowships are full-time and fixed-term for up to 36 months; applications for job shares or for part-time working to accommodate family circumstances will be considered. Candidates will have research interests compatible with those of our current faculty in the following research groups: Mathematical Biology, Machine Learning and Data Science, Mathematical and Computational Finance, Numerical Analysis, or the Centre for Industrial and Applied Mathematics (OCIAM). You must clearly state at the start of your supporting letter which one research group you feel is closest to your work. You may also indicate a second-choice research group. Candidates whose research straddles the two schemes are encouraged to apply separately for both positions. The successful applicants will be talented researchers who have already produced excellent research during their PhD and in any postdoctoral positions. Securing a Fellowship will offer you the freedom to conduct your own research programme. Fellows will have access to office space, a desktop computer and £3,000 per annum for travel and other academic expenses. To aid the development of their academic profile, Fellows will be required to contribute up to three hours of teaching per week for the 24 weeks of each academic year, averaged over the period of the Fellowship to allow for extended visits elsewhere. The exact nature of teaching will be determined by departmental need and may include teaching in a college to provide cover for a member of faculty. Candidates will have, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in mathematics or a related discipline. They must demonstrate independence and the ability to undertake research of the highest academic standard, an outstanding publication record commensurate with career stage, and a realisable and relevant research plan which would enhance the research profile of the Mathematical Institute. Further selection criteria are available in the job description. We proudly hold a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award and an institutional Race Equality Charter Bronze Award. We welcome applications from all backgrounds, particularly from women and ethnic minorities. Please direct informal enquiries to recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 189333. Applicants should refer to the job description and ensure they follow all the instructions in the ‘How to Apply’ section. Applicants must also complete the research-group selection form . Those applying for both Fellowships must submit a separate application and form for each vacancy. Applicants are responsible for contacting two referees and ensuring that reference letters are uploaded to the portal by the closing date. Full details are contained in the job description. Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on Tuesday 17th November will be considered. Interviews are anticipated to take place in early January 2027. Contact Person : Recruitment Coordinator Vacancy ID : 189333 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 17-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recrutiment@maths.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £43,984 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189327": {
      "title": "Titchmarsh Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Titchmarsh Research Fellow Andrew Wiles Building, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG We are seeking talented postdoctoral researchers to take up exciting Fellowship opportunities at the Mathematical Institute. The prestigious Titchmarsh Research Fellowships provide an ideal opportunity for candidates to pursue an independent research programme in pure mathematics, and to join a large existing cohort of leading researchers on Fellowship programmes at the Mathematical Institute . The Department is also advertising a similar fellowship scheme for Hooke Research Fellows in applied mathematics ( vacancy 189333 ). The Fellowships are full-time and fixed-term for up to 36 months; applications for job shares or for part-time working to accommodate family circumstances will be considered. Candidates will have research interests compatible with those of our current faculty in the following research groups: Algebra, Combinatorics, Geometry, History of Mathematics, Logic, Number Theory, OxPDE, Functional Analysis, Stochastic Analysis, or Topology. You must clearly state at the start of your supporting letter which one research group you feel is closest to your work. You may also indicate a second-choice research group. Candidates whose research straddles the two schemes are encouraged to apply separately for both positions. The successful applicants will be talented researchers who have already produced excellent research during their PhD and in any postdoctoral positions. Securing a Fellowship will offer you the freedom to conduct your own research programme. Fellows will have access to office space, a desktop computer and £3,000 per annum for travel and other academic expenses. To aid the development of their academic profile, Fellows will be required to contribute up to three hours of teaching per week for the 24 weeks of each academic year, averaged over the period of the Fellowship to allow for extended visits elsewhere. The exact nature of teaching will be determined by departmental need and may include teaching in a college to provide cover for a member of faculty. Candidates will have, or be close to completing, a PhD/DPhil in mathematics or a related discipline. They must demonstrate independence and the ability to undertake research of the highest academic standard, an outstanding publication record commensurate with career stage, and a realisable and relevant research plan which would enhance the research profile of the Mathematical Institute. Further selection criteria are available in the job description. We proudly hold a departmental Athena SWAN Silver Award and an institutional Race Equality Charter Bronze Award. We welcome applications from all backgrounds, particularly from women and ethnic minorities. Please direct informal enquiries to recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk, quoting vacancy reference 189327. Applicants should refer to the job description and ensure they follow all the instructions in the ‘How to Apply’ section. Applicants must also complete the research-group selection form . Those applying for both Fellowships must submit a separate application and form for each vacancy. Applicants are responsible for contacting two referees and ensuring that reference letters are uploaded to the portal by the closing date. Full details are contained in the job description. Only applications received before 12.00 noon UK time on Tuesday 17th November will be considered. Interviews are anticipated to take place in early January 2027. Contact Person : Recruitment Coordinator Vacancy ID : 189327 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 17-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@maths.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £43,984 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189357": {
      "title": "Computational Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/11/2026 Computational Postdoctoral Research Associate NDM Experimental Medicine, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford. OX3 9DU About the role We are seeking a creative, collaborative and enthusiastic computational chemist or biophysicist to lead on the molecular simulation work, including free energy calculations, to support our Gram-Negative Antibiotic Discovery Innovator (Gr-ADI) project. This is an exciting opportunity for a postdoctoral researcher to join an interdisciplinary research team who are working together to discover novel small molecules able to inhibit key protein targets in Klebsiella pneumoniae with the aim that these could be used as antimicrobials to treat infections. The key scientific objective for the postholder will be help mitigate the emergence of antimicrobial resistance by using free energy simulations to identify, for each target/lead pair, which genetic mutations reduce the binding of the ligand, and thereby could lead to resistance. By being part of the core team your results will help guide subsequent optimisation of the lead compounds. The postholder will be based in the Modernising Medical Microbiology (MMM) Unit at the John Radcliffe Hospital in Oxford (https://www.expmedndm.ox.ac.uk/mmm). Expertise within MMM spans clinical and laboratory microbiology, public health, genomics (including WGS and metagenomics), statistics, machine learning, bioinformatics and software engineering, with a culture in which knowledge is shared and individuals supported. We aim to improve population and individual health through our research and ensure our findings are applied quickly through our relationships with the NHS, public health bodies and the private sector. The main responsibilities will include working with the wider team to identify suitable target/ligand complexes, preparing, running and analysing the required molecular simulations, updating the project team with your results, which may in turn lead to updated target/ligand complexes. You will need to manage your time, lead on the production of scientific abstracts and manuscripts and attend, where necessary, scientific meetings and conferences. As an experienced researcher you will be expected to provide advice and support for other group members who may be less experienced (e.g. DPhil students) or are trained in a different discipline. About you You will hold a PhD in a relevant scientific subject such as computational chemistry or biophysics and have experience setting up, running and analysing classical molecular dynamics simulations and ideally also have carried out alchemical free energy simulations on protein/ligand complexes. You will have familiarity with Linux/Unix command-line environments and high-level programming languages, preferably Python3. Familiarity with high-performance computing and/or cloud computing and an interest in antimicrobial resistance and drug development would be advantageous but not required. If you have experience in molecular simulation and want to apply your skills and experience to a real-world problem, in this case to help discover new antimicrobials, we’d love to hear from you. Benefits of working In return we offer an excellent benefits package including: • 38 days annual leave (pro rata for part time) • Hybrid working • An excellent contributory pension scheme • Childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle and electric car loan schemes • Employee Assistance Programme • Membership to social and sports clubs • Discounted bus travel and travel loans • Weekly coffee and cake mornings, and social events throughout the year Application Process This role is based in Oxford and offered on a full-time basis. It is a fixed-term post with funding available for 24 months and the project is funded until 28 Feb 2029. The closing date for applications is noon on Wednesday 18/11/2026. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. You will be required to upload a supporting evidence form (available below) and a CV as part of your online application. No covering letter is required, and CVs alone will not be considered. The supporting evidence form must explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable selection criteria for the post (listed in the job description) using examples drawing on your skills and experience. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189357 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 18-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : exp.med-hr@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 p.a."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189356": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Multi-view 3D Ultrasound Reconstruction",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Research Associate in Multi-view 3D Ultrasound Reconstruction Computer Science - Wolfson Building, Parks Road, Oxford We are currently looking for a Research Associate in Multi-view 3D Ultrasound Reconstruction in the Oxford Machine Learning in Neuroimaging (OMNI) lab at Oxford’s Department of Computer Science, funded by the European Research Council through the WOMB2COT Starting Grant. The post-holder will develop 3D reconstruction and multi-view data fusion methods for neonatal cranial ultrasound (cUS), and validate them on data collected in the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU). Reporting directly to Professor Ana Namburete, the successful candidate will work within a multidisciplinary team of deep learning researchers, research software engineers, and neonatologists, and will collaborate with Dr Frances O’Brien (Consultant Neonatologist, Oxford University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust). The post-holder will contribute to research at the intersection of 3D computer vision, neural rendering, and medical image analysis. This includes physics-informed rendering models that account for view-dependent acoustic appearance, and working with multi-fontanelle data acquired by clinical colleagues. This post is based at the Department of Computer Science. About Us The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Computer Science at Oxford is renowned for pioneering research and teaching across diverse fields, consistently ranking among the best in the world. Our commitment to innovation drives us to tackle complex technological and societal challenges. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing, reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • Excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave (including public holidays) – pro rata for part time jobs • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme and discounted public transport The successful candidate will be based onsite in the Department of Computer Science buildings in central Oxford. However, remote working from within the UK and flexible working arrangements can be considered. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity. Application Process You will be required to upload a supporting statement and an up-to-date CV as part of your online application. Your supporting statement must clearly demonstrate how you meet each of the essential selection criteria listed in the job description. Applications that do not include a supporting statement or CV, or fail to address the criteria in sufficient detail, will not be considered. While we recognise the value of AI tools in assisting with application preparation, submissions that are clearly AI-generated without personalisation or insight will be rejected. It's crucial that your application reflects your own experiences and understanding of the role. The closing date for applications is midday on 23 November 2026 . Interviews are expected to be held in early December. Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189356 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@cs.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 07RS: £39,424 – £43,984 per annum inclusive of Oxford University weighting"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189383": {
      "title": "Arab Enlightenment Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Arab Enlightenment Research Fellow Voltaire Foundation, University of Oxford, Clarendon Institute, Walton Street, Oxford OX1 2HG Contract type Fixed Term Contract (2 years) Hours Full time (37.5 hours) About the role The Voltaire Foundation is a busy and friendly research department within the Humanities Division, based at the Clarendon Institute, Walton Street, Oxford, specialising in the European 18th Century and digital text editing. We seek a Research Fellow who will pursue their own two-year research project in the general area of the relationship between the European Enlightenment and the Arabic and Islamic worlds, including the intellectual, philosophical, cultural, political and religious exchanges that shaped Enlightenment thought in a global context. The Research Fellow will also assist with a programme of events relating to the reception of Enlightenment debates in the modern world, such as lecture organisation and seminars. The role is fixed-term for 2 years and fully supported by The Arab Enlightenment Fellowship, Professor Al Khalifa, University College Bahrain. Application process Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a CV and a supporting statement explaining why you consider yourself particularly suited to this position, with reference to the essentials of the person specification. As part of your application you will be asked to provide details of two referees and indicate whether we can contact them now. For further details, please refer to the How to apply section of the job description. Any further enquires may be directed to hr@humanities.ox.ac.uk. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on Friday 27 November 2026. Only applications received before this time can be considered. Shortlisting will take place 2-4 December and interviews will be held in the week of 14 December 2026, day to be confirmed. Please quote Vacancy ID 189383 on all correspondence. Committed to equality and valuing diversity. Contact Person : Andrea Williams Vacancy ID : 189383 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 27-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@humanities.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : (Grade 7.1 – 7.3) £39,424-£41,636 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189392": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Associate in the Economics of Technological Change",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Post Doctoral Research Associate in the Economics of Technological Change Smith School, School of Geography and the Environment, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QY The University of Oxford is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to the Oxford Martin Programme on Forecasting Technological Change. Led by Dr François Lafond, Professor J. Doyne Farmer and Professor Max Roser, the programme aims to improve understanding of how technologies develop, diffuse and evolve, with a particular focus on the net zero transition and artificial intelligence. Working within the Complexity Economics research group at the Institute for New Economic Thinking (INET), you will contribute to the development of a comprehensive database on technological progress and undertake quantitative research examining the drivers of technological change. The research will explore how technological characteristics influence innovation and diffusion, whether rates of technological progress can be predicted, and how declining costs and increasing adoption interact. The role combines independent research, quantitative analysis and research project management. You will lead the preparation of peer-reviewed publications, develop and apply research methodologies, analyse complex datasets, and contribute to research proposals and funding applications. You will also coordinate and supervise a small team of part-time research assistants and collaborate with researchers across disciplines and partner institutions. We are seeking a candidate who holds a PhD/DPhil or be close to completion in a relevant discipline, with strong quantitative and statistical skills, programming experience, and knowledge of complex systems science and/or advanced forecasting techniques. You should have an understanding of the economics of innovation, net zero technologies and/or artificial intelligence, including experience with sophisticated applications of large language models. Previous contributions to publications in leading academic journals, excellent communication skills and the ability to manage research activities are essential. The successful candidate will join an interdisciplinary research environment as both a member of INET's Complexity Economics research group and an Oxford Martin Fellow, with opportunities to contribute to research on the future of technological development. This position offers an opportunity to advance quantitative research on technological change and contribute to new approaches for forecasting developments in clean technologies and artificial intelligence. This post is offered on a full time, fixed-term basis for 2 years from start date . What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ well-being, and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter with a supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. In your supporting statement, please explain how you meet each of the selection criteria found in the job description, and why you would like to do this role. See guidance at https://www.jobs.ox.ac.uk/cv-and-supporting-statement. Any technical questions related to this vacancy can be sent to: recruit@ouce.ox.ac.uk The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon (UK time) on 27 November 2026 . Only applications submitted by this time will be considered. The University of Oxford and The School of Geography and the Environment are Silver Athena SWAN holders. Applications are particularly welcome and encouraged from women, black, and minority ethnic candidates, who are under-represented in posts in The School of Geography and the Environment. The School of Geography and the Environment are committed to equality and values diversity Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189392 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 27-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ouce.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 39424 - 47779"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189347": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral researcher in millimetre transients with The Africa Millimetre Telescope",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Postdoctoral researcher in millimetre transients with The Africa Millimetre Telescope Department of Physics, Denys Wilkinson Building, Oxford The Sub-department of Astrophysics at the University of Oxford invites applications for a postdoctoral research position focused on the design and implementation of a large-scale astrophysical transients programme using the Africa Millimetre Telescope (AMT). The successful candidate will work closely with the Oxford-based and international Blackholistic and AMT teams to develop this pioneering scientific programme. The AMT is a new millimetre-wavelength telescope under construction in Namibia, with first observations anticipated in 2027–2028. It will become an integral component of the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) array, significantly enhancing its uv coverage and improving its snapshot imaging capabilities. Alongside its contribution to the EHT, the AMT will undertake a major dedicated programme of rapid-response observations and high-cadence monitoring of astrophysical transients. The programme will encompass a broad range of transient phenomena, from extragalactic events such as gamma-ray bursts to flaring stars in the local Universe, and will incorporate a rapid-response observing mode. The AMT will be the first millimetre-wavelength facility to undertake a transient science programme of this scale, opening new opportunities for time-domain astrophysics. The postholder will have opportunity to teach. Applicants should hold a PhD, or be close to completing one, in astrophysics or a related discipline. Previous experience in radio or millimetre astronomy and astrophysical transients would be highly desirable. Candidates should also demonstrate excellent communication skills and the ability to work effectively and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary, international research environment. Please direct enquiries about the role to Prof. Rob Fender ( Rob.Fender@physics.ox.ac.uk ). This is a full-time post, fixed term for 2 years The closing date for applications is noon GMT time on Monday, 30 November 2026 You will be asked to upload a CV and a supporting statement. The CV should include a publication list (or ADS/SciX link to one), and should be up to 3 pages long. The supporting statement should be up to 3 pages, may include figures, and should cover (i) your background and experience and how it relates to the post and the listed selection criteria, (ii) your thoughts on a how a millimetre transients programme on the AMT could be best designed and implemented, (iii) what you think the most exciting science from such a programme could be. You should arrange for your 2 referees to send your reference to leanne.odonnell@physics.ox.ac.uk ** before the closing date. ** Contact Person : Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189347 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 30-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@physics.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 7RS: £39,424-£47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189502": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Tensegrity Robot Design and Control",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/12/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Tensegrity Robot Design and Control central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant to join the Protean Robotics Lab, directed by Prof. Robert Baines, at the Department of Engineering Science in central Oxford. The post is funded by the European Research Commission and is fixed term to 31/12/2028, with the possibility to extend. You will be contributing to a project on designing and controlling robots built from tensegrity structures. By exploiting tensegrities’ shape change and nonlinear mechanics, the research aims to unlock locomotion and manipulation skills impossible for conventional robots. You will be involved in co-optimizing tensegrities’ designs and control policies via simulation, as well as building and testing tensegrity hardware. You are expected to engage in supervision of undergraduate and graduate students working on the project. You should possess a PhD/DPhil in Engineering, Robotics, or other related field, together with established knowledge of constructing and programming robots, including motor control and using simulators like IsaacLab or MujoCo. Experience with tensegrity systems is not required but is a plus. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Robert Baines (email: robert.baines@eng.ox.ac.uk) For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/. The Protean Robotics Lab is affiliated with the Oxford Robotics Institute, a group of 10 faculty working at the cutting edge of robotics and AI: https://ori.ox.ac.uk/. Only online applications received before midday on 1 December 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, including a brief statement of research interests (describing how past experience and future plans fit with the advertised position), CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Keywords : Robotics, Tensegrity, Simulation, Mechanisms, Machine Learning Contact Person : Professor Robert Baines Vacancy ID : 189502 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 01-Dec-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : robert.baines@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189419": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Neuromorphic Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/12/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Neuromorphic Engineering Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN), Brain Network Dynamics Unit, Mansfield Road, Oxford, OX1 3TH We are looking for an outstanding researcher for a joint project between the University of Oxford and an industrial partner, Great Sky. The postholder will develop an implementation of a model of information processing in the brain, called predictive coding, in hardware employing superconducting computational circuits. The research will employ mathematical analysis and computer simulations to investigate how the predictive coding model can be adjusted so it can be implemented in the neuromorphic hardware, while preserving the computational advantages of the model. Predictive coding is a brain-inspired model which learns with fewer examples than traditional artificial neural networks, used in artificial intelligence (AI). Therefore, it has a potential to speed up training of AI models. However, simulating this model on current digital computers is slow, as they are not optimized for the computations which predictive coding requires. By contrast, the new hardware developed by Great Sky performs computations in a fundamentally different way, which is compatible with the requirements of the predictive coding model. Implementing predictive coding on this hardware will unlock the advantages of predictive coding, resulting in faster and more energy-efficient training of AI. The postholder will be co-supervised by Rafal Bogacz from University of Oxford and Jeff Shainline from Great Sky. The post holder will work as a part of an interdisciplinary team including computational neuroscientists studying learning in brain networks in Oxford, and will collaborate with engineers developing neuromorphic hardware at Great Sky. The post holder will also be a member of a research group of Professor Rafal Bogacz, and based in the Brain Network Dynamics Unit, which includes an interdisciplinary community of neurobiologists, engineers and computational scientists. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the role, responsibilities, and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. For any queries regarding visa sponsorship, HR and the University's Staff Immigration Team will provide advice and assistance when the chosen candidate for the role has been identified. This post is full time, fixed term for 2 years. Only applications received before midday 12:00 on Monday 14th December 2026 will be considered. Interviews are expected to take place in the new year. Contact Person : NDCN Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189419 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 14-Dec-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189482": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Nurse",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Clinical Research Nurse Oxford Respiratory Trials Unit (ORTU), Churchill Hospital site Join Our Team at Oxford Respiratory Trials Unit Are you a registered nurse looking to contribute to clinical research in the field of respiratory diseases? We have an exciting opportunity for a research nurse to join our existing clinical research delivery team. We value teamwork and are looking for a nurse to contribute to a cooperative and supportive environment. About the Role Our diverse portfolio includes: Observational studies Clinical trials of investigational medicinal products (CTIMPs) Medical device trials Research areas include a range of respiratory conditions including interstitial lung disease, pleural diseases, lung cancer, COPD, chronic cough and respiratory sleep and breathing disorders. As a key member of the team, you will be responsible for the day-to-day management of allocated respiratory trials and main responsibilities will include: Coordinating and providing care for trial participants Collecting and processing biological samples Gathering and managing trial data You will take part in all aspects of respiratory care and trial assessments, including spirometry, phlebotomy, ECGs, observations and history taking. About You You will be responsible for the planning and day-to-day management of assigned respiratory studies. Essential qualities and skills include: NMC Registered Adult Nurse Have excellent record keeping skills and maintain files and notes accurately. Educate patients about medication use and diary completion Collaborate with the multidisciplinary team and trial monitors to ensure recruitment and follow-up according to trial protocols and Good Clinical Practice. Work closely with other research nurses and practitioners in the delivery team to ensure optimal care and support for trial participants in our research portfolio. Participate in team meetings and attend relevant training sessions and investigator meetings, which may require travel. Desirable skills include venepuncture, spirometry and Good Clinical Practice (GCP) training. If you are motivated to improve patient outcomes and contribute to a dynamic and supportive team, we invite you to apply and look forward to hearing from you. About the Nuffield Department of Clinical Medicine The Nuffield Department of Clinical Medicine (NDM) is one of the largest departments of the University of Oxford and is part of the Medical Sciences Division, with responsibility for a significant part of the teaching of clinical students within the Medical School. Benefits of working University employees enjoy 38 days’ paid holiday, generous pension schemes, travel discounts, and a variety of professional development opportunities. Our range of other employee benefits and discounts also includes free entry to the Botanic Gardens and University colleges, and discounts at University museums. See https://hr.admin.ox.ac.uk/staff-benefits Application Process The post is full time, with one year fixed term. Flexible working options, including part-time will be considered. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Only applications received before 12:00 midday on Tuesday 10th November 2026 will be considered. You are welcome to contact team lead Tine Panduro to organise informal visit and learn more about the role. Please e-mail: tine.panduro@ndm.ox.ac.uk. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189482 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : exp.med-hr@ndm.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £36,360 - £42,434 p.a"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189457": {
      "title": "Research Assistant: MARGINS",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Research Assistant: MARGINS Department of Psychiatry, Warneford Hospital, Oxford, OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. We are looking for a Research Assistant to join the MARGINS (Mental health And Restrictions: Generating new INSights) study in the Department of Psychiatry What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The post involves the day-to-day delivery of a longitudinal study with children and young people, including participant recruitment, engagement and follow-up, and therefore requires regular on-site. Some flexibility to work remotely may be possible, subject to the operational requirements of the study and agreement with the line manager. About the Role The post is full-time and funded for two years. It is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. Applicants must be available to start by 1 February 2027 at the latest. You will work primarily on the project's longitudinal study, which will follow young people aged 10–17 across four waves of data collection to understand how their digital experiences, behaviours and mental health change as technology restrictions are introduced. Working closely with the OxWell Project Manager and senior researchers, you will manage your own research and administration activities relating to the study, within guidelines provided by senior colleagues. You will contribute to the planning, set-up and day-to-day delivery of the longitudinal study, including preparation of study materials, coordination of data collection across four waves, and identifying opportunities to improve study procedures and participant retention. You will also select, follow and, where appropriate, adapt established quantitative and/or qualitative research methodologies to gather, analyse and present data, identifying suitable alternative approaches when methodological or practical challenges arise. About You You will hold a first undergraduate degree in psychology, health sciences, social sciences or another relevant discipline, together with relevant research experience. You will possess sufficient specialist knowledge of relevant research methods to contribute to an established research programme and exercise judgement in applying appropriate approaches. Experience of conducting research with adolescents aged approximately 12–18 years, with a focus on mental health and wellbeing is essential. Experience of recruiting and engaging research participants, with an understanding of research ethics, confidentiality and data protection, is also essential. Experience of longitudinal research and/or repeated follow-up of research participants and of mental health research and/or research into young people's digital environments or experiences would be desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity, our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Your covering letter/supporting statement should clearly demonstrate how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria separately. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/jobs/explore-our-careers/applications-process . The closing date for applications is 12:00 midday on 11 November 2026 . Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189457 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Grade 6: £35,681-£39,424 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189397": {
      "title": "Research Officer – Oxford Climate Policy Monitor (Oxford Climate Policy Hub)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Research Officer – Oxford Climate Policy Monitor (Oxford Climate Policy Hub) Blavatnik School of Government, Radcliffe Observatory Quarter, Woodstock Road, Oxford, OX2 6GG About the Blavatnik School of Government Our vision is of a world better led, a world better served and a world better governed. We are a global school committed to improving the quality of government and public policymaking worldwide, through three routes: teaching current and future leaders ; generating research with impact ; and engaging with governments and practitioners . What we offer The School is a collaborative, friendly, and dynamic department based in an award-winning building in Oxford. We host regular events and talks with well-known guest speakers – all of which are open to staff. On the social side, we have a regular events calendar that are designed to appeal to all, including our Christmas and summer parties. As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days’ annual leave Family leave schemes and a comprehensive range of childcare services Cycle loan scheme and discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs Opportunities for hybrid working ( typically 60% of working hours on-site and 40% remotely) More information about working at the School can be found on our jobs page . About the role You will support the delivery and development of the Climate Policy Monitor, taking responsibility for its day-to-day research activities across the data collection and evaluation phases. Working closely with participating law firms, you will coordinate the identification and assessment of relevant climate policy tools, administer and quality-check surveys, and reconcile responses to ensure the accuracy and consistency of research findings. You will contribute to the development and refinement of research methodologies, including survey instruments and coding procedures, and undertake the collection, analysis and presentation of qualitative and quantitative data. You will also maintain the Monitor’s database and research documentation, ensuring that data is managed in accordance with ethical standards and commitments to transparency and open access. Alongside these responsibilities, you will undertake literature reviews, contribute to academic publications and wider research planning, and share findings with colleagues and research partners. You will also represent the research group at external meetings, seminars and conferences. About you You will hold, or be close to completing, a master’s degree or equivalent in a relevant discipline, such as public policy, political science, law, economics or environmental policy. You will have specialist knowledge of climate policy, regulation or governance in at least one sector or jurisdiction, together with the ability to undertake research within an established programme. You will bring strong organisational and data-handling skills, including experience of collecting, structuring and analysing policy, legal or regulatory information, and confidence in managing structured datasets using spreadsheets or databases. You will have a systematic and thorough approach to research, with excellent attention to detail, the ability to work accurately with complex legal or policy material, and the confidence to manage your own work and meet deadlines. Excellent written and oral communication skills are essential, including the ability to produce publication-quality writing, present research findings at conferences and represent the research group at meetings. You will also be able to work collaboratively with colleagues and research partners and communicate professionally with external contributors, including law firms and subject specialists. Application process Please upload a supporting statement that outlines how you meet the selection criteria in your own words, along with your CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The closing date for applications is at 12 noon (UK time) on Monday 16 November 2026 . Interviews are expected to take place during the week commencing 7 December 2026 and will be on-line. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189397 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruit@bsg.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £36,360 - £42,434 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189499": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Bioelectronics and Neuroengineering for Pain",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Bioelectronics and Neuroengineering for Pain Department of Engineering Science, Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Old Road Campus Research Building, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7DQ Are you passionate about pioneering neurotechnology to transform lives affected by chronic pain? Join the University of Oxford’s EPIONE programme, a six-year EPSRC-funded initiative that brings together leading researchers from Oxford, Cambridge, UCL, and Glasgow to develop next-generation brain stimulation and modulation systems. We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Bioelectronics and Neuroengineering to join the team at the Institute of Biomedical Engineering within the Department of Engineering Science at the University of Oxford. The post is full-time and fixed term for up to 4.5 years (the EPSRC grant ends in September 2031). About the role We are seeking a talented and driven Postdoctoral Research Assistant to contribute to the design and implementation of novel engineering technologies for chronic pain. You will work on advanced brain-machine interfaces and non-invasive stimulation systems, integrating real-time behavioural and brain activity monitoring to deliver closed-loop interventions. This role offers the opportunity to contribute to the establishment and optimisation of these experimental systems in compliance with regulatory requirements within the host group, collaborate across disciplines, and contribute to clinical translation efforts. You will work with Professor Tim Denison’s Brain Engineering Lab at Oxford’s Institute of Biomedical Engineering, collaborating with the Oxford Centre for Integrative Neuroimaging, MRC BNDU, and the Oxford Health NIHR BRC. About you You will hold a PhD in biomedical engineering, neuroscience, or a related field, and have experience in the development of technological systems for real-time human data processing in interactive settings. Technical expertise in areas such as electrophysiological recording, VR paradigm design, closed-loop algorithm development, or clinical neuroscience is highly desirable. We are looking for someone with: Experience in real-time human data processing Advanced programming skills (e.g., Python, MATLAB, C) A collaborative mindset and excellent communication skills A commitment to high-quality research and innovation What we offer Your happiness and wellbeing at work matters to us, so we offer a range of family friendly and financial benefits including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans The University also runs an enormous number of social groups and sports clubs for those looking for more than just a great place to work. Application process Informal enquiries may be addressed to Dr Leen Van Broeck (epione@eng.ox.ac.uk). For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ . Only online applications received before midday on Monday, 23 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, (describing how past experience fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. The Department holds an Athena Swan Bronze award, highlighting its commitment to promoting women in Science, Engineering and Technology. Contact Person : Dr Leen Van Broeck Vacancy ID : 189499 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 23-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : epione@eng.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £39,424 - £47,779 per annum, with a discretionary range to £53,483 per annum (Inclusive of the Oxford University weighting)"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189406": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL About the role We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the Jarvis group in the Section of Molecular Plant Biology at the Department of Biology, to work on a new BBSRC project focused on deciphering the structure and mechanism of the chloroplast-associated protein degradation (CHLORAD) machinery. Our research group has a long track-record of studying chloroplast protein import mechanisms. We uncovered how the import machinery is regulated by a proteolytic mechanism called CHLORAD, and how such regulation is vital for diverse aspects of plant growth, including resilience to adverse environments and fruit ripening. However, while we have a good appreciation of the composition and importance of the CHLORAD machinery, our understanding of its structural characteristics and the molecular mechanisms that underlie its operation are rudimentary. This project will directly address these knowledge gaps. About you If appointed, you will independently manage your own research within guidelines provided by the supervisor, and will contribute to wider project planning. You will actively foster the intellectual environment of the research group through interactions with colleagues, providing training, advice or supervision as necessary. You will possess a PhD/DPhil degree in a relevant discipline, and the specialist expertise needed to carry out the project work effectively. You will have considerable research experience in protein biochemistry and molecular biology. How to apply Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Paul Jarvis (paul.jarvis@biology.ox.ac.uk) The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. This post is full time (37.5 hours) and fixed term. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 24 November 2026. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Contact Person : HR Vacancy ID : 189406 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 24-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@biology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £40,178 - £48,700 per annum inclusive of Oxford University Weighting"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189408": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Research Assistant Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3EL About the role We are seeking a Research Assistant to join the Jarvis group at the Department of Biology to work on a new project focused on elucidating the mechanism of assembly of the chloroplast-associated protein degradation (CHLORAD) system. Our research group has a long track-record of studying chloroplast protein homeostasis mechanisms in plants. We discovered the CHLORAD system, and showed how it mediates ubiquitin-dependent regulation of chloroplast proteins. Such regulation is vital for diverse aspects of plant growth, including resilience to adverse environments and fruit ripening. This project aims to elucidate how the CHLORAD machinery is assembled and organized mechanistically so as to deliver efficient extraction and degradation of chloroplast proteins. Greater understanding in these areas may offer applications in the future development of crops with improved performance. About you You will independently manage your own research within guidelines provided by senior colleagues, including accurate record keeping, and will contribute to wider project planning. You will determine the most appropriate methodologies to test hypotheses, and identify suitable alternatives if technical problems arise. You will possess a bachelor’s degree in a relevant discipline and sufficient specialist knowledge in the discipline to carry out the project work effectively. You will also have considerable relevant research experience in molecular biology and/or protein biochemistry. Experience of following and adapting protocols and selecting appropriate experimental methodologies is essential. How to apply The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being a place where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. This post is full time and fixed-term. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 24 November 2026. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online via our e-recruitment system, and you will be required to upload a supporting statement and CV as part of your online application. Contact Person : HR Vacancy ID : 189408 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 24-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 6 Contact Email : recruitment@biology.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £36,360 per annum inclusive of Oxford University weighting."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189418": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Cognitive Neuroscience",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Cognitive Neuroscience Oxford Centre for Integrative Neuroimaging (OxCIN), John Radcliffe Hospital, Headington, Oxford, OX3 9DU. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Cognitive Neuroanatomy Lab, led by Dr Daniel Margulies, to work on the ERC-funded project ‘Cortical gradients of functional integration’ (CORTIGRAD). The project aims to characterise the organisational principles of the cerebral cortex, and this post will play a central role in bringing together findings across species and imaging modalities into overarching theoretical frameworks. Reporting to the Principal Investigator, the post holder will be a member of the Cognitive Neuroanatomy Lab with responsibility for carrying out research for a discrete area of the CORTIGRAD project and will provide guidance to junior members of the research group, including PhD students and research assistants. The post holder will focus on synthesising the scientific literature, high-quality scientific writing, and cross-species comparative analyses of cortical organisation. The work will include reviewing comparative neuroanatomical and neuroimaging literature across primate and other species, leading and contributing to manuscripts, and carrying out targeted analyses of existing datasets using analytical tools developed within the project. The post holder will work closely with the Principal Investigator and other team members to bring the project's key outputs to publication. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the responsibilities and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. For any queries regarding visa sponsorship, HR and the University's Staff Immigration Team will provide advice and assistance when the chosen candidate for the role has been identified. The post is full time for a fixed term until the 31st of May 2028 Only applications received before 12.00 midday on Wednesday 4th of November 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN HR Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189418 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £40,178– £48,700per annum A less experienced candidate may be appointed at Research Grade 6 (£36,360- £42,434 per annum), with a commensurate adjustment in either the essential criteria, responsibilities or duties."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189561": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher – Immunotherapy and Neuropathic Pain",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher – Immunotherapy and Neuropathic Pain Nuffield Department of Clinical Neurosciences (NDCN) - John Radcliffe Hospital, Headington, Oxford, OX3 9DU Anti-GD2 immunotherapy has transformed survival rates in children with high-risk neuroblastoma. However, treatment efficacy is limited by high-grade adverse events, in particular neurotoxicity and pain, for reasons that are not fully understood. This project, funded by Medical Research UK, aims to determine the cellular and molecular immune mechanisms underlying neuropathic pain caused by anti-GD2 antibodies. Your primary role will be to establish a behavioural assay of spontaneous pain in a mouse model of neuroblastoma and use this to interrogate the underlying cellular and molecular immune mechanisms evoked by anti-GD2 antibodies. You will work with collaborators to develop and test new antibody derivatives using in vitro and in vivo assays. You will also be responsible for processing and analysing blood samples collected from patients undergoing anti-GD2 therapy for neuroblastoma (Note, this not a patient facing role). You will join a multi-disciplinary team led by UKRI Future Leaders Fellow Dr Alexander Davies, head of the Peripheral Neuroimmunity and Pain Lab at the University of Oxford supported by experts in neurological biomarker detection (Prof Rinaldi), neuroblastoma treatment (Prof Gray), and antibody engineering (Prof Beers). You will attend termly project meetings with the team to share results and receive regular guidance and support. Training will be provided in immune-phenotyping where required. As a postdoctoral researcher you will be encouraged to mentor less experienced members of the research group, as well as gain experience in supervising MSc and summer project students including via the UNIQ+ programme. The Peripheral Neuroimmunity and Pain Lab is part of the Neural Injury Group at NDCN: a highly collaborative group of clinician and non-clinician researchers with complementary expertise in peripheral neuropathy and neuropathic pain. We value team work and a community approach to working together in a research laboratory. You will take part in regular presentation and journal club meetings as well as attend yearly networking opportunities such as Oxford Neuroscience, Thomas Willis Day and OxPain events. As a group we subscribe to the University of Oxford Equality and Diversity policy, and are committed to providing an environment where everyone feels equal, respected and safe. NDCN has a strong track record of early career researchers transitioning to independence with fair and transparent processes in place to support applications for grants and fellowships as appropriate for each career stage. This project provides the ideal opportunity for a preclinical researcher to gain experience leading a multi-disciplinary project with strong clinical translation. Candidates with a background in either neuroscience or immunology are welcome to apply. Please see the below 'Job Description' for further details on the responsibilities and selection criteria, as well as further information about the university and how to apply. For any queries regarding visa sponsorship, HR and the University's Staff Immigration Team will provide advice and assistance when the chosen candidate for the role has been identified. The post is full time for a fixed term until the 30th of June 2028. Only applications received before 12.00 midday on Wednesday 11th of November 2026 will be considered. Interviews will be held as soon as possible thereafter. Contact Person : NDCN HR Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189561 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 11-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@ndcn.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £40,178 – £48,700 per annum A less experienced candidate may be appointed at Research Grade 6 (£36,360 - £42,434 per annum), with a commensurate adjustment in either the essential criteria, responsibilities or duties."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189560": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Learning Perceptive Contact-Rich LocoManipulation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Assistant in Learning Perceptive Contact-Rich LocoManipulation central Oxford We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Assistant to join the Machine Learning & Articulated Robots Group (ARG) of the Oxford Robotics Institute and the Department of Engineering Science in central Oxford. The post is funded by the EPSRC’s Centre to Centre (C2C) Grant, “Mobile Robotic Inspector: Learning to Explore and Manipulate in the Real World” and is fixed-term for 24 months. The project will develop leaning-based approaches to contact rich mobile manipulation (LocoManipulation) with a focus on legged robots -mainly humanoids- in challenging scenarios. This C2C project is in collaboration with ETH Zurich’s “RobotX - Center for Robotics” and is build around a rich schedule of visits to and from ETH. The goal is to further develop our mobile manipulation capabilities, able of physically demanding and dexterous mobile manipulation tasks in a range of dynamic domains (industrial, service, domestic). This position will focus on using and developing learning-based perceptive approaches. You will develop a control framework integrating multi-modal perception and tactile feedback, for locomotion and manipulation. You will be responsible for the design, development, implementation and evaluation of learning-based loco-manipulation methods suitable for legged systems in physically-realistic simulated environments and on real robots. You should hold or be close to completion of a PhD/DPhil in robotics, computer science, machine learning, informatics, AI, or a closely related field. You will have an excellent academic track record in topics relevant to manipulation and locomotion; path and motion planning, whole-body control, multi-contact manipulation/locomotion, machine learning and optimisation, avatar animation or related areas. You have experience working on real robots and great team working skills. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Prof. Ioannis Havoutis. For more information about working at the Department, see www.eng.ox.ac.uk/about/work-with-us/ Only applications received before noon (12.00pm GMT) on 25 November 2026 can be considered. You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, (describing how past experience fit with the advertised position), CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Keywords: Humanoids, Machine Learning, Manipulation, Locomotion, Reinforcement Learning Contact Person : Ioannis Havoutis Vacancy ID : 189560 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 25-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : ioannis@robots.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £40,178 - £48,700 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189372": {
      "title": "Senior Epidemiologist and Science Team Lead\nUK Biobank Epidemiology Group",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/11/2026 Senior Epidemiologist and Science Team Lead UK Biobank Epidemiology Group Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford), Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford, OX3 7LF University of Oxford Senior Epidemiologist and Science Team Lead UK Biobank Epidemiology Group Grade 8: £49,119 - £58,265 per annum (including Oxford University weighting) Oxford Population Health (the Nuffield Department of Population Health) provides an excellent environment for multi-disciplinary research and teaching and for professional and support staff. We work together to answer some of the most important questions about the causes, prevention and treatment of disease. We are seeking a Senior Epidemiologist to lead the Science Team within the UK Biobank Epidemiology Group. The postholder will lead the team, oversee its review activities and contribute epidemiological expertise to the continued development and effective use of the UK Biobank resource. Alongside these responsibilities, the postholder will have protected time to develop and lead epidemiological research using UK Biobank’s genetic, phenotypic and linked health data. The post is based at the Big Data Institute within the Nuffield Department of Population Health and reports to Associate Professor Ben Lacey, who leads the UK Biobank Epidemiology Group. To be considered you must hold a PhD in epidemiology, statistics or a related subject. You will have extensive experience in the design, analysis and interpretation of large-scale cohort studies and extensive experience in the formal assessment of research applications within established governance and review frameworks. You will have proven ability to gather requirements to streamline/optimise processes, or identify opportunities to optimise services. This is a full time, fixed term post (part time considered) for 2 years. The closing date for applications is 25th November 2026 at 12.00pm You will be required to upload a CV and a cover letter as part of your online application. The cover letter should clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Contact Person : NDPH Recruitment Vacancy ID : 189372 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 25-Nov-2026 09:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruit@ndph.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 50067 - 59396"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189576": {
      "title": "Researcher in Data Science and Informatics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Researcher in Data Science and Informatics Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford), Old Road Campus, Headington, Oxford Oxford Population Health (Nuffield Department of Population Health) is home to world-renowned population health research groups and provides an excellent environment for multi-disciplinary research and teaching. Based at Oxford Population Health, the Demographic Science Unit (DSU) is at the forefront of demographic research that aids society, government and industry. The DSU is home to the Leverhulme Centre for Demographic Science, an interdisciplinary research centre funded by the Leverhulme Trust and directed by Professor Melinda Mills which aims to disrupt and realign how we measure and model populations by infusing new types of data, methods and unconventional approaches to tackle the most challenging demographic problems of our time. We are looking to recruit highly capable Researcher in Data Science and Informatics who will have the responsibility for carrying out research in methodological developments and access facilitation related to large scale population register resource utilisation. Working with Associate Professor Charles Rahal and with the wider team of data scientists in the Demographic Science Unit and the Leverhulme Centre for Demographic Science at the University of Oxford, the post holder will also provide guidance to junior members of the research group including research assistants, PhD students, and/or project volunteers. To be considered for the role you will hold a PhD/DPhil. You will have specialist knowledge in the discipline to develop research projects and methodologies, with a substantial focus on methodological developments for evidence extraction of academic articles at scale. Ability to manage own academic research and excellent communication skills are also essential. The position is part time at 20% FTE and fixed term for 18 months. The closing date for applications is noon on 5 November 2026. You will be required to upload a CV and a cover letter as part of your online application. The cover letter should clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. Contact Person : NDPH Recruitment Team Vacancy ID : 189576 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruit@ndph.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : 40178 - 48700"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189601": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Associate Life and Mind Building, South Parks Road, OX1 3EL Grade 7: £40,178-£48,700 per annum The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Experimental Psychology is based in the new, purpose-built Life and Mind Building, a multidisciplinary research and teaching facility. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the Neuromodulatory Systems and Cognition Lab, led by Nima Khalighinejad, in the Department of Experimental Psychology. The successful candidate will work on a Wellcome Trust-funded project investigating how dopaminergic and serotonergic systems interact in the non-human primate frontal cortex to support learning and decision-making. The successful applicant will design novel behavioural tasks, train and test non-human primates, and conduct MRI experiments and causal manipulations of neuromodulatory systems using blood-brain barrier opening, targeted drug delivery and non-invasive neuromodulation techniques. The postholder will undertake quantitative and statistical analysis of behavioural and neuroimaging data, work closely with multidisciplinary collaborators, and take intellectual leadership of the project, including driving publications. The post is full-time, fixed term for 2 years, and is available from January 2027. When not involved in activities requiring on-site presence, such as data collection or group meetings, remote working may be possible. Applicants should hold a PhD/DPhil, or be close to completion, in neuroscience, psychology, computational neuroscience, biomedical engineering or a closely related quantitative STEM discipline. A strong background in cognitive, behavioural and/or systems neuroscience is essential, together with experience of designing behavioural tasks and analysing behavioural data. Applicants should also have experience of working with non-human primates, including behavioural training, and experience of transcranial ultrasound techniques and MRI data collection and/or analysis, or strong motivation to develop further expertise in neuroimaging methods. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: An excellent contributory pension scheme 38 days annual leave A comprehensive range of childcare services Family leave schemes Cycle loan scheme Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The University of Oxford is committed to equal opportunity, and to being an inclusive institution where everyone belongs and is supported to succeed. We recognise how the diversity of our community enriches our ability to deliver on our academic mission. We welcome applications from individuals from all backgrounds, including those under-represented within higher education. No applicant or members of staff shall be unlawfully discriminated against on the basis of age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage or civil partnership, pregnancy or maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, or sexual orientation. Applications for this vacancy are to be made online. You will be required to upload a supporting statement addressing the selection criteria, your CV, and the contact details of two referees as part of your application. For further details please refer to the “How to apply” section of the job description. The closing date for applications is midday on 6th November 2026 and only applications received by this date will be considered. Contact Person : HR Team Vacancy ID : 189601 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 06-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : hr@psy.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £40,178-£48,700 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189458": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Researcher: MARGINS",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Researcher: MARGINS Department of Psychiatry, Warneford Hospital, Oxford OX3 7JX The University of Oxford is a stimulating work environment, which enjoys an international reputation as a world-class centre of excellence. Our research plays a key role in tackling many global challenges, from reducing our carbon emissions to developing vaccines during a pandemic. The Department of Psychiatry is based on the Warneford Hospital site in Oxford – a friendly, welcoming place of work with an international reputation for excellence. The Department has a substantial research programme, with major funding from Medical Research Council (MRC), Wellcome Trust and National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) and provides highly rated medical training in psychiatry. The Head of Department is Professor Belinda Lennox. We are looking for a Senior Postdoctoral Researcher to join MARGINS (Mental health And Restrictions: Generating new INSights) in the Department of Psychiatry. What We Offer As an employer, we genuinely care about our employees’ wellbeing and this is reflected in the range of benefits that we offer including: • An excellent contributory pension scheme • 38 days annual leave • A comprehensive range of childcare services • Family leave schemes • Cycle loan scheme • Discounted bus travel and Season Ticket travel loans • Membership to a variety of social and sports clubs The postholder will normally be expected to work on site for a minimum of three days per week, reflecting the importance of close collaboration with the wider MARGINS research team. The remaining working time may be undertaken flexibly/hybrid, subject to agreement with the line manager and the operational requirements of the project. About the Role The post is full-time and funded for two years. It is based in the Department of Psychiatry at the Warneford Hospital. Applicants must be available to start by 1 February 2027 at the latest. MARGINS is part of a research programme investigating the impact of proposed policy changes to online technology on children and young people. This comprises three complementary research streams and you will work across all three, providing senior quantitative and methodological leadership across the programme. Working closely with Professor Mina Fazel, Dr Holly Bear, Dr Emma Soneson, the project's statistical lead and international collaborators, you will lead the development of research questions and analytical approaches within the agreed aims of MARGINS, generating original ideas and identifying opportunities for further analyses. In addition, you will lead and develop complex quantitative analyses, using large-scale cross-sectional, longitudinal and international datasets and contribute to the interpretation and integration of findings and to high-quality research publications, reports and presentations. About You You will hold a relevant PhD/DPhil in a health, behavioural or social science discipline (for example, psychology, epidemiology, public health or mental health research), with post-qualification research experience. Experience of conducting research with adolescents aged approximately 12–18 years, with a focus on mental health and wellbeing and the ability is essential as is the ability to independently plan and manage a research project, including a research budget and to raise research funds through making grant applications. Experience of, or demonstrable interest in, research relating to young people's digital environments and experiences, and/or population health would be desirable. Diversity Committed to equality and valuing diversity, our active Psychiatry People and Culture teams and initiatives including our values and behaviours framework , work to make the Department of Psychiatry as supportive, welcoming and inclusive as possible. Application Process You will be required to upload a covering letter/supporting statement, CV and the details of two referees as part of your online application. Your covering letter/supporting statement should clearly demonstrate how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria separately. Please see the University pages on the application process at https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/jobs/explore-our-careers/applications-process . The closing date for applications is 12:00 midday on 16 November 2026 . Contact Person : HR Officer Vacancy ID : 189458 Contact Phone : Closing Date & Time : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : vacancies@psych.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : £50,067–£59,396 per annum"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189594": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/11/2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist Nuffield Department of Women’s and Reproductive Health, level 3, Women’s Centre, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford. The Nuffield Department of Women’s and Reproductive Health is looking for a Postdoctoral Research Scientist to join the Oxford Endometriosis CaRe Centre (EndoCaRe) and contribute to an exciting research programme investigating new therapeutic targets for endometriosis. Working within a multidisciplinary team and in collaboration with our industrial partner, Hirundo Biosciences, you will help translate human genetic discoveries into potential new treatments for endometriosis. You will be responsible for developing and optimising a macrophage–endometriosis co-culture system using patient-derived organoids and primary immune cells to investigate the cellular interactions involved in inflammation and disease progression. You will undertake laboratory experiments, including cell culture, flow cytometry and other single-cell techniques, analyse and interpret research data, and contribute to scientific publications and presentations. You will also work closely with colleagues and collaborators, contribute to research proposals and provide guidance to less experienced members of the research group. You will hold a PhD/DPhil, or be close to completion, in immunology or a related biomedical discipline, with specialist knowledge and practical experience of macrophage biology and in vitro experimental techniques. You will have experience of managing your own research activities and contributing to peer-reviewed publications, together with excellent communication and organisational skills. You will be able to work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team, communicate research findings effectively and contribute to the supervision of graduate students. Experience of epithelial tissue culture, human co-culture models, advanced cellular analysis techniques or bioinformatics would be advantageous. Applications for flexible working arrangements are welcomed and will be considered in line with business needs. Please note that occasional out-of-hours working may be required to accommodate the timing of patient tissue collection. You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your Supporting Statement should clearly demonstrate, using specific examples, how your skills and experience meet the selection criteria for the post. The post is offered on a full-time basis and is fixed term for three years. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Dr Jennifer Southcombe at jen.southcombe@wrh.ox.ac.uk. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 26th November 2026. Interviews are expected to take place on 17th December 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Manager Vacancy ID : 189594 Contact Phone : 01865221002 Closing Date & Time : 26-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 7 Contact Email : recruitment@wrh.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 7: £39,424 - £53,483"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uoxrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=189595": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/11/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist Nuffield Department of Women’s and Reproductive Health, level 3, Women’s Centre, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford. The Nuffield Department of Women’s and Reproductive Health is looking for an experienced and highly motivated Senior Postdoctoral Research Scientist to join the internationally recognised Oxford Endometriosis CaRe Centre (EndoCaRe). You will contribute to an exciting translational research programme, working in collaboration with our industrial partner, Hirundo Biosciences, to translate human genetic discoveries into potential new treatments for endometriosis. You will lead research activities to validate novel therapeutic targets using patient-derived endometriosis tissues, including gene and protein expression analysis, primary cell culture and genome editing techniques. You will develop and optimise experimental protocols, investigate disease-relevant cellular processes and work closely with clinicians, geneticists and computational scientists. You will also contribute to scientific publications, research funding applications and presentations, manage research activities and budgets, and provide supervision and guidance to other members of the research team. You will hold a PhD/DPhil in cell biology, molecular biology, genetics, reproductive biology or a related discipline, together with postdoctoral research experience in genome editing methodologies. You will have a strong publication record, including first-author papers in peer-reviewed journals, and experience of gene and protein expression analysis in primary tissues, tissue culture and in vitro functional assays. You will demonstrate the ability to independently plan and manage research projects and budgets, supervise graduate research students and work effectively within a multidisciplinary team. Experience of bioinformatic analysis of genomic or transcriptomic data is also essential. Previous experience of working with primary human tissues, organoids, genetic manipulation of primary cells or single-cell and spatial sequencing data would be advantageous. Applications for flexible working arrangements are welcomed and will be considered in line with business needs. Please note that occasional out-of-hours working may be required to accommodate the timing of patient tissue collection. You will be required to upload a CV and Supporting Statement as part of your online application. Your Supporting Statement should clearly demonstrate, using specific examples, how your skills and experience meet the selection criteria for the post. The post is offered on a full-time basis and is fixed term for three years. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Dr Jennifer Southcombe at jen.southcombe@wrh.ox.ac.uk. The closing date for applications is 12.00 noon on 26th November 2026. Interviews are expected to take place on 16th December 2026. Contact Person : Recruitment Manager Vacancy ID : 189595 Contact Phone : 01865221002 Closing Date & Time : 26-Nov-2026 12:00 Pay Scale : RESEARCH GRADE 8 Contact Email : recruitment@wrh.ox.ac.uk Salary (£) : Research Grade 8: £49,119 - £65,336"
    }
  },
